window.trendingPapers = {
    "today": [{"paper": {"id": "2610.35201", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7ae", "name": "Hongze Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7af", "name": "Miao Pan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b0", "name": "Lizhou Liang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b1", "name": "Qinfeng Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b2", "name": "Xinyin Ma", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b3", "name": "Yongliang Shen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b4", "name": "Weishi Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b5", "name": "Daniel Dahlmeier", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b6", "name": "Xuhong Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b7", "name": "Wenqi Zhang", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d/-HwSwdNgn5m4Bx9b0Riez.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "DTCA: Denoising Token Credit Assignment for Reinforcement Learning in Diffusion Language Models", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/18348cb2ef91757f3450c18debd11599.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Wenqi Zhang", "user": "zwq2018", "type": "user", "name": "zwq2018"}, "summary": "Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate text through iterative denoising, enabling bidirectional context modeling and parallel token refinement. Recent studies have applied reinforcement learning (RL) to DLMs and improved their reasoning performance. However, the iterative denoising process makes credit assignment more challenging, as final outcome rewards provide limited information about how different tokens contribute to the final output. In this paper, we propose Denoising Token Credit Assignment (DTCA), a framework for token-level credit assignment in reinforcement learning for diffusion language models. DTCA estimates token contributions by measuring the improvement in prediction confidence of remaining masked positions after token commitment and groups these signals according to part-of-speech (POS) categories. These contribution weights are used to select informative denoising timesteps and assign token-level learning signals during RL training. DTCA further calibrates these learning signals according to token-level entropy changes induced by policy updates. Experiments on LLaDA-8B-Instruct show that DTCA consistently improves mathematical reasoning performance across different generation budgets, with gains of up to 9.0 points on MATH500 and 5.3 points on GSM8K over the base model.", "upvotes": 254, "discussionId": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b8", "projectPage": "https://zju-omniai.github.io/DTCA/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/ZJU-OmniAI/DTCA", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "DTCA improves reinforcement learning for diffusion language models by assigning token-level credit via confidence improvements and entropy calibration, boosting mathematical reasoning.", "ai_keywords": ["diffusion language models", "reinforcement learning", "token-level credit assignment", "denoising token credit assignment", "part-of-speech categories", "denoising timesteps", "token-level entropy"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 3, "organization": {"_id": "696461ab2d94e9a07cdb8efd", "name": "OmniAI-ZJU", "fullname": "ZJU-OmniAI", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65f2595830354d0ee043b25a/eEeRdHlGyJ148JQ6BAJ4O.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u6269\u6563\u8bed\u8a00\u6a21\u578b\uff08DLMs\uff09\u901a\u8fc7\u8fed\u4ee3\u53bb\u566a\u751f\u6210\u6587\u672c\uff0c\u652f\u6301\u53cc\u5411\u4e0a\u4e0b\u6587\u5efa\u6a21\u548c\u5e76\u884c\u4ee4\u724c\u4f18\u5316\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6700\u8fd1\u7684\u7814\u7a76\u5c06\u5f3a\u5316\u5b66\u4e60\uff08RL\uff09\u5e94\u7528\u4e8eDLMs\uff0c\u63d0\u5347\u4e86\u63a8\u7406\u6027\u80fd\uff0c\u4f46\u53bb\u566a\u8fc7\u7a0b\u4f7f\u5f97\u4fe1\u8d37\u5206\u914d\u53d8\u5f97\u66f4\u52a0\u590d\u6742\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u672c\u6587\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86\u4e00\u79cd\u65b0\u7684\u6846\u67b6\u2014\u2014\u53bb\u566a\u4ee4\u724c\u4fe1\u8d37\u5206\u914d\uff08DTCA\uff09\uff0c\u7528\u4e8e\u5728\u5f3a\u5316\u5b66\u4e60\u4e2d\u5b9e\u73b0\u4ee4\u724c\u7ea7\u7684\u4fe1\u8d37\u5206\u914d\u3002</li>\n    <li>DTCA\u901a\u8fc7\u6d4b\u91cf\u4ee4\u724c\u627f\u8bfa\u540e\u5269\u4f59\u63a9\u7801\u4f4d\u7f6e\u7684\u9884\u6d4b\u7f6e\u4fe1\u5ea6\u6539\u5584\u6765\u4f30\u7b97\u4ee4\u724c\u8d21\u732e\uff0c\u5e76\u6309\u8bcd\u6027\u7c7b\u522b\u8fdb\u884c\u4fe1\u53f7\u5206\u7ec4\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u8868\u660e\uff0cDTCA\u5728\u4e0d\u540c\u751f\u6210\u9884\u7b97\u4e0b\u63d0\u5347\u4e86\u6570\u5b66\u63a8\u7406\u6027\u80fd\uff0cMATH500\u4e0a\u63d0\u5347\u4e86\u6700\u591a9.0\u5206\uff0cGSM8K\u4e0a\u63d0\u5347\u4e865.3\u5206\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate text by gradually removing noise, allowing for better understanding of context.</li>\n    <li>Reinforcement learning (RL) has been applied to DLMs to enhance their reasoning abilities.</li>\n    <li>However, it's hard to determine how much each word contributes to the final text because of the way DLMs work.</li>\n    <li>The paper introduces a new method called Denoising Token Credit Assignment (DTCA) to better assign credit to individual words in RL training.</li>\n    <li>Tests show that DTCA significantly improves mathematical reasoning in models, with notable performance increases in specific tasks.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "DTCA: Denoising Token Credit Assignment for Reinforcement Learning in Diffusion Language Models", "summary": "Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate text through iterative denoising, enabling bidirectional context modeling and parallel token refinement. Recent studies have applied reinforcement learning (RL) to DLMs and improved their reasoning performance. However, the iterative denoising process makes credit assignment more challenging, as final outcome rewards provide limited information about how different tokens contribute to the final output. In this paper, we propose Denoising Token Credit Assignment (DTCA), a framework for token-level credit assignment in reinforcement learning for diffusion language models. DTCA estimates token contributions by measuring the improvement in prediction confidence of remaining masked positions after token commitment and groups these signals according to part-of-speech (POS) categories. These contribution weights are used to select informative denoising timesteps and assign token-level learning signals during RL training. DTCA further calibrates these learning signals according to token-level entropy changes induced by policy updates. Experiments on LLaDA-8B-Instruct show that DTCA consistently improves mathematical reasoning performance across different generation budgets, with gains of up to 9.0 points on MATH500 and 5.3 points on GSM8K over the base model.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d/-HwSwdNgn5m4Bx9b0Riez.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.35201.png", "numComments": 3, "submittedBy": {"_id": "6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/18348cb2ef91757f3450c18debd11599.svg", "fullname": "Wenqi Zhang", "name": "zwq2018", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 16, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "696461ab2d94e9a07cdb8efd", "name": "OmniAI-ZJU", "fullname": "ZJU-OmniAI", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65f2595830354d0ee043b25a/eEeRdHlGyJ148JQ6BAJ4O.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.34283", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713b", "name": "Yihao Hu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713c", "name": "Yitong Lin", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713d", "name": "Pan Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713e", "name": "Zhihao Wen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713f", "name": "Hang Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907140", "name": "Yiwen Zhao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907141", "name": "Deyuan Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907142", "name": "Yutong Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907143", "name": "Mingda Wan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907144", "name": "Yuchen Mao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907145", "name": "Tao Lin", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "ChronoReplay: One Answer Interface Is Not Enough for Streaming Spatial Intelligence", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "67e40cea6d0207b1fa43c826", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/_OzAJCjFC49dEkHZm4AQb.png", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Yihao Hu", "user": "HU22333", "type": "user", "name": "HU22333"}, "summary": "Training a spatial VLM to answer which side an object lies on does not require it to preserve the metric displacement that determines that answer, while supervision for a distance estimate may leave categorical relations largely unconstrained. When post-training emphasizes one readout, improvements can therefore concentrate on that interface while transfer to others remains weak or even degrades; we call this observed specialization interface lock-in. ChronoReplay addresses it by replaying heterogeneous spatial supervision as chronologically ordered episodes, retaining source-native targets, and balancing supervision across families. Under the same observation access, a single ChronoReplay checkpoint improves OVO-S-Bench, OST-Bench, and UCS-Bench together, including a 13.41-point gain on OST-Bench, and achieves the highest three-benchmark mean among the supervision settings compared here. Same-data controls further separate the ingredients of this transfer: answer serialization redistributes gains across OST and UCS, family-balanced learning improves over ordinary joint SFT and natural turn weighting, and chronological replay exceeds a shifted-order control by about 2.41 OST points across two paired seeds. Adaptive frame selection then changes evidence access while keeping the trained model fixed, providing an additional inference-time gain at lower visual cost. Streaming spatial performance depends on what distinctions supervision preserves, how heterogeneous histories are organized during learning, and what causal evidence inference makes available at test time.", "upvotes": 77, "discussionId": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907146", "projectPage": "https://yihaohu0118.github.io/ChronoReplay/", "ai_summary": "ChronoReplay mitigates interface lock-in in spatial vision-language models by replaying heterogeneous spatial supervision chronologically, improving cross-task transfer and enabling efficient inference-time evidence selection.", "ai_keywords": ["spatial VLM", "interface lock-in", "ChronoReplay", "joint SFT", "adaptive frame selection", "OVO-S-Bench", "OST-Bench", "UCS-Bench"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "6650967d53061f0d1b5a7962", "name": "LINs-lab", "fullname": "LINs lab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65296b8a6cdea40585bd81e2/M80w6f0H8dfx3Sa1nVmfq.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u7a7a\u95f4\u89c6\u89c9\u8bed\u8a00\u6a21\u578b\uff08VLM\uff09\u7684\u8bad\u7ec3\u4e0d\u9700\u8981\u4fdd\u7559\u51b3\u5b9a\u7269\u4f53\u4f4d\u7f6e\u7684\u5ea6\u91cf\u4f4d\u79fb\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5f53\u8bad\u7ec3\u5f3a\u8c03\u5355\u4e00\u8f93\u51fa\u65f6\uff0c\u53ef\u80fd\u5bfc\u81f4\u5176\u4ed6\u63a5\u53e3\u7684\u8f6c\u79fb\u6548\u679c\u8f83\u5f31\uff0c\u79f0\u4e3a\u201c\u4e13\u4e1a\u5316\u63a5\u53e3\u9501\u5b9a\u201d\u3002</li>\n    <li>ChronoReplay\u901a\u8fc7\u987a\u5e8f\u91cd\u64ad\u4e0d\u540c\u7c7b\u578b\u7684\u7a7a\u95f4\u76d1\u7763\u6765\u89e3\u51b3\u8fd9\u4e00\u95ee\u9898\uff0c\u4fdd\u6301\u6e90\u76ee\u6807\u7684\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u4e0d\u540c\u7c7b\u522b\u4e4b\u95f4\u5e73\u8861\u76d1\u7763\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4f7f\u7528ChronoReplay\u7684\u6a21\u578b\u5728\u591a\u4e2a\u57fa\u51c6\u6d4b\u8bd5\u4e2d\u8868\u73b0\u4f18\u5f02\uff0c\u5305\u62ecOST-Bench\u63d0\u9ad8\u4e8613.41\u5206\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u6240\u6709\u76d1\u7763\u8bbe\u7f6e\u4e2d\u83b7\u5f97\u6700\u9ad8\u7684\u5e73\u5747\u5206\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u9002\u5e94\u6027\u5e27\u9009\u62e9\u5728\u4fdd\u6301\u6a21\u578b\u4e0d\u53d8\u7684\u60c5\u51b5\u4e0b\u6539\u53d8\u8bc1\u636e\u83b7\u53d6\uff0c\u8fdb\u4e00\u6b65\u63d0\u5347\u63a8\u7406\u65f6\u7684\u6027\u80fd\u3002 </li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Training a spatial Visual Language Model (VLM) to determine object positions doesn't always keep accurate distance measurements, leading to a focus on specific tasks instead of a balanced approach.</li>\n    <li>This issue, called \"specialization interface lock-in,\" can limit performance in other areas when training emphasizes one aspect too much.</li>\n    <li>ChronoReplay addresses this by using diverse spatial supervision in a chronological order, which helps improve overall performance across different benchmarks.</li>\n    <li>With ChronoReplay, there were significant gains in testing benchmarks, including a notable improvement of 13.41 points on OST-Bench, and it achieved the best average scores among the compared methods.</li>\n    <li>Adaptive frame selection allows for better use of evidence during testing, enhancing performance while minimizing visual processing demands.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-24T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "ChronoReplay: One Answer Interface Is Not Enough for Streaming Spatial Intelligence", "summary": "Training a spatial VLM to answer which side an object lies on does not require it to preserve the metric displacement that determines that answer, while supervision for a distance estimate may leave categorical relations largely unconstrained. When post-training emphasizes one readout, improvements can therefore concentrate on that interface while transfer to others remains weak or even degrades; we call this observed specialization interface lock-in. ChronoReplay addresses it by replaying heterogeneous spatial supervision as chronologically ordered episodes, retaining source-native targets, and balancing supervision across families. Under the same observation access, a single ChronoReplay checkpoint improves OVO-S-Bench, OST-Bench, and UCS-Bench together, including a 13.41-point gain on OST-Bench, and achieves the highest three-benchmark mean among the supervision settings compared here. Same-data controls further separate the ingredients of this transfer: answer serialization redistributes gains across OST and UCS, family-balanced learning improves over ordinary joint SFT and natural turn weighting, and chronological replay exceeds a shifted-order control by about 2.41 OST points across two paired seeds. Adaptive frame selection then changes evidence access while keeping the trained model fixed, providing an additional inference-time gain at lower visual cost. Streaming spatial performance depends on what distinctions supervision preserves, how heterogeneous histories are organized during learning, and what causal evidence inference makes available at test time.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.34283.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "67e40cea6d0207b1fa43c826", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/_OzAJCjFC49dEkHZm4AQb.png", "fullname": "Yihao Hu", "name": "HU22333", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 1, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6650967d53061f0d1b5a7962", "name": "LINs-lab", "fullname": "LINs lab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65296b8a6cdea40585bd81e2/M80w6f0H8dfx3Sa1nVmfq.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.36729", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907149", "name": "Qiaoling Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714a", "name": "Jiaxiang Cheng", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714b", "name": "Xueqing Deng", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714c", "name": "Tiankai Hang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714d", "name": "Ji Hou", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714e", "name": "Jiaqi Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714f", "name": "Yifan Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907150", "name": "Tao Lu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907151", "name": "Junshu Tang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907152", "name": "Yu Tang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907153", "name": "Yao Teng", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907154", "name": "Yu Tian", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907155", "name": "Heng Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907156", "name": "Weizhi Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907157", "name": "Xiyao Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907158", "name": "Xilun Wu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907159", "name": "Mingyu Yang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715a", "name": "Yangbin Yu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715b", "name": "Rui Zhao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715c", "name": "Longjun Cao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715d", "name": "Linghao Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715e", "name": "Haoyi Duan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715f", "name": "Jiaming Han", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907160", "name": "Mingji Han", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907161", "name": "Enhua Jiang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907162", "name": "Shunkai Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907163", "name": "Yanzhe Liang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907164", "name": "Qinwei Ma", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907165", "name": "Cong Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907166", "name": "Lei Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907167", "name": "Bing Wu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907168", "name": "Ke Xing", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907169", "name": "Ray Ying", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716a", "name": "Zhentao Yu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716b", "name": "Key Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716c", "name": "Yuan Zhou", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716d", "name": "Zhengguang Zhou", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716e", "name": "Kai Zou", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/63d34004b734eaa4d4faeccf/lOBvI5ytl4xxTPiMTwOCy.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Hy-Nomas: Towards Free-form Interactive World Generation", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "63d34004b734eaa4d4faeccf", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/7431a0177ac618bfcdffbf436e689acc.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Weizhi Wang", "user": "weizhiwang", "type": "user", "name": "weizhiwang"}, "summary": "Interactive world generation requires models to follow successive commands while preserving relevant history and action consequences. We present Hy-Nomas, a video world model with an 8B diffusion backbone that supports free-form text instructions and continuous camera control across multiple interaction turns. It generates variable-length segments conditioned on current inputs and cached history. Our central design aligns structured video captions with action-aware causal attention: descriptive context remains accessible across segments, while action instructions are restricted to their intended temporal windows. A vision-language Prompt Enhancer (PE) translates user requests and multimodal interaction histories into structured conditions for the next segment. A multi-stage curriculum progressively introduces causal generation, text interaction, camera control, and training on model-generated histories, followed by diffusion distillation. In human evaluation, Hy-Nomas shows superior performance over existing world models. We release model weights, code and benchmarks, together with a lightweight adaptation workflow, to support reproducible research on interactive world generation.", "upvotes": 49, "discussionId": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716f", "projectPage": "https://hy-nomas.hy.tencent.com/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/Tencent-Hunyuan/Hy-Nomas", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "Hy-Nomas is a video world model that uses causal attention and structured captions to follow multi-turn text and camera instructions while preserving interaction history.", "ai_keywords": ["video world model", "diffusion backbone", "action-aware causal attention", "vision-language Prompt Enhancer", "multi-stage curriculum", "diffusion distillation", "interactive world generation"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 274, "organization": {"_id": "6645f953c39288df638dbdd5", "name": "Tencent-Hunyuan", "fullname": "Tencent Hunyuan", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/62d22496c58f969c152bcefd/woKSjt2wXvBNKussyYPsa.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>Hy-Nomas \u662f\u4e00\u79cd\u89c6\u9891\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\uff0c\u80fd\u591f\u5904\u7406\u8fde\u7eed\u7684\u6307\u4ee4\u548c\u76f8\u673a\u63a7\u5236\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8be5\u6a21\u578b\u57fa\u4e8e 8B \u6269\u6563\u9aa8\u5e72\u7f51\u7edc\uff0c\u751f\u6210\u4e0e\u5f53\u524d\u8f93\u5165\u548c\u5386\u53f2\u8bb0\u5f55\u76f8\u5173\u7684\u53ef\u53d8\u957f\u5ea6\u6bb5\u843d\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8bbe\u8ba1\u4e2d\u7ed3\u5408\u4e86\u7ed3\u6784\u5316\u89c6\u9891\u5b57\u5e55\u548c\u5177\u5907\u52a8\u4f5c\u611f\u77e5\u7684\u56e0\u679c\u6ce8\u610f\u529b\uff0c\u786e\u4fdd\u63cf\u8ff0\u6027\u5185\u5bb9\u5728\u5404\u6bb5\u843d\u95f4\u53ef\u8bbf\u95ee\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5f15\u5165\u4e86\u89c6\u89c9-\u8bed\u8a00\u63d0\u793a\u589e\u5f3a\u5668\uff0c\u5c06\u7528\u6237\u8bf7\u6c42\u8f6c\u5316\u4e3a\u4e0b\u4e00\u4e2a\u6bb5\u843d\u7684\u7ed3\u6784\u5316\u6761\u4ef6\u3002</li>\n    <li>Hy-Nomas \u5728\u4eba\u7c7b\u8bc4\u4f30\u4e2d\u8868\u73b0\u4f18\u4e8e\u73b0\u6709\u7684\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\uff0c\u5e76\u53d1\u5e03\u4e86\u6a21\u578b\u6743\u91cd\u548c\u4ee3\u7801\u4ee5\u652f\u6301\u53ef\u91cd\u590d\u7684\u7814\u7a76\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Hy-Nomas is a new model for creating interactive video worlds that can follow commands and remember past actions.</li>\n    <li>It uses an advanced 8 billion parameter diffusion model that allows for free-form text instructions and camera control.</li>\n    <li>The model generates video segments based on current inputs and past interactions, ensuring relevant information is kept accessible.</li>\n    <li>A special tool called Prompt Enhancer converts user requests into structured instructions for generating the next video segment.</li>\n    <li>Hy-Nomas outperforms other existing models in human evaluations and offers tools and resources for further research in this area.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Hy-Nomas: Towards Free-form Interactive World Generation", "summary": "Interactive world generation requires models to follow successive commands while preserving relevant history and action consequences. We present Hy-Nomas, a video world model with an 8B diffusion backbone that supports free-form text instructions and continuous camera control across multiple interaction turns. It generates variable-length segments conditioned on current inputs and cached history. Our central design aligns structured video captions with action-aware causal attention: descriptive context remains accessible across segments, while action instructions are restricted to their intended temporal windows. A vision-language Prompt Enhancer (PE) translates user requests and multimodal interaction histories into structured conditions for the next segment. A multi-stage curriculum progressively introduces causal generation, text interaction, camera control, and training on model-generated histories, followed by diffusion distillation. In human evaluation, Hy-Nomas shows superior performance over existing world models. We release model weights, code and benchmarks, together with a lightweight adaptation workflow, to support reproducible research on interactive world generation.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/63d34004b734eaa4d4faeccf/lOBvI5ytl4xxTPiMTwOCy.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.36729.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "63d34004b734eaa4d4faeccf", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/7431a0177ac618bfcdffbf436e689acc.svg", "fullname": "Weizhi Wang", "name": "weizhiwang", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 23, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6645f953c39288df638dbdd5", "name": "Tencent-Hunyuan", "fullname": "Tencent Hunyuan", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/62d22496c58f969c152bcefd/woKSjt2wXvBNKussyYPsa.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.36068", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070f8", "name": "Jiayi Luo", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070f9", "name": "Yang Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070fa", "name": "Liliang Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070fb", "name": "Nan Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070fc", "name": "Chen Gao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070fd", "name": "Boxiang Qiu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070fe", "name": "Zhuoxuan Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070ff", "name": "Baorui Peng", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907100", "name": "Chuankang Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907101", "name": "Yuxiang Yan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907102", "name": "Si Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907103", "name": "Qingyun Sun", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907104", "name": "Shuicheng Yan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907105", "name": "Maoqing Yao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907106", "name": "Jianxin Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907107", "name": "Guanghui Ren", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Rollback: Experience Backtracking in World Models for Policy Improvement", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "66c4816e96583c59b09fec30", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/66c4816e96583c59b09fec30/RLurCsmcgOfyuWVkpsv1L.jpeg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Ryan Chen", "user": "ryancll118", "type": "user", "name": "ryancll118"}, "summary": "Action-conditioned world models offer a scalable alternative to costly real-world interaction for improving robot policies. However, existing world-model-based methods largely treat world models as forward-only surrogates of the physical environment, leaving untapped a capability no physical environment affords: rewinding to past decision points to explore counterfactual futures. We introduce Rollback, a policy improvement paradigm that turns world models from passive simulators into counterfactual engines: it rolls back to critical decision points, branches into alternative actions, and contrasts their outcomes to attribute success to individual decisions. This concentrates imagination where it matters most and distills counterfactual outcomes into fine-grained, action-level supervision without a separately learned value model. We build a scalable training system that unifies distributed world-model search with asynchronous real-robot execution, forming a self-reinforcing cycle: real-world experience continually sharpens the world model, and a sharper world model in turn drives further policy improvement. We term the world-model side of this cycle test-time physics grounding, a capability that hand-built physics simulators lack: while the world model serves as the training environment, real transitions from the policy it trains correct its dynamics through a lightweight LoRA pipeline. Experiments on three challenging real-world tasks show that Rollback improves success rates over the strongest baseline by an absolute 10.67%, 11.11%, and 11.85% on block extraction, ball tossing, and tabletop curling, respectively, while achieving more stable improvement throughout training. Although initialized from teleoperated demonstrations far below human level, Rollback surpasses the average success rate of human players on both ball tossing and tabletop curling within 8 hours of training.", "upvotes": 41, "discussionId": "6ae1517ea5eb0647c2907108", "projectPage": "https://rollback-wm.github.io/", "ai_summary": "Rollback improves robot policies by using world models to rewind to past decisions, explore counterfactual actions, and generate fine-grained supervision, while continuously refining dynamics via real-world feedback.", "ai_keywords": ["action-conditioned world models", "counterfactual futures", "Rollback", "world-model search", "test-time physics grounding", "LoRA", "distributed search", "asynchronous execution"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "676fc7c31c48eff17fac3135", "name": "agibot-world", "fullname": "AgiBot World", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64e57309b78bc92221ce3b70/ewI1QvFVMDgSsShQeSvlX.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u884c\u52a8\u6761\u4ef6\u7684\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\u4e3a\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u653f\u7b56\u7684\u6539\u8fdb\u63d0\u4f9b\u4e86\u4e00\u79cd\u53ef\u6269\u5c55\u7684\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u51cf\u5c11\u4e86\u4e0e\u771f\u5b9e\u73af\u5883\u4e92\u52a8\u7684\u6210\u672c\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u73b0\u6709\u7684\u65b9\u6cd5\u4e3b\u8981\u5c06\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\u89c6\u4e3a\u7269\u7406\u73af\u5883\u7684\u524d\u5411\u6a21\u62df\u5668\uff0c\u672a\u80fd\u5229\u7528\u201c\u56de\u6eda\u201d\u5230\u8fc7\u53bb\u51b3\u7b56\u70b9\u7684\u80fd\u529b\u3002</li>\n    <li>Rollback\u662f\u4e00\u79cd\u65b0\u578b\u7684\u653f\u7b56\u6539\u8fdb\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u53ef\u4ee5\u5728\u5173\u952e\u51b3\u7b56\u70b9\u56de\u6eda\uff0c\u63a2\u7d22\u4e0d\u540c\u7684\u884c\u52a8\u53ca\u5176\u7ed3\u679c\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u901a\u8fc7\u5c06\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\u4e0e\u771f\u5b9e\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u6267\u884c\u76f8\u7ed3\u5408\uff0c\u5f62\u6210\u4e00\u4e2a\u81ea\u6211\u5f3a\u5316\u7684\u8bad\u7ec3\u5faa\u73af\uff0c\u6301\u7eed\u4f18\u5316\u6a21\u578b\u548c\u653f\u7b56\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u7ed3\u679c\u8868\u660e\uff0cRollback\u5728\u591a\u9879\u771f\u5b9e\u4e16\u754c\u4efb\u52a1\u4e2d\u663e\u8457\u63d0\u9ad8\u4e86\u6210\u529f\u7387\uff0c\u4e14\u8bad\u7ec3\u8fc7\u7a0b\u66f4\u7a33\u5b9a\uff0c\u751a\u81f3\u8d85\u8d8a\u4e86\u4eba\u7c7b\u73a9\u5bb6\u7684\u5e73\u5747\u6210\u529f\u7387\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Action-conditioned world models help improve robot policies without needing expensive real-world interactions.</li>\n    <li>The new approach, called Rollback, allows robots to rewind to past decisions and explore different actions to see their effects.</li>\n    <li>Rollback focuses on important decisions to improve learning without needing a separate value model.</li>\n    <li>This method combines virtual world training with real-world robot actions, creating a cycle that enhances both the model and the robot's performance.</li>\n    <li>In tests, Rollback showed significant improvements in success rates for three tasks, even outperforming human players after just a few hours of training.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Rollback: Experience Backtracking in World Models for Policy Improvement", "summary": "Action-conditioned world models offer a scalable alternative to costly real-world interaction for improving robot policies. However, existing world-model-based methods largely treat world models as forward-only surrogates of the physical environment, leaving untapped a capability no physical environment affords: rewinding to past decision points to explore counterfactual futures. We introduce Rollback, a policy improvement paradigm that turns world models from passive simulators into counterfactual engines: it rolls back to critical decision points, branches into alternative actions, and contrasts their outcomes to attribute success to individual decisions. This concentrates imagination where it matters most and distills counterfactual outcomes into fine-grained, action-level supervision without a separately learned value model. We build a scalable training system that unifies distributed world-model search with asynchronous real-robot execution, forming a self-reinforcing cycle: real-world experience continually sharpens the world model, and a sharper world model in turn drives further policy improvement. We term the world-model side of this cycle test-time physics grounding, a capability that hand-built physics simulators lack: while the world model serves as the training environment, real transitions from the policy it trains correct its dynamics through a lightweight LoRA pipeline. Experiments on three challenging real-world tasks show that Rollback improves success rates over the strongest baseline by an absolute 10.67%, 11.11%, and 11.85% on block extraction, ball tossing, and tabletop curling, respectively, while achieving more stable improvement throughout training. Although initialized from teleoperated demonstrations far below human level, Rollback surpasses the average success rate of human players on both ball tossing and tabletop curling within 8 hours of training.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.36068.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "66c4816e96583c59b09fec30", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/66c4816e96583c59b09fec30/RLurCsmcgOfyuWVkpsv1L.jpeg", "fullname": "Ryan Chen", "name": "ryancll118", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 4, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "676fc7c31c48eff17fac3135", "name": "agibot-world", "fullname": "AgiBot World", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64e57309b78bc92221ce3b70/ewI1QvFVMDgSsShQeSvlX.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.37666", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907233", "name": "Chaehyun Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907234", "name": "Chi-Pin Huang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907235", "name": "Jisoo Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907236", "name": "Min-Hung Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907237", "name": "Yu-Chiang Frank Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907238", "name": "Seungryong Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907239", "name": "Fu-En Yang", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/63f727b1bd28622c9b951114/upl0lRpybvBMpqUSl4LGv.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "ThinkWorld: World Imagination Reinforces Chain-of-Dynamics Reasoning", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "63f727b1bd28622c9b951114", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/0b52ae1ff473615fdd665cdbc91f6db9.svg", "isPro": true, "fullname": "Chaehyun Kim", "user": "chyun", "type": "user", "name": "chyun"}, "summary": "Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have made substantial progress in robotic manipulation, but their generalization remains constrained by the diversity and coverage of action-labeled robot demonstrations. Human videos provide much broader behavioral and environmental experience, while learning from them faces a fundamental trade-off between scalable data and explicit action grounding. We introduce ThinkWorld, a VLA learning framework that grounds action reasoning in world dynamics and strengthens it through reinforcement learning with world imagination. ThinkWorld represents each action thought as a Chain-of-Dynamics (CoD), a sequence of discrete motion tokens that captures the intermediate dynamics connecting the current world to a future state and serves as a common action representation across embodiments. We first initialize CoD with action-labeled demonstrations, then predict the future induced by each CoD and reinforce those whose predictions better match the observed outcome. This enables videos without action annotations to provide direct supervision from observed state transitions, allowing human and robot experience to refine the same reasoning space. Experiments in simulation and on real robots demonstrate effective human-to-robot transfer and strong generalization to unseen tasks and environments.", "upvotes": 36, "discussionId": "6ae15d94a5eb0647c290723a", "projectPage": "https://thinkworld-project.github.io/", "ai_summary": "ThinkWorld improves robotic generalization by representing actions as dynamics chains and using world imagination to learn from unlabeled human videos.", "ai_keywords": ["Vision-Language-Action models", "Chain-of-Dynamics", "motion tokens", "world dynamics", "reinforcement learning", "world imagination"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "60262b67268c201cdc8b7d43", "name": "nvidia", "fullname": "NVIDIA", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65df9200dc3292a8983e5017/Vs5FPVCH-VZBipV3qKTuy.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>Vision-Language-Action (VLA) \u6a21\u578b\u5728\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u64cd\u4f5c\u4e2d\u53d6\u5f97\u4e86\u663e\u8457\u8fdb\u5c55\uff0c\u4f46\u5176\u6cdb\u5316\u80fd\u529b\u53d7\u5230\u52a8\u4f5c\u6807\u8bb0\u793a\u8303\u591a\u6837\u6027\u548c\u8986\u76d6\u8303\u56f4\u7684\u9650\u5236\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4eba\u7c7b\u89c6\u9891\u63d0\u4f9b\u4e86\u66f4\u5e7f\u6cdb\u7684\u884c\u4e3a\u548c\u73af\u5883\u7ecf\u9a8c\uff0c\u4f46\u5728\u5b66\u4e60\u65f6\u9762\u4e34\u6570\u636e\u89c4\u6a21\u4e0e\u660e\u786e\u52a8\u4f5c\u57fa\u7840\u4e4b\u95f4\u7684\u6743\u8861\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86 ThinkWorld\uff0c\u4e00\u79cd\u5c06\u52a8\u4f5c\u63a8\u7406\u4e0e\u4e16\u754c\u52a8\u6001\u76f8\u7ed3\u5408\u7684 VLA \u5b66\u4e60\u6846\u67b6\uff0c\u5e76\u901a\u8fc7\u5f3a\u5316\u5b66\u4e60\u4e0e\u4e16\u754c\u60f3\u8c61\u5f3a\u5316\u8be5\u63a8\u7406\u3002</li>\n    <li>ThinkWorld \u4f7f\u7528\u540d\u4e3a Chain-of-Dynamics (CoD) \u7684\u5e8f\u5217\u8868\u793a\u6bcf\u4e2a\u52a8\u4f5c\u601d\u7ef4\uff0c\u6355\u6349\u5f53\u524d\u4e16\u754c\u4e0e\u672a\u6765\u72b6\u6001\u4e4b\u95f4\u7684\u4e2d\u95f4\u52a8\u6001\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u8868\u660e\uff0c\u8be5\u65b9\u6cd5\u53ef\u4ee5\u6709\u6548\u5730\u5b9e\u73b0\u4eba\u7c7b\u4e0e\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u4e4b\u95f4\u7684\u77e5\u8bc6\u8f6c\u79fb\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u672a\u89c1\u4efb\u52a1\u548c\u73af\u5883\u4e2d\u8868\u73b0\u51fa\u826f\u597d\u7684\u6cdb\u5316\u80fd\u529b\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models help robots perform tasks, but they struggle to generalize due to limited action-labeled data.</li>\n    <li>Human videos offer a wider variety of experiences, but using them for training is challenging because of differences in data and action understanding.</li>\n    <li>ThinkWorld is a new framework that helps robots learn by connecting actions to the changing world around them.</li>\n    <li>It uses a method called Chain-of-Dynamics (CoD) to represent actions as sequences that show how the world changes from one state to another.</li>\n    <li>Experiments show that this approach improves the ability of robots to learn from human videos and perform new tasks successfully.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "ThinkWorld: World Imagination Reinforces Chain-of-Dynamics Reasoning", "summary": "Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have made substantial progress in robotic manipulation, but their generalization remains constrained by the diversity and coverage of action-labeled robot demonstrations. Human videos provide much broader behavioral and environmental experience, while learning from them faces a fundamental trade-off between scalable data and explicit action grounding. We introduce ThinkWorld, a VLA learning framework that grounds action reasoning in world dynamics and strengthens it through reinforcement learning with world imagination. ThinkWorld represents each action thought as a Chain-of-Dynamics (CoD), a sequence of discrete motion tokens that captures the intermediate dynamics connecting the current world to a future state and serves as a common action representation across embodiments. We first initialize CoD with action-labeled demonstrations, then predict the future induced by each CoD and reinforce those whose predictions better match the observed outcome. This enables videos without action annotations to provide direct supervision from observed state transitions, allowing human and robot experience to refine the same reasoning space. Experiments in simulation and on real robots demonstrate effective human-to-robot transfer and strong generalization to unseen tasks and environments.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/63f727b1bd28622c9b951114/upl0lRpybvBMpqUSl4LGv.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.37666.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "63f727b1bd28622c9b951114", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/0b52ae1ff473615fdd665cdbc91f6db9.svg", "fullname": "Chaehyun Kim", "name": "chyun", "type": "user", "isPro": true, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 2, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "60262b67268c201cdc8b7d43", "name": "nvidia", "fullname": "NVIDIA", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65df9200dc3292a8983e5017/Vs5FPVCH-VZBipV3qKTuy.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.32749", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae29be0d670fc6ea896c79e", "name": "Wendi Sang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae29be0d670fc6ea896c79f", "name": "Kai Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae29be0d670fc6ea896c7a0", "name": "Yifan Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae29be0d670fc6ea896c7a1", "name": "Jianqiang Huang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae29be0d670fc6ea896c7a2", "name": "Xiaolin Hu", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6387676c23da90491eb9fb16/fyfAlyMRka4HqWEFfbAoN.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-23T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Efficient Streaming Audio-Visual Target Speaker Extraction for Real-World Acoustic Scenes", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "6387676c23da90491eb9fb16", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1669818175965-noauth.jpeg", "isPro": true, "fullname": "Kai Li", "user": "JusperLee", "type": "user", "name": "JusperLee"}, "summary": "Streaming audio-visual target speaker extraction (AVTSE) is essential for latency-sensitive applications. However, current causal systems face two coupled gaps. First, standard benchmarks fix the number of speakers at two and ignore the near-/far-field interference, reverberation, and sudden noise of real scenes. Second, high-performance causal architectures are too heavy for edge deployment. Lightweight alternatives try to reclaim capacity by repeatedly invoking a small separator, which offsets the savings. To address the first gap, we release RealSSA, a realistic AVTSE benchmark consisting of RealSSA-Sim and RealSSA-Real. RealSSA-Sim provides controllable simulated scenes with 1--5 dynamic near-field interfering speakers; scenes with far-field interference additionally include 10--15 far-field interfering speakers. RealSSA-Real provides a held-out real-recorded evaluation set. To address the second gap, we propose Falcon, a causal AVTSE method. Its Separator performs multi-scale time-frequency separation in a single encoder--decoder pass. We train Falcon with an encoder-space speaker contrastive loss. This loss suppresses near-field leakage at zero inference cost and transfers across backbones. Falcon achieves state-of-the-art extraction quality on RealSSA-Sim, LRS2-2Mix, LRS3-2Mix, and VoxCeleb2-2Mix. Compared with the causal AV-TFGridNet baseline, it cuts parameters by 91.9%, computation by 20.2 times, and GPU inference latency by 11.1 times. Code and demo are available at https://cslikai.cn/Falcon/.", "upvotes": 32, "discussionId": "6ae29be1d670fc6ea896c7a3", "projectPage": "https://cslikai.cn/Falcon/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/JusperLee/Falcon", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "Falcon is a lightweight causal audio-visual target speaker extraction method that achieves high quality with drastically reduced parameters and latency, supported by the realistic RealSSA benchmark.", "ai_keywords": ["audio-visual target speaker extraction", "causal AVTSE", "RealSSA", "Falcon", "multi-scale time-frequency separation", "encoder-space speaker contrastive loss", "near-field leakage suppression"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 13, "organization": {"_id": "640d3084536d9fe0f005cac3", "name": "thu-ml", "fullname": "Tsinghua Machine Learning Group", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1678587085174-633131798ef21f47308ce49b.jpeg"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u63a8\u51fa\u4e86RealSSA\uff0c\u4e00\u4e2a\u65b0\u7684\u97f3\u89c6\u9891\u76ee\u6807\u8bf4\u8bdd\u4eba\u63d0\u53d6\u57fa\u51c6\uff0c\u5305\u542b\u6a21\u62df\u548c\u771f\u5b9e\u573a\u666f\u3002</li>\n    <li>RealSSA-Sim\u63d0\u4f9b\u4e861-5\u4e2a\u8fd1\u573a\u5e72\u6270\u8bf4\u8bdd\u4eba\u7684\u53ef\u63a7\u6a21\u62df\u573a\u666f\uff0c\u548c10-15\u4e2a\u8fdc\u573a\u5e72\u6270\u8bf4\u8bdd\u4eba\u7684\u573a\u666f\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86Falcon\uff0c\u4e00\u79cd\u8f7b\u91cf\u7ea7\u7684\u97f3\u89c6\u9891\u76ee\u6807\u8bf4\u8bdd\u4eba\u63d0\u53d6\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u80fd\u591f\u5728\u5355\u6b21\u7f16\u7801-\u89e3\u7801\u4e2d\u8fdb\u884c\u591a\u5c3a\u5ea6\u65f6\u95f4\u9891\u7387\u5206\u79bb\u3002</li>\n    <li>Falcon\u4f7f\u7528\u4e86\u4e00\u79cd\u7f16\u7801\u7a7a\u95f4\u8bf4\u8bdd\u4eba\u5bf9\u6bd4\u635f\u5931\uff0c\u6709\u6548\u6291\u5236\u8fd1\u573a\u6cc4\u6f0f\uff0c\u4e14\u51e0\u4e4e\u6ca1\u6709\u63a8\u7406\u6210\u672c\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4e0e\u57fa\u7ebf\u6a21\u578b\u76f8\u6bd4\uff0cFalcon\u5728\u53c2\u6570\u3001\u8ba1\u7b97\u91cf\u548cGPU\u63a8\u7406\u5ef6\u8fdf\u4e0a\u90fd\u6709\u663e\u8457\u51cf\u5c11\uff0c\u8fbe\u5230\u6700\u65b0\u63d0\u53d6\u8d28\u91cf\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>The study focuses on improving audio-visual target speaker extraction (AVTSE) for applications sensitive to delays.</li>\n    <li>Current benchmarks only test with two speakers and do not account for real-world noises and interference.</li>\n    <li>RealSSA is introduced as a new benchmark featuring both simulated and real-world audio scenes with multiple speakers and noises.</li>\n    <li>Falcon is a new AVTSE method that uses a unique approach to separate sounds efficiently while reducing the size and speed requirements for edge devices.</li>\n    <li>Falcon significantly outperforms previous models, reducing parameters and computation while improving extraction quality.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-22T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Efficient Streaming Audio-Visual Target Speaker Extraction for Real-World Acoustic Scenes", "summary": "Streaming audio-visual target speaker extraction (AVTSE) is essential for latency-sensitive applications. However, current causal systems face two coupled gaps. First, standard benchmarks fix the number of speakers at two and ignore the near-/far-field interference, reverberation, and sudden noise of real scenes. Second, high-performance causal architectures are too heavy for edge deployment. Lightweight alternatives try to reclaim capacity by repeatedly invoking a small separator, which offsets the savings. To address the first gap, we release RealSSA, a realistic AVTSE benchmark consisting of RealSSA-Sim and RealSSA-Real. RealSSA-Sim provides controllable simulated scenes with 1--5 dynamic near-field interfering speakers; scenes with far-field interference additionally include 10--15 far-field interfering speakers. RealSSA-Real provides a held-out real-recorded evaluation set. To address the second gap, we propose Falcon, a causal AVTSE method. Its Separator performs multi-scale time-frequency separation in a single encoder--decoder pass. We train Falcon with an encoder-space speaker contrastive loss. This loss suppresses near-field leakage at zero inference cost and transfers across backbones. Falcon achieves state-of-the-art extraction quality on RealSSA-Sim, LRS2-2Mix, LRS3-2Mix, and VoxCeleb2-2Mix. Compared with the causal AV-TFGridNet baseline, it cuts parameters by 91.9%, computation by 20.2 times, and GPU inference latency by 11.1 times. Code and demo are available at https://cslikai.cn/Falcon/.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6387676c23da90491eb9fb16/fyfAlyMRka4HqWEFfbAoN.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.32749.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "6387676c23da90491eb9fb16", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1669818175965-noauth.jpeg", "fullname": "Kai Li", "name": "JusperLee", "type": "user", "isPro": true, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 62, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "640d3084536d9fe0f005cac3", "name": "thu-ml", "fullname": "Tsinghua Machine Learning Group", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1678587085174-633131798ef21f47308ce49b.jpeg"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.36456", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070c7", "name": "Yuxuan Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070c8", "name": "Feng Xie", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070c9", "name": "Jiagao Hu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070ca", "name": "Fuhao Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070cb", "name": "Zepeng Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070cc", "name": "Fei Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070cd", "name": "Daiguo Zhou", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/649176436cadae13f22d014b/5rZZkERA6tUqDATPYhbhm.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "LociArt: A Unified Paradigm for Free-Style Local Video Stylization", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "649176436cadae13f22d014b", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/2cab0804761d986e2769d68063a59081.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Higher", "user": "HigherHu", "type": "user", "name": "HigherHu"}, "summary": "Video stylization typically transforms entire scenes, while local artistic editing requires precise region--style association, spatial control, and temporal consistency. We introduce LociArt, a unified paradigm for free-style local video stylization that explicitly specifies where to stylize and what style to apply while preserving non-target content. At its core, GRNStyle builds on Generative Refinement Networks (GRN) and introduces a polar style-aware mask that embeds style identities as equal-norm directions within selected regions. Complementary representation--geometry conditioning combines encoded mask features with raw-mask spatial support, integrating spatiotemporal context with geometric control. To provide paired supervision, we develop an automated pipeline combining global stylization, video object removal, matting, and compositing, and construct the LociArt-100K Dataset with 100K local stylization pairs. We further establish the LociArt Benchmark, comprising 80 source videos and 84 target masks covering 168 foreground/background stylization cases, with a Style Prototype Similarity (SPS) evaluation scheme for regional style quality and complementary measures of non-target preservation and temporal consistency. Experiments show that GRNStyle outperforms existing methods in regional style quality and spatial control while maintaining strong temporal consistency. Qualitative examples further illustrate joint stylization of multiple regions with the same or different learned styles, despite training only on single-region, single-style pairs. Together, these components provide a unified foundation for learning and evaluating controllable local video stylization. Project page: https://higherhu.github.io/lociart/.", "upvotes": 25, "discussionId": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070ce", "projectPage": "https://higherhu.github.io/lociart/", "ai_summary": "LociArt enables precise local video stylization with region-style control and temporal consistency via a generative refinement network, automated dataset construction, and a dedicated benchmark.", "ai_keywords": ["Generative Refinement Networks", "polar style-aware mask", "representation-geometry conditioning", "LociArt-100K Dataset", "Style Prototype Similarity", "local video stylization", "temporal consistency"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "6821ba7e5a7efab94a235406", "name": "xiaomi-research", "fullname": "Xiaomi Research", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/673735e4373ad40af7f81ea1/DR4m0bz2Du1l0Z8Txg351.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86\u4e00\u79cd\u540d\u4e3a LociArt \u7684\u65b0\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u53ef\u4ee5\u8fdb\u884c\u81ea\u7531\u98ce\u683c\u7684\u5c40\u90e8\u89c6\u9891\u98ce\u683c\u5316\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8be5\u65b9\u6cd5\u5141\u8bb8\u7528\u6237\u7cbe\u786e\u6307\u5b9a\u60f3\u8981\u98ce\u683c\u5316\u7684\u533a\u57df\u548c\u5e94\u7528\u7684\u98ce\u683c\uff0c\u540c\u65f6\u4fdd\u6301\u975e\u76ee\u6807\u5185\u5bb9\u4e0d\u53d8\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4f7f\u7528\u751f\u6210\u7cbe\u70bc\u7f51\u7edc\uff08GRN\uff09\u548c\u6837\u5f0f\u611f\u77e5\u63a9\u7801\uff0c\u4ee5\u5b9e\u73b0\u7a7a\u95f4\u63a7\u5236\u548c\u65f6\u95f4\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5f00\u53d1\u4e86\u4e00\u4e2a\u81ea\u52a8\u5316\u6d41\u7a0b\uff0c\u7ed3\u5408\u5168\u7403\u98ce\u683c\u5316\u3001\u89c6\u9891\u5bf9\u8c61\u79fb\u9664\u3001\u62a0\u56fe\u548c\u5408\u6210\uff0c\u6784\u5efa\u4e86\u5305\u542b 10 \u4e07\u5bf9\u5c40\u90e8\u98ce\u683c\u5316\u7684 LociArt-100K \u6570\u636e\u96c6\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u7ed3\u679c\u8868\u660e\uff0cGRNStyle \u5728\u533a\u57df\u98ce\u683c\u8d28\u91cf\u548c\u7a7a\u95f4\u63a7\u5236\u65b9\u9762\u4f18\u4e8e\u73b0\u6709\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u5e76\u4e14\u4fdd\u6301\u4e86\u826f\u597d\u7684\u65f6\u95f4\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>LociArt is a new method for local video stylization that allows users to choose specific areas to apply artistic styles while keeping the rest of the video unchanged.</li>\n    <li>It uses Generative Refinement Networks (GRN) and a special mask to define styles for different regions in a video.</li>\n    <li>They created a large dataset (LociArt-100K) with 100,000 pairs of videos and stylizations to train their model.</li>\n    <li>LociArt Benchmark includes 80 videos and 84 masks to evaluate the quality of style application and consistency over time.</li>\n    <li>Tests show that GRNStyle is better than other methods at maintaining style quality and ensuring smooth transitions in videos.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "LociArt: A Unified Paradigm for Free-Style Local Video Stylization", "summary": "Video stylization typically transforms entire scenes, while local artistic editing requires precise region--style association, spatial control, and temporal consistency. We introduce LociArt, a unified paradigm for free-style local video stylization that explicitly specifies where to stylize and what style to apply while preserving non-target content. At its core, GRNStyle builds on Generative Refinement Networks (GRN) and introduces a polar style-aware mask that embeds style identities as equal-norm directions within selected regions. Complementary representation--geometry conditioning combines encoded mask features with raw-mask spatial support, integrating spatiotemporal context with geometric control. To provide paired supervision, we develop an automated pipeline combining global stylization, video object removal, matting, and compositing, and construct the LociArt-100K Dataset with 100K local stylization pairs. We further establish the LociArt Benchmark, comprising 80 source videos and 84 target masks covering 168 foreground/background stylization cases, with a Style Prototype Similarity (SPS) evaluation scheme for regional style quality and complementary measures of non-target preservation and temporal consistency. Experiments show that GRNStyle outperforms existing methods in regional style quality and spatial control while maintaining strong temporal consistency. Qualitative examples further illustrate joint stylization of multiple regions with the same or different learned styles, despite training only on single-region, single-style pairs. Together, these components provide a unified foundation for learning and evaluating controllable local video stylization. Project page: https://higherhu.github.io/lociart/.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/649176436cadae13f22d014b/5rZZkERA6tUqDATPYhbhm.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.36456.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "649176436cadae13f22d014b", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/2cab0804761d986e2769d68063a59081.svg", "fullname": "Higher", "name": "HigherHu", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 1, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6821ba7e5a7efab94a235406", "name": "xiaomi-research", "fullname": "Xiaomi Research", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/673735e4373ad40af7f81ea1/DR4m0bz2Du1l0Z8Txg351.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.35684", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae2bb70d670fc6ea896c825", "name": "Junyeong Ahn", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2bb70d670fc6ea896c826", "name": "Geonwoo Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2bb70d670fc6ea896c827", "name": "Kinam Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2bb70d670fc6ea896c828", "name": "Gihwi Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2bb70d670fc6ea896c829", "name": "Hoonjin Jung", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2bb70d670fc6ea896c82a", "name": "Hyojin Jang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2bb70d670fc6ea896c82b", "name": "Jaegul Choo", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/632872d5ed473b0259e482fa/eEUbC0NeLSc1fr70XJZGw.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "OmniShowcase: Omnidirectional Subject-Consistent Video Generation from Multi-View Images", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "632872d5ed473b0259e482fa", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/f05033fdcc255b4d5eb15109f3043c22.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Junyeong Ahn", "user": "Justin4AI", "type": "user", "name": "Justin4AI"}, "summary": "Generating videos of a specific subject from a few reference images remains challenging when the camera reveals surfaces that are not visible in the input. Early subject-to-video (S2V) methods condition on a single reference image and must therefore infer the subject's unseen appearance and geometry as the viewpoint changes. To address this limitation, recent methods extend S2V to multiple reference views, providing more information about the subject from different viewpoints. However, they do not explicitly specify the 3D structure that connects these observations. In addition, existing training data do not guarantee that viewpoint changes preserve the same underlying 3D subject. We present OmniShowcase, built on two components: (i) a fully automated data synthesis pipeline that produces training videos along diverse camera trajectories, with viewpoint variation driven by underlying subject geometry, even spanning full 360-degree orbits; and (ii) a geometry-conditioning method that provides the video model with a turntable of surface-normal maps reconstructed from the reference views without requiring temporal alignment with the generated frames. We construct Showcase-4K, a corpus of 3D-asset-based videos covering twelve camera motions across 88 trajectory configurations with reconstruction-derived surface-normal conditioning, and ShowcaseBench, a held-out evaluation set with the source meshes and camera parameters used to render each video. Experiments show state-of-the-art subject identity and 3D consistency while maintaining competitive video quality and motion. Project page: justin4ai.github.io/OmniShowcase", "upvotes": 19, "discussionId": "6ae2bb71d670fc6ea896c82c", "projectPage": "https://justin4ai.github.io/OmniShowcase/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/justin4ai/OmniShowcase", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "OmniShowcase improves subject-to-video generation by using geometry-driven training data and surface-normal conditioning to maintain 3D consistency across novel viewpoints.", "ai_keywords": ["subject-to-video", "surface-normal maps", "geometry-conditioning", "3D consistency", "data synthesis pipeline", "camera trajectories"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 4, "organization": {"_id": "6475760c33192631bad2bb38", "name": "kaist-ai", "fullname": "KAIST AI", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6469949654873f0043b09c22/aaZFiyXe1qR-Dmy_xq67m.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n  <li>\u751f\u6210\u7279\u5b9a\u4e3b\u9898\u7684\u89c6\u9891\u4ecd\u7136\u5177\u6709\u6311\u6218\u6027\uff0c\u5c24\u5176\u662f\u5f53\u76f8\u673a\u663e\u793a\u8f93\u5165\u4e2d\u4e0d\u53ef\u89c1\u7684\u8868\u9762\u65f6\u3002</li>\n  <li>\u65e9\u671f\u7684\u65b9\u6cd5\u4f9d\u8d56\u5355\u4e2a\u53c2\u8003\u56fe\u50cf\uff0c\u96be\u4ee5\u63a8\u65ad\u4e3b\u9898\u5728\u4e0d\u540c\u89c6\u89d2\u4e0b\u7684\u5916\u89c2\u548c\u51e0\u4f55\u5f62\u72b6\u3002</li>\n  <li>\u6700\u8fd1\u7684\u65b9\u6cd5\u4f7f\u7528\u591a\u4e2a\u53c2\u8003\u89c6\u56fe\uff0c\u4f46\u672a\u660e\u786e\u6307\u5b9a\u8fd9\u4e9b\u89c2\u5bdf\u4e4b\u95f4\u76843D\u7ed3\u6784\u3002</li>\n  <li>\u6211\u4eec\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86OmniShowcase\uff0c\u5305\u62ec\u81ea\u52a8\u6570\u636e\u5408\u6210\u548c\u51e0\u4f55\u6761\u4ef6\u5316\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u4ee5\u6539\u8fdb\u89c6\u9891\u751f\u6210\u3002</li>\n  <li>\u6784\u5efa\u4e86Showcase-4K\u89c6\u9891\u5e93\u548cShowcaseBench\u8bc4\u4f30\u96c6\uff0c\u5b9e\u9a8c\u8868\u660e\u5177\u6709\u5148\u8fdb\u7684\u4e3b\u9898\u8bc6\u522b\u548c3D\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Creating videos from reference images is hard, especially when parts of the subject aren't visible.</li>\n    <li>Most methods rely on a single image and struggle to show how the subject looks from different angles.</li>\n    <li>Newer methods use multiple images for better perspective but don't clarify the 3D structure of the subject.</li>\n    <li>OmniShowcase introduces a system that generates training videos using diverse camera angles based on 3D geometry.</li>\n    <li>It includes a large dataset called Showcase-4K with various camera movements and a separate evaluation set for testing.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "OmniShowcase: Omnidirectional Subject-Consistent Video Generation from Multi-View Images", "summary": "Generating videos of a specific subject from a few reference images remains challenging when the camera reveals surfaces that are not visible in the input. Early subject-to-video (S2V) methods condition on a single reference image and must therefore infer the subject's unseen appearance and geometry as the viewpoint changes. To address this limitation, recent methods extend S2V to multiple reference views, providing more information about the subject from different viewpoints. However, they do not explicitly specify the 3D structure that connects these observations. In addition, existing training data do not guarantee that viewpoint changes preserve the same underlying 3D subject. We present OmniShowcase, built on two components: (i) a fully automated data synthesis pipeline that produces training videos along diverse camera trajectories, with viewpoint variation driven by underlying subject geometry, even spanning full 360-degree orbits; and (ii) a geometry-conditioning method that provides the video model with a turntable of surface-normal maps reconstructed from the reference views without requiring temporal alignment with the generated frames. We construct Showcase-4K, a corpus of 3D-asset-based videos covering twelve camera motions across 88 trajectory configurations with reconstruction-derived surface-normal conditioning, and ShowcaseBench, a held-out evaluation set with the source meshes and camera parameters used to render each video. Experiments show state-of-the-art subject identity and 3D consistency while maintaining competitive video quality and motion. Project page: justin4ai.github.io/OmniShowcase", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/632872d5ed473b0259e482fa/eEUbC0NeLSc1fr70XJZGw.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.35684.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "632872d5ed473b0259e482fa", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/f05033fdcc255b4d5eb15109f3043c22.svg", "fullname": "Junyeong Ahn", "name": "Justin4AI", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6475760c33192631bad2bb38", "name": "kaist-ai", "fullname": "KAIST AI", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6469949654873f0043b09c22/aaZFiyXe1qR-Dmy_xq67m.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.37230", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae1d18da5eb0647c290774c", "name": "Shuhuai Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1d18da5eb0647c290774d", "name": "Shengdong Ye", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1d18da5eb0647c290774e", "name": "Dongdong Ge", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1d18da5eb0647c290774f", "name": "Yinyu Ye", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1d18da5eb0647c2907750", "name": "Jianghao Lin", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "TAO: Tool-Aware Joint Scheduling for LLM-Based Agentic Serving", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "661cb57f851baf948bca33a4", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/5077723bfe0b5600f0346cc995cc0dc1.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Li shuhuai", "user": "lsh-algorithm", "type": "user", "name": "lsh-algorithm"}, "summary": "Agentic services alternate between model inference on clustered GPUs and external tools on distributed CPUs. In this scenario, existing systems (1) overlook tool-return readiness in service evaluation, wasting limited resources, and (2) use heuristic strategies to decide admission and placement on GPUs, resulting in suboptimal scheduling decisions. To bridge this gap, we present TAO, a tool-aware joint scheduling framework for LLM-based agentic serving. Its value interface combines near-term tool readiness, node-specific cache credit with time-aware decay, and token footprint; a dynamic program jointly selects and places waiting sessions under capacity constraints. Across the 12 reported model-workload-concurrency settings, TAO reduces latency by up to 26.8%, reduces mean time to first token (TTFT) by up to 36.3%, and increases decode throughput by up to 29.5% relative to the best evaluated state-of-the-art baseline for each metric.", "upvotes": 2, "discussionId": "6ae1d18ea5eb0647c2907751", "ai_summary": "TAO improves scheduling for LLM agent services by considering tool readiness and using dynamic programming to place sessions, reducing latency and increasing throughput.", "ai_keywords": ["LLM-based agentic serving", "tool-aware scheduling", "tool-return readiness", "node-specific cache credit", "time-aware decay", "token footprint", "dynamic programming", "admission and placement", "decode throughput", "time to first token (TTFT)"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "6a914351ceb09c999ea12f84", "name": "AIOR-Research", "fullname": "AIOR", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/63f33193520c146189241780/UyBZGY7cm0LlIbu8qm0H4.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u4ee3\u7406\u670d\u52a1\u5728\u96c6\u7fa4GPU\u4e0a\u8fdb\u884c\u6a21\u578b\u63a8\u7406\uff0c\u5e76\u5229\u7528\u5206\u5e03\u5f0fCPU\u7684\u5916\u90e8\u5de5\u5177\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u73b0\u6709\u7cfb\u7edf\u5728\u670d\u52a1\u8bc4\u4f30\u4e2d\u5ffd\u89c6\u5de5\u5177\u51c6\u5907\u60c5\u51b5\uff0c\u6d6a\u8d39\u6709\u9650\u8d44\u6e90\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4f7f\u7528\u542f\u53d1\u5f0f\u7b56\u7565\u51b3\u5b9aGPU\u7684\u63a5\u7eb3\u548c\u653e\u7f6e\uff0c\u5bfc\u81f4\u8c03\u5ea6\u51b3\u7b56\u4e0d\u7406\u60f3\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86TAO\u6846\u67b6\uff0c\u7ed3\u5408\u5de5\u5177\u51c6\u5907\u60c5\u51b5\u548c\u8282\u70b9\u7f13\u5b58\uff0c\u8fdb\u884c\u52a8\u6001\u8c03\u5ea6\u3002</li>\n    <li>TAO\u5728\u591a\u79cd\u8bbe\u7f6e\u4e0b\u663e\u8457\u964d\u4f4e\u5ef6\u8fdf\u548c\u9996\u6b21\u4ee4\u724c\u65f6\u95f4\uff0c\u63d0\u9ad8\u89e3\u7801\u541e\u5410\u91cf\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n  <li>Current systems don't consider if tools are ready when evaluating services, wasting resources.</li>\n  <li>They also use basic strategies for deciding how to manage GPUs, leading to poor scheduling.</li>\n  <li>TAO is a new framework that improves scheduling for AI services by considering tool readiness and resource use.</li>\n  <li>It combines several factors like cache, time, and demand to make better decisions about resource allocation.</li>\n  <li>TAO significantly improves performance, reducing latency and increasing throughput compared to existing methods.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "TAO: Tool-Aware Joint Scheduling for LLM-Based Agentic Serving", "summary": "Agentic services alternate between model inference on clustered GPUs and external tools on distributed CPUs. In this scenario, existing systems (1) overlook tool-return readiness in service evaluation, wasting limited resources, and (2) use heuristic strategies to decide admission and placement on GPUs, resulting in suboptimal scheduling decisions. To bridge this gap, we present TAO, a tool-aware joint scheduling framework for LLM-based agentic serving. Its value interface combines near-term tool readiness, node-specific cache credit with time-aware decay, and token footprint; a dynamic program jointly selects and places waiting sessions under capacity constraints. Across the 12 reported model-workload-concurrency settings, TAO reduces latency by up to 26.8%, reduces mean time to first token (TTFT) by up to 36.3%, and increases decode throughput by up to 29.5% relative to the best evaluated state-of-the-art baseline for each metric.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.37230.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "661cb57f851baf948bca33a4", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/5077723bfe0b5600f0346cc995cc0dc1.svg", "fullname": "Li shuhuai", "name": "lsh-algorithm", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 1, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6a914351ceb09c999ea12f84", "name": "AIOR-Research", "fullname": "AIOR", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/63f33193520c146189241780/UyBZGY7cm0LlIbu8qm0H4.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.31670", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae2c512d670fc6ea896c854", "name": "Yunfei Xie", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2c512d670fc6ea896c855", "name": "Howard H. Qian", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2c512d670fc6ea896c856", "name": "Shiyi Lan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2c512d670fc6ea896c857", "name": "Kaiyu Hang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2c512d670fc6ea896c858", "name": "Chen Wei", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65f6afa36ea40b9a29dd45ca/XSpr8GDMKI7TKNxxE1l2e.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-23T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Sensorimotor Alignment: A Platonic Proxy for Embodied Policy Success", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "65f6afa36ea40b9a29dd45ca", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/f8ece25687802cede4b23280325fb4c8.svg", "isPro": true, "fullname": "Yunfei Xie", "user": "yunfeixie", "type": "user", "name": "yunfeixie"}, "summary": "Robot foundation models are typically evaluated through costly closed-loop rollouts, motivating low-cost offline proxies for model capability. The Platonic Representation Hypothesis (PRH) suggests that as representations improve, modalities such as vision and language converge toward a shared statistical model of reality. We ask whether motion trajectories form another such modality: as embodied models become more capable, should their sensory representations converge toward the structure of physical motion? To test this, we propose Sensorimotor Alignment (S^2), a training-free offline metric that measures how well a model's sensory representation aligns with the geometry of physical motion. We find that S^2 correlates positively with downstream robot task success across model families, making it a low-cost offline proxy for prioritizing models before closed-loop evaluation. S^2 can also identify promising vision-language model (VLM) backbones before costly fine-tuning into vision-language-action models (VLAs). Beyond this correlation, S^2 offers representation-level explanations for phenomena observed in VLAs and world action models (WAMs). With S^2, we extend PRH to robotics and make sensorimotor alignment measurable, offering a low-cost, representation-level view of robot foundation models that closed-loop success rates alone cannot reveal.", "upvotes": 1, "discussionId": "6ae2c512d670fc6ea896c859", "projectPage": "https://yunfeixie233.github.io/s2/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/yunfeixie233/sensorimotor-alignment", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "Sensorimotor alignment measures how sensory representations match physical motion geometry, serving as a low-cost offline predictor of robot model performance.", "ai_keywords": ["Platonic Representation Hypothesis", "Sensorimotor Alignment", "vision-language-action models", "world action models", "sensory representations", "physical motion"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "674fce4ce838eaae7482802e", "name": "RiceUniversity", "fullname": "Rice University", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6630443e246247eeadf6e565/RsWYMEcEsnef5OsxZAZpp.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u57fa\u7840\u6a21\u578b\u901a\u5e38\u901a\u8fc7\u6602\u8d35\u7684\u95ed\u73af\u6d4b\u8bd5\u8bc4\u4f30\uff0c\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86\u4f4e\u6210\u672c\u7684\u79bb\u7ebf\u4ee3\u7406\u65b9\u6cd5\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86\u201c\u67cf\u62c9\u56fe\u8868\u5f81\u5047\u8bf4\u201d\uff0c\u8ba4\u4e3a\u968f\u7740\u8868\u5f81\u7684\u6539\u5584\uff0c\u89c6\u89c9\u548c\u8bed\u8a00\u7b49\u6a21\u6001\u4f1a\u8d8b\u5411\u4e8e\u5171\u4eab\u7684\u7edf\u8ba1\u6a21\u578b\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u7814\u7a76\u8fd0\u52a8\u8f68\u8ff9\u662f\u5426\u4e5f\u5f62\u6210\u4e00\u79cd\u6a21\u6001\uff0c\u63a2\u8ba8\u8eab\u4f53\u5316\u6a21\u578b\u7684\u611f\u77e5\u8868\u5f81\u662f\u5426\u4e0e\u7269\u7406\u8fd0\u52a8\u7684\u7ed3\u6784\u8d8b\u540c\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86\u4f20\u611f\u8fd0\u52a8\u5bf9\u9f50\uff08S^2\uff09\u4f5c\u4e3a\u4e00\u79cd\u65e0\u8bad\u7ec3\u7684\u79bb\u7ebf\u6307\u6807\uff0c\u7528\u4e8e\u8bc4\u4f30\u6a21\u578b\u7684\u611f\u77e5\u8868\u5f81\u4e0e\u7269\u7406\u8fd0\u52a8\u51e0\u4f55\u7ed3\u6784\u7684\u5bf9\u9f50\u7a0b\u5ea6\u3002</li>\n    <li>S^2\u4e0e\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u4efb\u52a1\u6210\u529f\u7387\u6b63\u76f8\u5173\uff0c\u4e3a\u6a21\u578b\u9009\u62e9\u63d0\u4f9b\u4e86\u4f4e\u6210\u672c\u7684\u79bb\u7ebf\u4ee3\u7406\uff0c\u5e76\u4e3a\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u57fa\u7840\u6a21\u578b\u7684\u8868\u5f81\u63d0\u4f9b\u4e86\u65b0\u7684\u53ef\u6d4b\u91cf\u89c6\u89d2\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Robot foundation models are usually tested through expensive methods, leading to the need for cheaper evaluation techniques.</li>\n    <li>The Platonic Representation Hypothesis (PRH) suggests that as models improve, different types of data (like vision and language) start to align with a common understanding of reality.</li>\n    <li>This study explores whether motion paths (trajectories) also align with improved model capabilities, proposing a new metric called Sensorimotor Alignment (S^2).</li>\n    <li>S^2 measures how well a model's sensory data matches the patterns of physical motion, and it has been found to predict the success of robot tasks.</li>\n    <li>Using S^2 can help identify effective models before expensive testing and offers insights into how different representations work in robotics.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-22T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Sensorimotor Alignment: A Platonic Proxy for Embodied Policy Success", "summary": "Robot foundation models are typically evaluated through costly closed-loop rollouts, motivating low-cost offline proxies for model capability. The Platonic Representation Hypothesis (PRH) suggests that as representations improve, modalities such as vision and language converge toward a shared statistical model of reality. We ask whether motion trajectories form another such modality: as embodied models become more capable, should their sensory representations converge toward the structure of physical motion? To test this, we propose Sensorimotor Alignment (S^2), a training-free offline metric that measures how well a model's sensory representation aligns with the geometry of physical motion. We find that S^2 correlates positively with downstream robot task success across model families, making it a low-cost offline proxy for prioritizing models before closed-loop evaluation. S^2 can also identify promising vision-language model (VLM) backbones before costly fine-tuning into vision-language-action models (VLAs). Beyond this correlation, S^2 offers representation-level explanations for phenomena observed in VLAs and world action models (WAMs). With S^2, we extend PRH to robotics and make sensorimotor alignment measurable, offering a low-cost, representation-level view of robot foundation models that closed-loop success rates alone cannot reveal.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65f6afa36ea40b9a29dd45ca/XSpr8GDMKI7TKNxxE1l2e.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.31670.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "65f6afa36ea40b9a29dd45ca", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/f8ece25687802cede4b23280325fb4c8.svg", "fullname": "Yunfei Xie", "name": "yunfeixie", "type": "user", "isPro": true, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 8, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "674fce4ce838eaae7482802e", "name": "RiceUniversity", "fullname": "Rice University", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6630443e246247eeadf6e565/RsWYMEcEsnef5OsxZAZpp.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}],
    "week": [{"paper": {"id": "2610.35201", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7ae", "name": "Hongze Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7af", "name": "Miao Pan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b0", "name": "Lizhou Liang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b1", "name": "Qinfeng Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b2", "name": "Xinyin Ma", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b3", "name": "Yongliang Shen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b4", "name": "Weishi Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b5", "name": "Daniel Dahlmeier", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b6", "name": "Xuhong Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b7", "name": "Wenqi Zhang", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d/-HwSwdNgn5m4Bx9b0Riez.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "DTCA: Denoising Token Credit Assignment for Reinforcement Learning in Diffusion Language Models", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/18348cb2ef91757f3450c18debd11599.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Wenqi Zhang", "user": "zwq2018", "type": "user", "name": "zwq2018"}, "summary": "Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate text through iterative denoising, enabling bidirectional context modeling and parallel token refinement. Recent studies have applied reinforcement learning (RL) to DLMs and improved their reasoning performance. However, the iterative denoising process makes credit assignment more challenging, as final outcome rewards provide limited information about how different tokens contribute to the final output. In this paper, we propose Denoising Token Credit Assignment (DTCA), a framework for token-level credit assignment in reinforcement learning for diffusion language models. DTCA estimates token contributions by measuring the improvement in prediction confidence of remaining masked positions after token commitment and groups these signals according to part-of-speech (POS) categories. These contribution weights are used to select informative denoising timesteps and assign token-level learning signals during RL training. DTCA further calibrates these learning signals according to token-level entropy changes induced by policy updates. Experiments on LLaDA-8B-Instruct show that DTCA consistently improves mathematical reasoning performance across different generation budgets, with gains of up to 9.0 points on MATH500 and 5.3 points on GSM8K over the base model.", "upvotes": 254, "discussionId": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b8", "projectPage": "https://zju-omniai.github.io/DTCA/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/ZJU-OmniAI/DTCA", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "DTCA improves reinforcement learning for diffusion language models by assigning token-level credit via confidence improvements and entropy calibration, boosting mathematical reasoning.", "ai_keywords": ["diffusion language models", "reinforcement learning", "token-level credit assignment", "denoising token credit assignment", "part-of-speech categories", "denoising timesteps", "token-level entropy"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 3, "organization": {"_id": "696461ab2d94e9a07cdb8efd", "name": "OmniAI-ZJU", "fullname": "ZJU-OmniAI", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65f2595830354d0ee043b25a/eEeRdHlGyJ148JQ6BAJ4O.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u6269\u6563\u8bed\u8a00\u6a21\u578b\uff08DLMs\uff09\u901a\u8fc7\u8fed\u4ee3\u53bb\u566a\u751f\u6210\u6587\u672c\uff0c\u652f\u6301\u53cc\u5411\u4e0a\u4e0b\u6587\u5efa\u6a21\u548c\u5e76\u884c\u4ee4\u724c\u4f18\u5316\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6700\u8fd1\u7684\u7814\u7a76\u5c06\u5f3a\u5316\u5b66\u4e60\uff08RL\uff09\u5e94\u7528\u4e8eDLMs\uff0c\u63d0\u5347\u4e86\u63a8\u7406\u6027\u80fd\uff0c\u4f46\u53bb\u566a\u8fc7\u7a0b\u4f7f\u5f97\u4fe1\u8d37\u5206\u914d\u53d8\u5f97\u66f4\u52a0\u590d\u6742\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u672c\u6587\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86\u4e00\u79cd\u65b0\u7684\u6846\u67b6\u2014\u2014\u53bb\u566a\u4ee4\u724c\u4fe1\u8d37\u5206\u914d\uff08DTCA\uff09\uff0c\u7528\u4e8e\u5728\u5f3a\u5316\u5b66\u4e60\u4e2d\u5b9e\u73b0\u4ee4\u724c\u7ea7\u7684\u4fe1\u8d37\u5206\u914d\u3002</li>\n    <li>DTCA\u901a\u8fc7\u6d4b\u91cf\u4ee4\u724c\u627f\u8bfa\u540e\u5269\u4f59\u63a9\u7801\u4f4d\u7f6e\u7684\u9884\u6d4b\u7f6e\u4fe1\u5ea6\u6539\u5584\u6765\u4f30\u7b97\u4ee4\u724c\u8d21\u732e\uff0c\u5e76\u6309\u8bcd\u6027\u7c7b\u522b\u8fdb\u884c\u4fe1\u53f7\u5206\u7ec4\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u8868\u660e\uff0cDTCA\u5728\u4e0d\u540c\u751f\u6210\u9884\u7b97\u4e0b\u63d0\u5347\u4e86\u6570\u5b66\u63a8\u7406\u6027\u80fd\uff0cMATH500\u4e0a\u63d0\u5347\u4e86\u6700\u591a9.0\u5206\uff0cGSM8K\u4e0a\u63d0\u5347\u4e865.3\u5206\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate text by gradually removing noise, allowing for better understanding of context.</li>\n    <li>Reinforcement learning (RL) has been applied to DLMs to enhance their reasoning abilities.</li>\n    <li>However, it's hard to determine how much each word contributes to the final text because of the way DLMs work.</li>\n    <li>The paper introduces a new method called Denoising Token Credit Assignment (DTCA) to better assign credit to individual words in RL training.</li>\n    <li>Tests show that DTCA significantly improves mathematical reasoning in models, with notable performance increases in specific tasks.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "DTCA: Denoising Token Credit Assignment for Reinforcement Learning in Diffusion Language Models", "summary": "Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate text through iterative denoising, enabling bidirectional context modeling and parallel token refinement. Recent studies have applied reinforcement learning (RL) to DLMs and improved their reasoning performance. However, the iterative denoising process makes credit assignment more challenging, as final outcome rewards provide limited information about how different tokens contribute to the final output. In this paper, we propose Denoising Token Credit Assignment (DTCA), a framework for token-level credit assignment in reinforcement learning for diffusion language models. DTCA estimates token contributions by measuring the improvement in prediction confidence of remaining masked positions after token commitment and groups these signals according to part-of-speech (POS) categories. These contribution weights are used to select informative denoising timesteps and assign token-level learning signals during RL training. DTCA further calibrates these learning signals according to token-level entropy changes induced by policy updates. Experiments on LLaDA-8B-Instruct show that DTCA consistently improves mathematical reasoning performance across different generation budgets, with gains of up to 9.0 points on MATH500 and 5.3 points on GSM8K over the base model.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d/-HwSwdNgn5m4Bx9b0Riez.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.35201.png", "numComments": 3, "submittedBy": {"_id": "6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/18348cb2ef91757f3450c18debd11599.svg", "fullname": "Wenqi Zhang", "name": "zwq2018", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 16, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "696461ab2d94e9a07cdb8efd", "name": "OmniAI-ZJU", "fullname": "ZJU-OmniAI", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65f2595830354d0ee043b25a/eEeRdHlGyJ148JQ6BAJ4O.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.34283", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713b", "name": "Yihao Hu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713c", "name": "Yitong Lin", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713d", "name": "Pan Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713e", "name": "Zhihao Wen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713f", "name": "Hang Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907140", "name": "Yiwen Zhao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907141", "name": "Deyuan Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907142", "name": "Yutong Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907143", "name": "Mingda Wan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907144", "name": "Yuchen Mao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907145", "name": "Tao Lin", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "ChronoReplay: One Answer Interface Is Not Enough for Streaming Spatial Intelligence", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "67e40cea6d0207b1fa43c826", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/_OzAJCjFC49dEkHZm4AQb.png", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Yihao Hu", "user": "HU22333", "type": "user", "name": "HU22333"}, "summary": "Training a spatial VLM to answer which side an object lies on does not require it to preserve the metric displacement that determines that answer, while supervision for a distance estimate may leave categorical relations largely unconstrained. When post-training emphasizes one readout, improvements can therefore concentrate on that interface while transfer to others remains weak or even degrades; we call this observed specialization interface lock-in. ChronoReplay addresses it by replaying heterogeneous spatial supervision as chronologically ordered episodes, retaining source-native targets, and balancing supervision across families. Under the same observation access, a single ChronoReplay checkpoint improves OVO-S-Bench, OST-Bench, and UCS-Bench together, including a 13.41-point gain on OST-Bench, and achieves the highest three-benchmark mean among the supervision settings compared here. Same-data controls further separate the ingredients of this transfer: answer serialization redistributes gains across OST and UCS, family-balanced learning improves over ordinary joint SFT and natural turn weighting, and chronological replay exceeds a shifted-order control by about 2.41 OST points across two paired seeds. Adaptive frame selection then changes evidence access while keeping the trained model fixed, providing an additional inference-time gain at lower visual cost. Streaming spatial performance depends on what distinctions supervision preserves, how heterogeneous histories are organized during learning, and what causal evidence inference makes available at test time.", "upvotes": 77, "discussionId": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907146", "projectPage": "https://yihaohu0118.github.io/ChronoReplay/", "ai_summary": "ChronoReplay mitigates interface lock-in in spatial vision-language models by replaying heterogeneous spatial supervision chronologically, improving cross-task transfer and enabling efficient inference-time evidence selection.", "ai_keywords": ["spatial VLM", "interface lock-in", "ChronoReplay", "joint SFT", "adaptive frame selection", "OVO-S-Bench", "OST-Bench", "UCS-Bench"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "6650967d53061f0d1b5a7962", "name": "LINs-lab", "fullname": "LINs lab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65296b8a6cdea40585bd81e2/M80w6f0H8dfx3Sa1nVmfq.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u7a7a\u95f4\u89c6\u89c9\u8bed\u8a00\u6a21\u578b\uff08VLM\uff09\u7684\u8bad\u7ec3\u4e0d\u9700\u8981\u4fdd\u7559\u51b3\u5b9a\u7269\u4f53\u4f4d\u7f6e\u7684\u5ea6\u91cf\u4f4d\u79fb\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5f53\u8bad\u7ec3\u5f3a\u8c03\u5355\u4e00\u8f93\u51fa\u65f6\uff0c\u53ef\u80fd\u5bfc\u81f4\u5176\u4ed6\u63a5\u53e3\u7684\u8f6c\u79fb\u6548\u679c\u8f83\u5f31\uff0c\u79f0\u4e3a\u201c\u4e13\u4e1a\u5316\u63a5\u53e3\u9501\u5b9a\u201d\u3002</li>\n    <li>ChronoReplay\u901a\u8fc7\u987a\u5e8f\u91cd\u64ad\u4e0d\u540c\u7c7b\u578b\u7684\u7a7a\u95f4\u76d1\u7763\u6765\u89e3\u51b3\u8fd9\u4e00\u95ee\u9898\uff0c\u4fdd\u6301\u6e90\u76ee\u6807\u7684\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u4e0d\u540c\u7c7b\u522b\u4e4b\u95f4\u5e73\u8861\u76d1\u7763\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4f7f\u7528ChronoReplay\u7684\u6a21\u578b\u5728\u591a\u4e2a\u57fa\u51c6\u6d4b\u8bd5\u4e2d\u8868\u73b0\u4f18\u5f02\uff0c\u5305\u62ecOST-Bench\u63d0\u9ad8\u4e8613.41\u5206\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u6240\u6709\u76d1\u7763\u8bbe\u7f6e\u4e2d\u83b7\u5f97\u6700\u9ad8\u7684\u5e73\u5747\u5206\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u9002\u5e94\u6027\u5e27\u9009\u62e9\u5728\u4fdd\u6301\u6a21\u578b\u4e0d\u53d8\u7684\u60c5\u51b5\u4e0b\u6539\u53d8\u8bc1\u636e\u83b7\u53d6\uff0c\u8fdb\u4e00\u6b65\u63d0\u5347\u63a8\u7406\u65f6\u7684\u6027\u80fd\u3002 </li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Training a spatial Visual Language Model (VLM) to determine object positions doesn't always keep accurate distance measurements, leading to a focus on specific tasks instead of a balanced approach.</li>\n    <li>This issue, called \"specialization interface lock-in,\" can limit performance in other areas when training emphasizes one aspect too much.</li>\n    <li>ChronoReplay addresses this by using diverse spatial supervision in a chronological order, which helps improve overall performance across different benchmarks.</li>\n    <li>With ChronoReplay, there were significant gains in testing benchmarks, including a notable improvement of 13.41 points on OST-Bench, and it achieved the best average scores among the compared methods.</li>\n    <li>Adaptive frame selection allows for better use of evidence during testing, enhancing performance while minimizing visual processing demands.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-24T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "ChronoReplay: One Answer Interface Is Not Enough for Streaming Spatial Intelligence", "summary": "Training a spatial VLM to answer which side an object lies on does not require it to preserve the metric displacement that determines that answer, while supervision for a distance estimate may leave categorical relations largely unconstrained. When post-training emphasizes one readout, improvements can therefore concentrate on that interface while transfer to others remains weak or even degrades; we call this observed specialization interface lock-in. ChronoReplay addresses it by replaying heterogeneous spatial supervision as chronologically ordered episodes, retaining source-native targets, and balancing supervision across families. Under the same observation access, a single ChronoReplay checkpoint improves OVO-S-Bench, OST-Bench, and UCS-Bench together, including a 13.41-point gain on OST-Bench, and achieves the highest three-benchmark mean among the supervision settings compared here. Same-data controls further separate the ingredients of this transfer: answer serialization redistributes gains across OST and UCS, family-balanced learning improves over ordinary joint SFT and natural turn weighting, and chronological replay exceeds a shifted-order control by about 2.41 OST points across two paired seeds. Adaptive frame selection then changes evidence access while keeping the trained model fixed, providing an additional inference-time gain at lower visual cost. Streaming spatial performance depends on what distinctions supervision preserves, how heterogeneous histories are organized during learning, and what causal evidence inference makes available at test time.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.34283.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "67e40cea6d0207b1fa43c826", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/_OzAJCjFC49dEkHZm4AQb.png", "fullname": "Yihao Hu", "name": "HU22333", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 1, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6650967d53061f0d1b5a7962", "name": "LINs-lab", "fullname": "LINs lab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65296b8a6cdea40585bd81e2/M80w6f0H8dfx3Sa1nVmfq.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.33568", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7903", "name": "Yihao Hu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7904", "name": "Zhihao Wen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7905", "name": "Yitong Lin", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7906", "name": "Siyuan Lu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7907", "name": "Pan Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7908", "name": "Wei Wu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7909", "name": "Jiang-Ming Yang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a790a", "name": "Tao Lin", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-24T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Trajectory2Gym: Decision-Grounded Compositional Interaction Synthesis for Tool-Use Agents", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "67e40cea6d0207b1fa43c826", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/_OzAJCjFC49dEkHZm4AQb.png", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Yihao Hu", "user": "HU22333", "type": "user", "name": "HU22333"}, "summary": "Successful tool-use trajectories are not necessarily useful supervision. Even successful interactions may underrepresent multi-step dependencies or contain actions that are unjustified by the information visible to the agent at decision time. We identify two coupled requirements for effective trajectory supervision: compositional coverage, so that important observation-action dependencies are represented, and decision grounding, so that each supervised action is supported by the learner-visible interaction history. We introduce Trajectory2Gym, which constructs executable interactions around under-supported compositional structures and derives supervision only from grounded decisions in their realized executions. Structural-support selection allocates the resulting experience, while parent-normalized supervised fine-tuning preserves the semantic interaction as the unit of training weight. This reframes trajectory generation as the deliberate construction of learnable interaction experience, rather than the collection of successful trajectories. Across five Qwen3 scales, Trajectory2Gym improves Tau1, Tau2, and BFCL at every tested scale, increasing the equal-weight three-benchmark mean by 16.9-29.8 percentage points. The gains are strongest in stateful and multi-turn interaction, where later decisions depend on earlier observations and feedback. Our results suggest that scaling tool-use agents depends not only on collecting more successful trajectories, but on constructing interactions whose dependencies are represented and whose decisions are learnable from the agent's visible history.", "upvotes": 51, "discussionId": "6ae00a70a5efa215400a790b", "projectPage": "https://yihaohu0118.github.io/Trajectory2Gym-page/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/yihaohu0118/Trajectory2Gym", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "Trajectory2Gym improves tool-use training by constructing interactions with compositional coverage and decision grounding rather than collecting successful trajectories.", "ai_keywords": ["compositional coverage", "decision grounding", "Trajectory2Gym", "structural-support selection", "parent-normalized supervised fine-tuning", "multi-turn interaction", "tool-use agents"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 2, "organization": {"_id": "68d4ae9941614425f8aa7490", "name": "ant-intl", "fullname": "ant-international", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68e9c86d2551cb38000fa77c/qG2KcieHqYFuWXi2-weuP.jpeg"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u6210\u529f\u7684\u5de5\u5177\u4f7f\u7528\u8f68\u8ff9\u5e76\u4e0d\u603b\u662f\u80fd\u63d0\u4f9b\u6709\u6548\u7684\u76d1\u7763\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6709\u6548\u7684\u8f68\u8ff9\u76d1\u7763\u9700\u8981\u6ee1\u8db3\u4e24\u4e2a\u8981\u6c42\uff1a\u89c2\u5bdf-\u52a8\u4f5c\u4f9d\u8d56\u7684\u7ec4\u5408\u8986\u76d6\u548c\u51b3\u7b56\u7684\u57fa\u7840\u652f\u6301\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86Trajectory2Gym\uff0c\u6784\u5efa\u53ef\u6267\u884c\u7684\u4ea4\u4e92\u5e76\u4ec5\u57fa\u4e8e\u53ef\u89c1\u7684\u51b3\u7b56\u5386\u53f2\u63d0\u4f9b\u76d1\u7763\u3002</li>\n    <li>Trajectory2Gym\u5728\u4e94\u4e2aQwen3\u89c4\u6a21\u4e0a\u63d0\u9ad8\u4e86Tau1\u3001Tau2\u548cBFCL\u7684\u8868\u73b0\uff0c\u589e\u5e45\u4e3a16.9-29.8\u4e2a\u767e\u5206\u70b9\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u7684\u7814\u7a76\u8868\u660e\uff0c\u63d0\u5347\u5de5\u5177\u4f7f\u7528\u4ee3\u7406\u7684\u80fd\u529b\u4e0d\u4ec5\u9700\u8981\u6536\u96c6\u6210\u529f\u8f68\u8ff9\uff0c\u8fd8\u9700\u8981\u6784\u5efa\u5177\u6709\u53ef\u5b66\u4e60\u4f9d\u8d56\u5173\u7cfb\u7684\u4ea4\u4e92\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Successful tool use doesn't always provide good guidance for learning.</li>\n    <li>Effective supervision needs to cover important observation-action links and ensure that actions are based on visible information.</li>\n    <li>Trajectory2Gym helps create better learning experiences by focusing on interactions that highlight these important connections.</li>\n    <li>This method shows significant improvements in performance across different test scales, especially in situations where past actions influence future decisions.</li>\n    <li>The study suggests that improving tool-use agents requires building interactions that clearly show dependencies and are based on what the agent can see and learn from.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-23T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Trajectory2Gym: Decision-Grounded Compositional Interaction Synthesis for Tool-Use Agents", "summary": "Successful tool-use trajectories are not necessarily useful supervision. Even successful interactions may underrepresent multi-step dependencies or contain actions that are unjustified by the information visible to the agent at decision time. We identify two coupled requirements for effective trajectory supervision: compositional coverage, so that important observation-action dependencies are represented, and decision grounding, so that each supervised action is supported by the learner-visible interaction history. We introduce Trajectory2Gym, which constructs executable interactions around under-supported compositional structures and derives supervision only from grounded decisions in their realized executions. Structural-support selection allocates the resulting experience, while parent-normalized supervised fine-tuning preserves the semantic interaction as the unit of training weight. This reframes trajectory generation as the deliberate construction of learnable interaction experience, rather than the collection of successful trajectories. Across five Qwen3 scales, Trajectory2Gym improves Tau1, Tau2, and BFCL at every tested scale, increasing the equal-weight three-benchmark mean by 16.9-29.8 percentage points. The gains are strongest in stateful and multi-turn interaction, where later decisions depend on earlier observations and feedback. Our results suggest that scaling tool-use agents depends not only on collecting more successful trajectories, but on constructing interactions whose dependencies are represented and whose decisions are learnable from the agent's visible history.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.33568.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "67e40cea6d0207b1fa43c826", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/_OzAJCjFC49dEkHZm4AQb.png", "fullname": "Yihao Hu", "name": "HU22333", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "68d4ae9941614425f8aa7490", "name": "ant-intl", "fullname": "ant-international", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68e9c86d2551cb38000fa77c/qG2KcieHqYFuWXi2-weuP.jpeg"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.34662", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7919", "name": "Chengzhi Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a791a", "name": "Yuxiang Lai", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a791b", "name": "Yuzhe Yang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a791c", "name": "Yanqing Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a791d", "name": "Jiheng Hou", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a791e", "name": "Yue Cao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a791f", "name": "Haonan Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7920", "name": "Ruixi Fan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7921", "name": "Lan Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7922", "name": "Sheng Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7923", "name": "Songyou Peng", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7924", "name": "Yale Song", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7925", "name": "Kevin Qinghong Lin", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7926", "name": "Xin Eric Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7927", "name": "Jui-Hsin Lai", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7928", "name": "Youbao Tang", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65e71f6bcd3df9b0f6b2678b/rDRpHvKCWXNBtytJY3XjT.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "StreamVibe: Proactive Video Agents for Evolving User Intent", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "65e71f6bcd3df9b0f6b2678b", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65e71f6bcd3df9b0f6b2678b/8mdex6eI80TGezot_GGI3.jpeg", "isPro": true, "fullname": "Chengzhi Liu", "user": "LCZZZZ", "type": "user", "name": "LCZZZZ"}, "summary": "Vision-language models are shifting from offline passive understanding toward online proactive interaction. However, prevailing methods largely rely on explicit requests while miss the implicit user intent (e.g., \"where should I click next?\" while a user navigate a website), meanwhile fail to update the state simultaneously as user requests might update continuously. These limitations make them less suited to intent-aware interaction in practical. To address these challenges, we introduce StreamVibe-Bench, a new benchmark serving as testbed for user intent interaction, with 4.1K evaluation samples and 85K training samples spanning explicit, implicit, and dynamic intents. Building on this benchmark, we develop StreamVibe-VL, a streaming proactive model that infers implicit user intents from continuous observations and dynamically tailors its interactions as these intents evolve. We further introduce StreamVibe-Harness, which maintains user intents as persistent states with a fleixble lifecycles via a hierarchical memory to track the latest user intent and environmental state. Our experiments reveal three key findings: (i) StreamVibe-VL preserves strong general video understanding while improving response timing and answer quality by 10.7% over other baselines; (ii) existing proactive models remain limited in understanding user intent and deciding when to respond, especially under implicit and dynamic intents; and (iii) StreamVibe-Harness further improves response consistency and adaptation over sustained interaction, resulting in a 12.3% gain in proactive interaction.", "upvotes": 50, "discussionId": "6ae00e8ba5efa215400a7929", "ai_summary": "StreamVibe introduces a benchmark, streaming vision-language model, and hierarchical memory harness to enable proactive, intent-aware interaction with implicit and evolving user goals.", "ai_keywords": ["vision-language models", "implicit user intent", "dynamic intent", "StreamVibe-Bench", "StreamVibe-VL", "streaming proactive model", "StreamVibe-Harness", "hierarchical memory", "proactive interaction"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "5e6aca39878b8b2bf9806447", "name": "google", "fullname": "Google", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/5dd96eb166059660ed1ee413/WtA3YYitedOr9n02eHfJe.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u89c6\u89c9\u8bed\u8a00\u6a21\u578b\u6b63\u5728\u4ece\u88ab\u52a8\u7406\u89e3\u8f6c\u5411\u4e3b\u52a8\u4ea4\u4e92\uff0c\u4f46\u73b0\u6709\u65b9\u6cd5\u4e3b\u8981\u4f9d\u8d56\u660e\u786e\u8bf7\u6c42\uff0c\u5ffd\u89c6\u4e86\u9690\u542b\u7528\u6237\u610f\u56fe\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4e3a\u4e86\u89e3\u51b3\u8fd9\u4e9b\u95ee\u9898\uff0c\u7814\u7a76\u56e2\u961f\u63a8\u51fa\u4e86StreamVibe-Bench\uff0c\u8fd9\u662f\u4e00\u79cd\u7528\u4e8e\u7528\u6237\u610f\u56fe\u4ea4\u4e92\u7684\u65b0\u57fa\u51c6\uff0c\u5305\u542b4100\u4e2a\u8bc4\u4f30\u6837\u672c\u548c85000\u4e2a\u8bad\u7ec3\u6837\u672c\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u57fa\u4e8e\u8be5\u57fa\u51c6\uff0c\u5f00\u53d1\u4e86StreamVibe-VL\u6a21\u578b\uff0c\u80fd\u591f\u4ece\u8fde\u7eed\u89c2\u5bdf\u4e2d\u63a8\u65ad\u9690\u542b\u7528\u6237\u610f\u56fe\uff0c\u5e76\u6839\u636e\u8fd9\u4e9b\u610f\u56fe\u7684\u53d8\u5316\u52a8\u6001\u8c03\u6574\u4ea4\u4e92\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5f15\u5165\u4e86StreamVibe-Harness\uff0c\u901a\u8fc7\u5c42\u6b21\u8bb0\u5fc6\u7cfb\u7edf\u7ef4\u62a4\u7528\u6237\u610f\u56fe\u7684\u6301\u4e45\u72b6\u6001\uff0c\u8ddf\u8e2a\u6700\u65b0\u7684\u7528\u6237\u610f\u56fe\u548c\u73af\u5883\u72b6\u6001\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u7ed3\u679c\u663e\u793a\uff0cStreamVibe-VL\u5728\u89c6\u9891\u7406\u89e3\u3001\u54cd\u5e94\u65f6\u673a\u548c\u7b54\u6848\u8d28\u91cf\u65b9\u9762\u5747\u6709\u663e\u8457\u63d0\u5347\uff0c\u540c\u65f6StreamVibe-Harness\u63d0\u9ad8\u4e86\u54cd\u5e94\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\u548c\u9002\u5e94\u6027\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Vision-language models are evolving to interact with users actively rather than just passively understanding their requests.</li>\n    <li>Current models struggle to recognize users' implicit intents and fail to update in real-time as user needs change.</li>\n    <li>StreamVibe-Bench is introduced as a new benchmark with thousands of samples to test user intent interactions.</li>\n    <li>StreamVibe-VL is a new model that detects implicit user intents and adjusts its responses as these intents change.</li>\n    <li>StreamVibe-Harness improves how user intents are tracked over time, leading to better response consistency and a significant increase in proactive interactions.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "StreamVibe: Proactive Video Agents for Evolving User Intent", "summary": "Vision-language models are shifting from offline passive understanding toward online proactive interaction. However, prevailing methods largely rely on explicit requests while miss the implicit user intent (e.g., \"where should I click next?\" while a user navigate a website), meanwhile fail to update the state simultaneously as user requests might update continuously. These limitations make them less suited to intent-aware interaction in practical. To address these challenges, we introduce StreamVibe-Bench, a new benchmark serving as testbed for user intent interaction, with 4.1K evaluation samples and 85K training samples spanning explicit, implicit, and dynamic intents. Building on this benchmark, we develop StreamVibe-VL, a streaming proactive model that infers implicit user intents from continuous observations and dynamically tailors its interactions as these intents evolve. We further introduce StreamVibe-Harness, which maintains user intents as persistent states with a fleixble lifecycles via a hierarchical memory to track the latest user intent and environmental state. Our experiments reveal three key findings: (i) StreamVibe-VL preserves strong general video understanding while improving response timing and answer quality by 10.7% over other baselines; (ii) existing proactive models remain limited in understanding user intent and deciding when to respond, especially under implicit and dynamic intents; and (iii) StreamVibe-Harness further improves response consistency and adaptation over sustained interaction, resulting in a 12.3% gain in proactive interaction.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65e71f6bcd3df9b0f6b2678b/rDRpHvKCWXNBtytJY3XjT.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.34662.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "65e71f6bcd3df9b0f6b2678b", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65e71f6bcd3df9b0f6b2678b/8mdex6eI80TGezot_GGI3.jpeg", "fullname": "Chengzhi Liu", "name": "LCZZZZ", "type": "user", "isPro": true, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 3, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "5e6aca39878b8b2bf9806447", "name": "google", "fullname": "Google", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/5dd96eb166059660ed1ee413/WtA3YYitedOr9n02eHfJe.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.36729", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907149", "name": "Qiaoling Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714a", "name": "Jiaxiang Cheng", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714b", "name": "Xueqing Deng", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714c", "name": "Tiankai Hang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714d", "name": "Ji Hou", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714e", "name": "Jiaqi Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290714f", "name": "Yifan Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907150", "name": "Tao Lu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907151", "name": "Junshu Tang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907152", "name": "Yu Tang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907153", "name": "Yao Teng", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907154", "name": "Yu Tian", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907155", "name": "Heng Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907156", "name": "Weizhi Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907157", "name": "Xiyao Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907158", "name": "Xilun Wu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907159", "name": "Mingyu Yang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715a", "name": "Yangbin Yu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715b", "name": "Rui Zhao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715c", "name": "Longjun Cao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715d", "name": "Linghao Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715e", "name": "Haoyi Duan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290715f", "name": "Jiaming Han", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907160", "name": "Mingji Han", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907161", "name": "Enhua Jiang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907162", "name": "Shunkai Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907163", "name": "Yanzhe Liang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907164", "name": "Qinwei Ma", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907165", "name": "Cong Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907166", "name": "Lei Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907167", "name": "Bing Wu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907168", "name": "Ke Xing", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c2907169", "name": "Ray Ying", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716a", "name": "Zhentao Yu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716b", "name": "Key Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716c", "name": "Yuan Zhou", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716d", "name": "Zhengguang Zhou", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716e", "name": "Kai Zou", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/63d34004b734eaa4d4faeccf/lOBvI5ytl4xxTPiMTwOCy.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Hy-Nomas: Towards Free-form Interactive World Generation", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "63d34004b734eaa4d4faeccf", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/7431a0177ac618bfcdffbf436e689acc.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Weizhi Wang", "user": "weizhiwang", "type": "user", "name": "weizhiwang"}, "summary": "Interactive world generation requires models to follow successive commands while preserving relevant history and action consequences. We present Hy-Nomas, a video world model with an 8B diffusion backbone that supports free-form text instructions and continuous camera control across multiple interaction turns. It generates variable-length segments conditioned on current inputs and cached history. Our central design aligns structured video captions with action-aware causal attention: descriptive context remains accessible across segments, while action instructions are restricted to their intended temporal windows. A vision-language Prompt Enhancer (PE) translates user requests and multimodal interaction histories into structured conditions for the next segment. A multi-stage curriculum progressively introduces causal generation, text interaction, camera control, and training on model-generated histories, followed by diffusion distillation. In human evaluation, Hy-Nomas shows superior performance over existing world models. We release model weights, code and benchmarks, together with a lightweight adaptation workflow, to support reproducible research on interactive world generation.", "upvotes": 49, "discussionId": "6ae15718a5eb0647c290716f", "projectPage": "https://hy-nomas.hy.tencent.com/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/Tencent-Hunyuan/Hy-Nomas", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "Hy-Nomas is a video world model that uses causal attention and structured captions to follow multi-turn text and camera instructions while preserving interaction history.", "ai_keywords": ["video world model", "diffusion backbone", "action-aware causal attention", "vision-language Prompt Enhancer", "multi-stage curriculum", "diffusion distillation", "interactive world generation"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 274, "organization": {"_id": "6645f953c39288df638dbdd5", "name": "Tencent-Hunyuan", "fullname": "Tencent Hunyuan", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/62d22496c58f969c152bcefd/woKSjt2wXvBNKussyYPsa.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>Hy-Nomas \u662f\u4e00\u79cd\u89c6\u9891\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\uff0c\u80fd\u591f\u5904\u7406\u8fde\u7eed\u7684\u6307\u4ee4\u548c\u76f8\u673a\u63a7\u5236\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8be5\u6a21\u578b\u57fa\u4e8e 8B \u6269\u6563\u9aa8\u5e72\u7f51\u7edc\uff0c\u751f\u6210\u4e0e\u5f53\u524d\u8f93\u5165\u548c\u5386\u53f2\u8bb0\u5f55\u76f8\u5173\u7684\u53ef\u53d8\u957f\u5ea6\u6bb5\u843d\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8bbe\u8ba1\u4e2d\u7ed3\u5408\u4e86\u7ed3\u6784\u5316\u89c6\u9891\u5b57\u5e55\u548c\u5177\u5907\u52a8\u4f5c\u611f\u77e5\u7684\u56e0\u679c\u6ce8\u610f\u529b\uff0c\u786e\u4fdd\u63cf\u8ff0\u6027\u5185\u5bb9\u5728\u5404\u6bb5\u843d\u95f4\u53ef\u8bbf\u95ee\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5f15\u5165\u4e86\u89c6\u89c9-\u8bed\u8a00\u63d0\u793a\u589e\u5f3a\u5668\uff0c\u5c06\u7528\u6237\u8bf7\u6c42\u8f6c\u5316\u4e3a\u4e0b\u4e00\u4e2a\u6bb5\u843d\u7684\u7ed3\u6784\u5316\u6761\u4ef6\u3002</li>\n    <li>Hy-Nomas \u5728\u4eba\u7c7b\u8bc4\u4f30\u4e2d\u8868\u73b0\u4f18\u4e8e\u73b0\u6709\u7684\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\uff0c\u5e76\u53d1\u5e03\u4e86\u6a21\u578b\u6743\u91cd\u548c\u4ee3\u7801\u4ee5\u652f\u6301\u53ef\u91cd\u590d\u7684\u7814\u7a76\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Hy-Nomas is a new model for creating interactive video worlds that can follow commands and remember past actions.</li>\n    <li>It uses an advanced 8 billion parameter diffusion model that allows for free-form text instructions and camera control.</li>\n    <li>The model generates video segments based on current inputs and past interactions, ensuring relevant information is kept accessible.</li>\n    <li>A special tool called Prompt Enhancer converts user requests into structured instructions for generating the next video segment.</li>\n    <li>Hy-Nomas outperforms other existing models in human evaluations and offers tools and resources for further research in this area.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Hy-Nomas: Towards Free-form Interactive World Generation", "summary": "Interactive world generation requires models to follow successive commands while preserving relevant history and action consequences. We present Hy-Nomas, a video world model with an 8B diffusion backbone that supports free-form text instructions and continuous camera control across multiple interaction turns. It generates variable-length segments conditioned on current inputs and cached history. Our central design aligns structured video captions with action-aware causal attention: descriptive context remains accessible across segments, while action instructions are restricted to their intended temporal windows. A vision-language Prompt Enhancer (PE) translates user requests and multimodal interaction histories into structured conditions for the next segment. A multi-stage curriculum progressively introduces causal generation, text interaction, camera control, and training on model-generated histories, followed by diffusion distillation. In human evaluation, Hy-Nomas shows superior performance over existing world models. We release model weights, code and benchmarks, together with a lightweight adaptation workflow, to support reproducible research on interactive world generation.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/63d34004b734eaa4d4faeccf/lOBvI5ytl4xxTPiMTwOCy.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.36729.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "63d34004b734eaa4d4faeccf", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/7431a0177ac618bfcdffbf436e689acc.svg", "fullname": "Weizhi Wang", "name": "weizhiwang", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 23, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6645f953c39288df638dbdd5", "name": "Tencent-Hunyuan", "fullname": "Tencent Hunyuan", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/62d22496c58f969c152bcefd/woKSjt2wXvBNKussyYPsa.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.36068", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070f8", "name": "Jiayi Luo", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070f9", "name": "Yang Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070fa", "name": "Liliang Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070fb", "name": "Nan Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070fc", "name": "Chen Gao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070fd", "name": "Boxiang Qiu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070fe", "name": "Zhuoxuan Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c29070ff", "name": "Baorui Peng", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907100", "name": "Chuankang Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907101", "name": "Yuxiang Yan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907102", "name": "Si Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907103", "name": "Qingyun Sun", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907104", "name": "Shuicheng Yan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907105", "name": "Maoqing Yao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907106", "name": "Jianxin Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae1517da5eb0647c2907107", "name": "Guanghui Ren", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Rollback: Experience Backtracking in World Models for Policy Improvement", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "66c4816e96583c59b09fec30", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/66c4816e96583c59b09fec30/RLurCsmcgOfyuWVkpsv1L.jpeg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Ryan Chen", "user": "ryancll118", "type": "user", "name": "ryancll118"}, "summary": "Action-conditioned world models offer a scalable alternative to costly real-world interaction for improving robot policies. However, existing world-model-based methods largely treat world models as forward-only surrogates of the physical environment, leaving untapped a capability no physical environment affords: rewinding to past decision points to explore counterfactual futures. We introduce Rollback, a policy improvement paradigm that turns world models from passive simulators into counterfactual engines: it rolls back to critical decision points, branches into alternative actions, and contrasts their outcomes to attribute success to individual decisions. This concentrates imagination where it matters most and distills counterfactual outcomes into fine-grained, action-level supervision without a separately learned value model. We build a scalable training system that unifies distributed world-model search with asynchronous real-robot execution, forming a self-reinforcing cycle: real-world experience continually sharpens the world model, and a sharper world model in turn drives further policy improvement. We term the world-model side of this cycle test-time physics grounding, a capability that hand-built physics simulators lack: while the world model serves as the training environment, real transitions from the policy it trains correct its dynamics through a lightweight LoRA pipeline. Experiments on three challenging real-world tasks show that Rollback improves success rates over the strongest baseline by an absolute 10.67%, 11.11%, and 11.85% on block extraction, ball tossing, and tabletop curling, respectively, while achieving more stable improvement throughout training. Although initialized from teleoperated demonstrations far below human level, Rollback surpasses the average success rate of human players on both ball tossing and tabletop curling within 8 hours of training.", "upvotes": 41, "discussionId": "6ae1517ea5eb0647c2907108", "projectPage": "https://rollback-wm.github.io/", "ai_summary": "Rollback improves robot policies by using world models to rewind to past decisions, explore counterfactual actions, and generate fine-grained supervision, while continuously refining dynamics via real-world feedback.", "ai_keywords": ["action-conditioned world models", "counterfactual futures", "Rollback", "world-model search", "test-time physics grounding", "LoRA", "distributed search", "asynchronous execution"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "676fc7c31c48eff17fac3135", "name": "agibot-world", "fullname": "AgiBot World", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64e57309b78bc92221ce3b70/ewI1QvFVMDgSsShQeSvlX.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u884c\u52a8\u6761\u4ef6\u7684\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\u4e3a\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u653f\u7b56\u7684\u6539\u8fdb\u63d0\u4f9b\u4e86\u4e00\u79cd\u53ef\u6269\u5c55\u7684\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u51cf\u5c11\u4e86\u4e0e\u771f\u5b9e\u73af\u5883\u4e92\u52a8\u7684\u6210\u672c\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u73b0\u6709\u7684\u65b9\u6cd5\u4e3b\u8981\u5c06\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\u89c6\u4e3a\u7269\u7406\u73af\u5883\u7684\u524d\u5411\u6a21\u62df\u5668\uff0c\u672a\u80fd\u5229\u7528\u201c\u56de\u6eda\u201d\u5230\u8fc7\u53bb\u51b3\u7b56\u70b9\u7684\u80fd\u529b\u3002</li>\n    <li>Rollback\u662f\u4e00\u79cd\u65b0\u578b\u7684\u653f\u7b56\u6539\u8fdb\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u53ef\u4ee5\u5728\u5173\u952e\u51b3\u7b56\u70b9\u56de\u6eda\uff0c\u63a2\u7d22\u4e0d\u540c\u7684\u884c\u52a8\u53ca\u5176\u7ed3\u679c\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u901a\u8fc7\u5c06\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\u4e0e\u771f\u5b9e\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u6267\u884c\u76f8\u7ed3\u5408\uff0c\u5f62\u6210\u4e00\u4e2a\u81ea\u6211\u5f3a\u5316\u7684\u8bad\u7ec3\u5faa\u73af\uff0c\u6301\u7eed\u4f18\u5316\u6a21\u578b\u548c\u653f\u7b56\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u7ed3\u679c\u8868\u660e\uff0cRollback\u5728\u591a\u9879\u771f\u5b9e\u4e16\u754c\u4efb\u52a1\u4e2d\u663e\u8457\u63d0\u9ad8\u4e86\u6210\u529f\u7387\uff0c\u4e14\u8bad\u7ec3\u8fc7\u7a0b\u66f4\u7a33\u5b9a\uff0c\u751a\u81f3\u8d85\u8d8a\u4e86\u4eba\u7c7b\u73a9\u5bb6\u7684\u5e73\u5747\u6210\u529f\u7387\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Action-conditioned world models help improve robot policies without needing expensive real-world interactions.</li>\n    <li>The new approach, called Rollback, allows robots to rewind to past decisions and explore different actions to see their effects.</li>\n    <li>Rollback focuses on important decisions to improve learning without needing a separate value model.</li>\n    <li>This method combines virtual world training with real-world robot actions, creating a cycle that enhances both the model and the robot's performance.</li>\n    <li>In tests, Rollback showed significant improvements in success rates for three tasks, even outperforming human players after just a few hours of training.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Rollback: Experience Backtracking in World Models for Policy Improvement", "summary": "Action-conditioned world models offer a scalable alternative to costly real-world interaction for improving robot policies. However, existing world-model-based methods largely treat world models as forward-only surrogates of the physical environment, leaving untapped a capability no physical environment affords: rewinding to past decision points to explore counterfactual futures. We introduce Rollback, a policy improvement paradigm that turns world models from passive simulators into counterfactual engines: it rolls back to critical decision points, branches into alternative actions, and contrasts their outcomes to attribute success to individual decisions. This concentrates imagination where it matters most and distills counterfactual outcomes into fine-grained, action-level supervision without a separately learned value model. We build a scalable training system that unifies distributed world-model search with asynchronous real-robot execution, forming a self-reinforcing cycle: real-world experience continually sharpens the world model, and a sharper world model in turn drives further policy improvement. We term the world-model side of this cycle test-time physics grounding, a capability that hand-built physics simulators lack: while the world model serves as the training environment, real transitions from the policy it trains correct its dynamics through a lightweight LoRA pipeline. Experiments on three challenging real-world tasks show that Rollback improves success rates over the strongest baseline by an absolute 10.67%, 11.11%, and 11.85% on block extraction, ball tossing, and tabletop curling, respectively, while achieving more stable improvement throughout training. Although initialized from teleoperated demonstrations far below human level, Rollback surpasses the average success rate of human players on both ball tossing and tabletop curling within 8 hours of training.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.36068.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "66c4816e96583c59b09fec30", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/66c4816e96583c59b09fec30/RLurCsmcgOfyuWVkpsv1L.jpeg", "fullname": "Ryan Chen", "name": "ryancll118", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 4, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "676fc7c31c48eff17fac3135", "name": "agibot-world", "fullname": "AgiBot World", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64e57309b78bc92221ce3b70/ewI1QvFVMDgSsShQeSvlX.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.33135", "authors": [{"_id": "6adffd62a5efa215400a7879", "name": "Lei Cao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6adffd62a5efa215400a787a", "name": "Yu Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6adffd62a5efa215400a787b", "name": "Wenhua Liao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6adffd62a5efa215400a787c", "name": "Peng Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6adffd62a5efa215400a787d", "name": "Fei Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6adffd62a5efa215400a787e", "name": "Hao Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6adffd62a5efa215400a787f", "name": "Jiajun Le", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6adffd62a5efa215400a7880", "name": "Daiguo Zhou", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6adffd62a5efa215400a7881", "name": "Jian Luan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6adffd62a5efa215400a7882", "name": "Jiayi Ma", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-23T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "WorldCast: World-Consistent Character Video Generation with Large-Angle Camera Control", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "65eaeda026fee9c11b52b3ca", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/8a701682ba6ff829af95425cf4c18e35.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "zhangpeng25", "user": "peng25", "type": "user", "name": "peng25"}, "summary": "Precise camera control is essential for cinematic character video generation, yet existing methods struggle to follow large-angle trajectories with combined rotation, translation, and radius variation from arbitrary initial azimuths. Such viewpoint changes also reveal character regions unseen in the initial frame, causing geometric distortions, appearance degradation, and identity drift. To systematically study these coupled challenges, we introduce CLACT, a large-scale Character-centric dataset and benchmark covering diverse Large-Angle Camera Trajectories, with accurate camera geometry and multi-view character observations for training and evaluation. We further propose WorldCast, a framework for world-consistent character video generation under diverse large-angle camera motions. Specifically, we back-project the initial frame into a 3D point cloud using estimated depth, align the point-cloud scale with the target camera trajectory, and render it along the trajectory to obtain explicit geometric guidance and coverage masks. To complement the incomplete character information, we introduce Geometry-Aligned Memory Attention (GAMA). Using relative camera poses estimated jointly from the initial frame and multi-view character anchors, we express anchor and target-view geometry in a common coordinate frame. GAMA uses this geometric alignment to guide the retrieval and injection of view-relevant character features during generation. Evaluations across diverse large-angle camera trajectories demonstrate accurate trajectory following while preserving character geometry, appearance, and identity.", "upvotes": 38, "discussionId": "6adffd62a5efa215400a7883", "projectPage": "https://pixctrol.github.io/WorldCast/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/PixCtrol/WorldCast", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "CLACT and WorldCast enable consistent character video generation under large-angle camera motions by combining 3D point-cloud guidance with geometry-aligned memory attention.", "ai_keywords": ["CLACT", "WorldCast", "3D point cloud", "depth estimation", "camera trajectory", "geometry-aligned memory attention", "GAMA", "multi-view character anchors", "relative camera poses"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 17, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u7cbe\u786e\u7684\u6444\u50cf\u673a\u63a7\u5236\u5bf9\u4e8e\u751f\u6210\u89d2\u8272\u89c6\u9891\u975e\u5e38\u91cd\u8981\uff0c\u4f46\u73b0\u6709\u65b9\u6cd5\u5728\u5904\u7406\u5927\u89d2\u5ea6\u8f68\u8ff9\u65f6\u8868\u73b0\u4e0d\u4f73\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8fd9\u4e9b\u89c6\u89d2\u53d8\u5316\u53ef\u80fd\u5bfc\u81f4\u51e0\u4f55\u5931\u771f\u3001\u5916\u89c2\u9000\u5316\u548c\u8eab\u4efd\u6f02\u79fb\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u5f15\u5165\u4e86CLACT\u6570\u636e\u96c6\uff0c\u6db5\u76d6\u591a\u6837\u5316\u7684\u5927\u89d2\u5ea6\u6444\u50cf\u673a\u8f68\u8ff9\uff0c\u4ee5\u4fbf\u8fdb\u884c\u8bad\u7ec3\u548c\u8bc4\u4f30\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86WorldCast\u6846\u67b6\uff0c\u7528\u4e8e\u5728\u5404\u79cd\u5927\u89d2\u5ea6\u6444\u50cf\u673a\u8fd0\u52a8\u4e0b\u751f\u6210\u4e00\u81f4\u7684\u89d2\u8272\u89c6\u9891\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u901a\u8fc7\u51e0\u4f55\u5bf9\u9f50\u8bb0\u5fc6\u6ce8\u610f\u529b\uff08GAMA\uff09\uff0c\u6211\u4eec\u5728\u751f\u6210\u8fc7\u7a0b\u4e2d\u5f15\u5bfc\u68c0\u7d22\u4e0e\u89c6\u89d2\u76f8\u5173\u7684\u89d2\u8272\u7279\u5f81\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Precise camera control is important for creating cinematic character videos, but existing methods have issues with large-angle movements.</li>\n    <li>These camera movements can reveal new parts of the character, leading to distortions and changes in appearance.</li>\n    <li>CLACT is a new dataset and benchmark designed to study large-angle camera movements with accurate camera and character observations.</li>\n    <li>WorldCast is a framework that helps generate consistent character videos by using 3D point clouds and aligning them with camera movements.</li>\n    <li>Geometry-Aligned Memory Attention (GAMA) helps maintain character features and identity by aligning different camera views during video generation.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-22T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "WorldCast: World-Consistent Character Video Generation with Large-Angle Camera Control", "summary": "Precise camera control is essential for cinematic character video generation, yet existing methods struggle to follow large-angle trajectories with combined rotation, translation, and radius variation from arbitrary initial azimuths. Such viewpoint changes also reveal character regions unseen in the initial frame, causing geometric distortions, appearance degradation, and identity drift. To systematically study these coupled challenges, we introduce CLACT, a large-scale Character-centric dataset and benchmark covering diverse Large-Angle Camera Trajectories, with accurate camera geometry and multi-view character observations for training and evaluation. We further propose WorldCast, a framework for world-consistent character video generation under diverse large-angle camera motions. Specifically, we back-project the initial frame into a 3D point cloud using estimated depth, align the point-cloud scale with the target camera trajectory, and render it along the trajectory to obtain explicit geometric guidance and coverage masks. To complement the incomplete character information, we introduce Geometry-Aligned Memory Attention (GAMA). Using relative camera poses estimated jointly from the initial frame and multi-view character anchors, we express anchor and target-view geometry in a common coordinate frame. GAMA uses this geometric alignment to guide the retrieval and injection of view-relevant character features during generation. Evaluations across diverse large-angle camera trajectories demonstrate accurate trajectory following while preserving character geometry, appearance, and identity.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.33135.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "65eaeda026fee9c11b52b3ca", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/8a701682ba6ff829af95425cf4c18e35.svg", "fullname": "zhangpeng25", "name": "peng25", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "isUserFollowing": false}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.37666", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907233", "name": "Chaehyun Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907234", "name": "Chi-Pin Huang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907235", "name": "Jisoo Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907236", "name": "Min-Hung Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907237", "name": "Yu-Chiang Frank Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907238", "name": "Seungryong Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15d93a5eb0647c2907239", "name": "Fu-En Yang", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/63f727b1bd28622c9b951114/upl0lRpybvBMpqUSl4LGv.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "ThinkWorld: World Imagination Reinforces Chain-of-Dynamics Reasoning", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "63f727b1bd28622c9b951114", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/0b52ae1ff473615fdd665cdbc91f6db9.svg", "isPro": true, "fullname": "Chaehyun Kim", "user": "chyun", "type": "user", "name": "chyun"}, "summary": "Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have made substantial progress in robotic manipulation, but their generalization remains constrained by the diversity and coverage of action-labeled robot demonstrations. Human videos provide much broader behavioral and environmental experience, while learning from them faces a fundamental trade-off between scalable data and explicit action grounding. We introduce ThinkWorld, a VLA learning framework that grounds action reasoning in world dynamics and strengthens it through reinforcement learning with world imagination. ThinkWorld represents each action thought as a Chain-of-Dynamics (CoD), a sequence of discrete motion tokens that captures the intermediate dynamics connecting the current world to a future state and serves as a common action representation across embodiments. We first initialize CoD with action-labeled demonstrations, then predict the future induced by each CoD and reinforce those whose predictions better match the observed outcome. This enables videos without action annotations to provide direct supervision from observed state transitions, allowing human and robot experience to refine the same reasoning space. Experiments in simulation and on real robots demonstrate effective human-to-robot transfer and strong generalization to unseen tasks and environments.", "upvotes": 36, "discussionId": "6ae15d94a5eb0647c290723a", "projectPage": "https://thinkworld-project.github.io/", "ai_summary": "ThinkWorld improves robotic generalization by representing actions as dynamics chains and using world imagination to learn from unlabeled human videos.", "ai_keywords": ["Vision-Language-Action models", "Chain-of-Dynamics", "motion tokens", "world dynamics", "reinforcement learning", "world imagination"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "60262b67268c201cdc8b7d43", "name": "nvidia", "fullname": "NVIDIA", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65df9200dc3292a8983e5017/Vs5FPVCH-VZBipV3qKTuy.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>Vision-Language-Action (VLA) \u6a21\u578b\u5728\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u64cd\u4f5c\u4e2d\u53d6\u5f97\u4e86\u663e\u8457\u8fdb\u5c55\uff0c\u4f46\u5176\u6cdb\u5316\u80fd\u529b\u53d7\u5230\u52a8\u4f5c\u6807\u8bb0\u793a\u8303\u591a\u6837\u6027\u548c\u8986\u76d6\u8303\u56f4\u7684\u9650\u5236\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4eba\u7c7b\u89c6\u9891\u63d0\u4f9b\u4e86\u66f4\u5e7f\u6cdb\u7684\u884c\u4e3a\u548c\u73af\u5883\u7ecf\u9a8c\uff0c\u4f46\u5728\u5b66\u4e60\u65f6\u9762\u4e34\u6570\u636e\u89c4\u6a21\u4e0e\u660e\u786e\u52a8\u4f5c\u57fa\u7840\u4e4b\u95f4\u7684\u6743\u8861\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86 ThinkWorld\uff0c\u4e00\u79cd\u5c06\u52a8\u4f5c\u63a8\u7406\u4e0e\u4e16\u754c\u52a8\u6001\u76f8\u7ed3\u5408\u7684 VLA \u5b66\u4e60\u6846\u67b6\uff0c\u5e76\u901a\u8fc7\u5f3a\u5316\u5b66\u4e60\u4e0e\u4e16\u754c\u60f3\u8c61\u5f3a\u5316\u8be5\u63a8\u7406\u3002</li>\n    <li>ThinkWorld \u4f7f\u7528\u540d\u4e3a Chain-of-Dynamics (CoD) \u7684\u5e8f\u5217\u8868\u793a\u6bcf\u4e2a\u52a8\u4f5c\u601d\u7ef4\uff0c\u6355\u6349\u5f53\u524d\u4e16\u754c\u4e0e\u672a\u6765\u72b6\u6001\u4e4b\u95f4\u7684\u4e2d\u95f4\u52a8\u6001\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u8868\u660e\uff0c\u8be5\u65b9\u6cd5\u53ef\u4ee5\u6709\u6548\u5730\u5b9e\u73b0\u4eba\u7c7b\u4e0e\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u4e4b\u95f4\u7684\u77e5\u8bc6\u8f6c\u79fb\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u672a\u89c1\u4efb\u52a1\u548c\u73af\u5883\u4e2d\u8868\u73b0\u51fa\u826f\u597d\u7684\u6cdb\u5316\u80fd\u529b\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models help robots perform tasks, but they struggle to generalize due to limited action-labeled data.</li>\n    <li>Human videos offer a wider variety of experiences, but using them for training is challenging because of differences in data and action understanding.</li>\n    <li>ThinkWorld is a new framework that helps robots learn by connecting actions to the changing world around them.</li>\n    <li>It uses a method called Chain-of-Dynamics (CoD) to represent actions as sequences that show how the world changes from one state to another.</li>\n    <li>Experiments show that this approach improves the ability of robots to learn from human videos and perform new tasks successfully.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "ThinkWorld: World Imagination Reinforces Chain-of-Dynamics Reasoning", "summary": "Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have made substantial progress in robotic manipulation, but their generalization remains constrained by the diversity and coverage of action-labeled robot demonstrations. Human videos provide much broader behavioral and environmental experience, while learning from them faces a fundamental trade-off between scalable data and explicit action grounding. We introduce ThinkWorld, a VLA learning framework that grounds action reasoning in world dynamics and strengthens it through reinforcement learning with world imagination. ThinkWorld represents each action thought as a Chain-of-Dynamics (CoD), a sequence of discrete motion tokens that captures the intermediate dynamics connecting the current world to a future state and serves as a common action representation across embodiments. We first initialize CoD with action-labeled demonstrations, then predict the future induced by each CoD and reinforce those whose predictions better match the observed outcome. This enables videos without action annotations to provide direct supervision from observed state transitions, allowing human and robot experience to refine the same reasoning space. Experiments in simulation and on real robots demonstrate effective human-to-robot transfer and strong generalization to unseen tasks and environments.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/63f727b1bd28622c9b951114/upl0lRpybvBMpqUSl4LGv.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.37666.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "63f727b1bd28622c9b951114", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/0b52ae1ff473615fdd665cdbc91f6db9.svg", "fullname": "Chaehyun Kim", "name": "chyun", "type": "user", "isPro": true, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 2, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "60262b67268c201cdc8b7d43", "name": "nvidia", "fullname": "NVIDIA", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65df9200dc3292a8983e5017/Vs5FPVCH-VZBipV3qKTuy.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.32749", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae29be0d670fc6ea896c79e", "name": "Wendi Sang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae29be0d670fc6ea896c79f", "name": "Kai Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae29be0d670fc6ea896c7a0", "name": "Yifan Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae29be0d670fc6ea896c7a1", "name": "Jianqiang Huang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae29be0d670fc6ea896c7a2", "name": "Xiaolin Hu", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6387676c23da90491eb9fb16/fyfAlyMRka4HqWEFfbAoN.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-23T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Efficient Streaming Audio-Visual Target Speaker Extraction for Real-World Acoustic Scenes", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "6387676c23da90491eb9fb16", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1669818175965-noauth.jpeg", "isPro": true, "fullname": "Kai Li", "user": "JusperLee", "type": "user", "name": "JusperLee"}, "summary": "Streaming audio-visual target speaker extraction (AVTSE) is essential for latency-sensitive applications. However, current causal systems face two coupled gaps. First, standard benchmarks fix the number of speakers at two and ignore the near-/far-field interference, reverberation, and sudden noise of real scenes. Second, high-performance causal architectures are too heavy for edge deployment. Lightweight alternatives try to reclaim capacity by repeatedly invoking a small separator, which offsets the savings. To address the first gap, we release RealSSA, a realistic AVTSE benchmark consisting of RealSSA-Sim and RealSSA-Real. RealSSA-Sim provides controllable simulated scenes with 1--5 dynamic near-field interfering speakers; scenes with far-field interference additionally include 10--15 far-field interfering speakers. RealSSA-Real provides a held-out real-recorded evaluation set. To address the second gap, we propose Falcon, a causal AVTSE method. Its Separator performs multi-scale time-frequency separation in a single encoder--decoder pass. We train Falcon with an encoder-space speaker contrastive loss. This loss suppresses near-field leakage at zero inference cost and transfers across backbones. Falcon achieves state-of-the-art extraction quality on RealSSA-Sim, LRS2-2Mix, LRS3-2Mix, and VoxCeleb2-2Mix. Compared with the causal AV-TFGridNet baseline, it cuts parameters by 91.9%, computation by 20.2 times, and GPU inference latency by 11.1 times. Code and demo are available at https://cslikai.cn/Falcon/.", "upvotes": 32, "discussionId": "6ae29be1d670fc6ea896c7a3", "projectPage": "https://cslikai.cn/Falcon/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/JusperLee/Falcon", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "Falcon is a lightweight causal audio-visual target speaker extraction method that achieves high quality with drastically reduced parameters and latency, supported by the realistic RealSSA benchmark.", "ai_keywords": ["audio-visual target speaker extraction", "causal AVTSE", "RealSSA", "Falcon", "multi-scale time-frequency separation", "encoder-space speaker contrastive loss", "near-field leakage suppression"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 13, "organization": {"_id": "640d3084536d9fe0f005cac3", "name": "thu-ml", "fullname": "Tsinghua Machine Learning Group", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1678587085174-633131798ef21f47308ce49b.jpeg"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u63a8\u51fa\u4e86RealSSA\uff0c\u4e00\u4e2a\u65b0\u7684\u97f3\u89c6\u9891\u76ee\u6807\u8bf4\u8bdd\u4eba\u63d0\u53d6\u57fa\u51c6\uff0c\u5305\u542b\u6a21\u62df\u548c\u771f\u5b9e\u573a\u666f\u3002</li>\n    <li>RealSSA-Sim\u63d0\u4f9b\u4e861-5\u4e2a\u8fd1\u573a\u5e72\u6270\u8bf4\u8bdd\u4eba\u7684\u53ef\u63a7\u6a21\u62df\u573a\u666f\uff0c\u548c10-15\u4e2a\u8fdc\u573a\u5e72\u6270\u8bf4\u8bdd\u4eba\u7684\u573a\u666f\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86Falcon\uff0c\u4e00\u79cd\u8f7b\u91cf\u7ea7\u7684\u97f3\u89c6\u9891\u76ee\u6807\u8bf4\u8bdd\u4eba\u63d0\u53d6\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u80fd\u591f\u5728\u5355\u6b21\u7f16\u7801-\u89e3\u7801\u4e2d\u8fdb\u884c\u591a\u5c3a\u5ea6\u65f6\u95f4\u9891\u7387\u5206\u79bb\u3002</li>\n    <li>Falcon\u4f7f\u7528\u4e86\u4e00\u79cd\u7f16\u7801\u7a7a\u95f4\u8bf4\u8bdd\u4eba\u5bf9\u6bd4\u635f\u5931\uff0c\u6709\u6548\u6291\u5236\u8fd1\u573a\u6cc4\u6f0f\uff0c\u4e14\u51e0\u4e4e\u6ca1\u6709\u63a8\u7406\u6210\u672c\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4e0e\u57fa\u7ebf\u6a21\u578b\u76f8\u6bd4\uff0cFalcon\u5728\u53c2\u6570\u3001\u8ba1\u7b97\u91cf\u548cGPU\u63a8\u7406\u5ef6\u8fdf\u4e0a\u90fd\u6709\u663e\u8457\u51cf\u5c11\uff0c\u8fbe\u5230\u6700\u65b0\u63d0\u53d6\u8d28\u91cf\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>The study focuses on improving audio-visual target speaker extraction (AVTSE) for applications sensitive to delays.</li>\n    <li>Current benchmarks only test with two speakers and do not account for real-world noises and interference.</li>\n    <li>RealSSA is introduced as a new benchmark featuring both simulated and real-world audio scenes with multiple speakers and noises.</li>\n    <li>Falcon is a new AVTSE method that uses a unique approach to separate sounds efficiently while reducing the size and speed requirements for edge devices.</li>\n    <li>Falcon significantly outperforms previous models, reducing parameters and computation while improving extraction quality.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-22T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Efficient Streaming Audio-Visual Target Speaker Extraction for Real-World Acoustic Scenes", "summary": "Streaming audio-visual target speaker extraction (AVTSE) is essential for latency-sensitive applications. However, current causal systems face two coupled gaps. First, standard benchmarks fix the number of speakers at two and ignore the near-/far-field interference, reverberation, and sudden noise of real scenes. Second, high-performance causal architectures are too heavy for edge deployment. Lightweight alternatives try to reclaim capacity by repeatedly invoking a small separator, which offsets the savings. To address the first gap, we release RealSSA, a realistic AVTSE benchmark consisting of RealSSA-Sim and RealSSA-Real. RealSSA-Sim provides controllable simulated scenes with 1--5 dynamic near-field interfering speakers; scenes with far-field interference additionally include 10--15 far-field interfering speakers. RealSSA-Real provides a held-out real-recorded evaluation set. To address the second gap, we propose Falcon, a causal AVTSE method. Its Separator performs multi-scale time-frequency separation in a single encoder--decoder pass. We train Falcon with an encoder-space speaker contrastive loss. This loss suppresses near-field leakage at zero inference cost and transfers across backbones. Falcon achieves state-of-the-art extraction quality on RealSSA-Sim, LRS2-2Mix, LRS3-2Mix, and VoxCeleb2-2Mix. Compared with the causal AV-TFGridNet baseline, it cuts parameters by 91.9%, computation by 20.2 times, and GPU inference latency by 11.1 times. Code and demo are available at https://cslikai.cn/Falcon/.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6387676c23da90491eb9fb16/fyfAlyMRka4HqWEFfbAoN.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.32749.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "6387676c23da90491eb9fb16", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1669818175965-noauth.jpeg", "fullname": "Kai Li", "name": "JusperLee", "type": "user", "isPro": true, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 62, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "640d3084536d9fe0f005cac3", "name": "thu-ml", "fullname": "Tsinghua Machine Learning Group", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/1678587085174-633131798ef21f47308ce49b.jpeg"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.36456", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070c7", "name": "Yuxuan Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070c8", "name": "Feng Xie", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070c9", "name": "Jiagao Hu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070ca", "name": "Fuhao Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070cb", "name": "Zepeng Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070cc", "name": "Fei Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070cd", "name": "Daiguo Zhou", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/649176436cadae13f22d014b/5rZZkERA6tUqDATPYhbhm.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "LociArt: A Unified Paradigm for Free-Style Local Video Stylization", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "649176436cadae13f22d014b", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/2cab0804761d986e2769d68063a59081.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Higher", "user": "HigherHu", "type": "user", "name": "HigherHu"}, "summary": "Video stylization typically transforms entire scenes, while local artistic editing requires precise region--style association, spatial control, and temporal consistency. We introduce LociArt, a unified paradigm for free-style local video stylization that explicitly specifies where to stylize and what style to apply while preserving non-target content. At its core, GRNStyle builds on Generative Refinement Networks (GRN) and introduces a polar style-aware mask that embeds style identities as equal-norm directions within selected regions. Complementary representation--geometry conditioning combines encoded mask features with raw-mask spatial support, integrating spatiotemporal context with geometric control. To provide paired supervision, we develop an automated pipeline combining global stylization, video object removal, matting, and compositing, and construct the LociArt-100K Dataset with 100K local stylization pairs. We further establish the LociArt Benchmark, comprising 80 source videos and 84 target masks covering 168 foreground/background stylization cases, with a Style Prototype Similarity (SPS) evaluation scheme for regional style quality and complementary measures of non-target preservation and temporal consistency. Experiments show that GRNStyle outperforms existing methods in regional style quality and spatial control while maintaining strong temporal consistency. Qualitative examples further illustrate joint stylization of multiple regions with the same or different learned styles, despite training only on single-region, single-style pairs. Together, these components provide a unified foundation for learning and evaluating controllable local video stylization. Project page: https://higherhu.github.io/lociart/.", "upvotes": 25, "discussionId": "6ae14bb6a5eb0647c29070ce", "projectPage": "https://higherhu.github.io/lociart/", "ai_summary": "LociArt enables precise local video stylization with region-style control and temporal consistency via a generative refinement network, automated dataset construction, and a dedicated benchmark.", "ai_keywords": ["Generative Refinement Networks", "polar style-aware mask", "representation-geometry conditioning", "LociArt-100K Dataset", "Style Prototype Similarity", "local video stylization", "temporal consistency"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "6821ba7e5a7efab94a235406", "name": "xiaomi-research", "fullname": "Xiaomi Research", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/673735e4373ad40af7f81ea1/DR4m0bz2Du1l0Z8Txg351.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86\u4e00\u79cd\u540d\u4e3a LociArt \u7684\u65b0\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u53ef\u4ee5\u8fdb\u884c\u81ea\u7531\u98ce\u683c\u7684\u5c40\u90e8\u89c6\u9891\u98ce\u683c\u5316\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8be5\u65b9\u6cd5\u5141\u8bb8\u7528\u6237\u7cbe\u786e\u6307\u5b9a\u60f3\u8981\u98ce\u683c\u5316\u7684\u533a\u57df\u548c\u5e94\u7528\u7684\u98ce\u683c\uff0c\u540c\u65f6\u4fdd\u6301\u975e\u76ee\u6807\u5185\u5bb9\u4e0d\u53d8\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4f7f\u7528\u751f\u6210\u7cbe\u70bc\u7f51\u7edc\uff08GRN\uff09\u548c\u6837\u5f0f\u611f\u77e5\u63a9\u7801\uff0c\u4ee5\u5b9e\u73b0\u7a7a\u95f4\u63a7\u5236\u548c\u65f6\u95f4\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5f00\u53d1\u4e86\u4e00\u4e2a\u81ea\u52a8\u5316\u6d41\u7a0b\uff0c\u7ed3\u5408\u5168\u7403\u98ce\u683c\u5316\u3001\u89c6\u9891\u5bf9\u8c61\u79fb\u9664\u3001\u62a0\u56fe\u548c\u5408\u6210\uff0c\u6784\u5efa\u4e86\u5305\u542b 10 \u4e07\u5bf9\u5c40\u90e8\u98ce\u683c\u5316\u7684 LociArt-100K \u6570\u636e\u96c6\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u7ed3\u679c\u8868\u660e\uff0cGRNStyle \u5728\u533a\u57df\u98ce\u683c\u8d28\u91cf\u548c\u7a7a\u95f4\u63a7\u5236\u65b9\u9762\u4f18\u4e8e\u73b0\u6709\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u5e76\u4e14\u4fdd\u6301\u4e86\u826f\u597d\u7684\u65f6\u95f4\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>LociArt is a new method for local video stylization that allows users to choose specific areas to apply artistic styles while keeping the rest of the video unchanged.</li>\n    <li>It uses Generative Refinement Networks (GRN) and a special mask to define styles for different regions in a video.</li>\n    <li>They created a large dataset (LociArt-100K) with 100,000 pairs of videos and stylizations to train their model.</li>\n    <li>LociArt Benchmark includes 80 videos and 84 masks to evaluate the quality of style application and consistency over time.</li>\n    <li>Tests show that GRNStyle is better than other methods at maintaining style quality and ensuring smooth transitions in videos.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "LociArt: A Unified Paradigm for Free-Style Local Video Stylization", "summary": "Video stylization typically transforms entire scenes, while local artistic editing requires precise region--style association, spatial control, and temporal consistency. We introduce LociArt, a unified paradigm for free-style local video stylization that explicitly specifies where to stylize and what style to apply while preserving non-target content. At its core, GRNStyle builds on Generative Refinement Networks (GRN) and introduces a polar style-aware mask that embeds style identities as equal-norm directions within selected regions. Complementary representation--geometry conditioning combines encoded mask features with raw-mask spatial support, integrating spatiotemporal context with geometric control. To provide paired supervision, we develop an automated pipeline combining global stylization, video object removal, matting, and compositing, and construct the LociArt-100K Dataset with 100K local stylization pairs. We further establish the LociArt Benchmark, comprising 80 source videos and 84 target masks covering 168 foreground/background stylization cases, with a Style Prototype Similarity (SPS) evaluation scheme for regional style quality and complementary measures of non-target preservation and temporal consistency. Experiments show that GRNStyle outperforms existing methods in regional style quality and spatial control while maintaining strong temporal consistency. Qualitative examples further illustrate joint stylization of multiple regions with the same or different learned styles, despite training only on single-region, single-style pairs. Together, these components provide a unified foundation for learning and evaluating controllable local video stylization. Project page: https://higherhu.github.io/lociart/.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/649176436cadae13f22d014b/5rZZkERA6tUqDATPYhbhm.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.36456.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "649176436cadae13f22d014b", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/2cab0804761d986e2769d68063a59081.svg", "fullname": "Higher", "name": "HigherHu", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 1, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6821ba7e5a7efab94a235406", "name": "xiaomi-research", "fullname": "Xiaomi Research", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/673735e4373ad40af7f81ea1/DR4m0bz2Du1l0Z8Txg351.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}],
    "month": [{"paper": {"id": "2610.35201", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7ae", "name": "Hongze Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7af", "name": "Miao Pan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b0", "name": "Lizhou Liang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b1", "name": "Qinfeng Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b2", "name": "Xinyin Ma", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b3", "name": "Yongliang Shen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b4", "name": "Weishi Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b5", "name": "Daniel Dahlmeier", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b6", "name": "Xuhong Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b7", "name": "Wenqi Zhang", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d/-HwSwdNgn5m4Bx9b0Riez.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "DTCA: Denoising Token Credit Assignment for Reinforcement Learning in Diffusion Language Models", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/18348cb2ef91757f3450c18debd11599.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Wenqi Zhang", "user": "zwq2018", "type": "user", "name": "zwq2018"}, "summary": "Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate text through iterative denoising, enabling bidirectional context modeling and parallel token refinement. Recent studies have applied reinforcement learning (RL) to DLMs and improved their reasoning performance. However, the iterative denoising process makes credit assignment more challenging, as final outcome rewards provide limited information about how different tokens contribute to the final output. In this paper, we propose Denoising Token Credit Assignment (DTCA), a framework for token-level credit assignment in reinforcement learning for diffusion language models. DTCA estimates token contributions by measuring the improvement in prediction confidence of remaining masked positions after token commitment and groups these signals according to part-of-speech (POS) categories. These contribution weights are used to select informative denoising timesteps and assign token-level learning signals during RL training. DTCA further calibrates these learning signals according to token-level entropy changes induced by policy updates. Experiments on LLaDA-8B-Instruct show that DTCA consistently improves mathematical reasoning performance across different generation budgets, with gains of up to 9.0 points on MATH500 and 5.3 points on GSM8K over the base model.", "upvotes": 254, "discussionId": "6ae2a281d670fc6ea896c7b8", "projectPage": "https://zju-omniai.github.io/DTCA/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/ZJU-OmniAI/DTCA", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "DTCA improves reinforcement learning for diffusion language models by assigning token-level credit via confidence improvements and entropy calibration, boosting mathematical reasoning.", "ai_keywords": ["diffusion language models", "reinforcement learning", "token-level credit assignment", "denoising token credit assignment", "part-of-speech categories", "denoising timesteps", "token-level entropy"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 3, "organization": {"_id": "696461ab2d94e9a07cdb8efd", "name": "OmniAI-ZJU", "fullname": "ZJU-OmniAI", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65f2595830354d0ee043b25a/eEeRdHlGyJ148JQ6BAJ4O.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u6269\u6563\u8bed\u8a00\u6a21\u578b\uff08DLMs\uff09\u901a\u8fc7\u8fed\u4ee3\u53bb\u566a\u751f\u6210\u6587\u672c\uff0c\u652f\u6301\u53cc\u5411\u4e0a\u4e0b\u6587\u5efa\u6a21\u548c\u5e76\u884c\u4ee4\u724c\u4f18\u5316\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6700\u8fd1\u7684\u7814\u7a76\u5c06\u5f3a\u5316\u5b66\u4e60\uff08RL\uff09\u5e94\u7528\u4e8eDLMs\uff0c\u63d0\u5347\u4e86\u63a8\u7406\u6027\u80fd\uff0c\u4f46\u53bb\u566a\u8fc7\u7a0b\u4f7f\u5f97\u4fe1\u8d37\u5206\u914d\u53d8\u5f97\u66f4\u52a0\u590d\u6742\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u672c\u6587\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86\u4e00\u79cd\u65b0\u7684\u6846\u67b6\u2014\u2014\u53bb\u566a\u4ee4\u724c\u4fe1\u8d37\u5206\u914d\uff08DTCA\uff09\uff0c\u7528\u4e8e\u5728\u5f3a\u5316\u5b66\u4e60\u4e2d\u5b9e\u73b0\u4ee4\u724c\u7ea7\u7684\u4fe1\u8d37\u5206\u914d\u3002</li>\n    <li>DTCA\u901a\u8fc7\u6d4b\u91cf\u4ee4\u724c\u627f\u8bfa\u540e\u5269\u4f59\u63a9\u7801\u4f4d\u7f6e\u7684\u9884\u6d4b\u7f6e\u4fe1\u5ea6\u6539\u5584\u6765\u4f30\u7b97\u4ee4\u724c\u8d21\u732e\uff0c\u5e76\u6309\u8bcd\u6027\u7c7b\u522b\u8fdb\u884c\u4fe1\u53f7\u5206\u7ec4\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u8868\u660e\uff0cDTCA\u5728\u4e0d\u540c\u751f\u6210\u9884\u7b97\u4e0b\u63d0\u5347\u4e86\u6570\u5b66\u63a8\u7406\u6027\u80fd\uff0cMATH500\u4e0a\u63d0\u5347\u4e86\u6700\u591a9.0\u5206\uff0cGSM8K\u4e0a\u63d0\u5347\u4e865.3\u5206\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate text by gradually removing noise, allowing for better understanding of context.</li>\n    <li>Reinforcement learning (RL) has been applied to DLMs to enhance their reasoning abilities.</li>\n    <li>However, it's hard to determine how much each word contributes to the final text because of the way DLMs work.</li>\n    <li>The paper introduces a new method called Denoising Token Credit Assignment (DTCA) to better assign credit to individual words in RL training.</li>\n    <li>Tests show that DTCA significantly improves mathematical reasoning in models, with notable performance increases in specific tasks.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "DTCA: Denoising Token Credit Assignment for Reinforcement Learning in Diffusion Language Models", "summary": "Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate text through iterative denoising, enabling bidirectional context modeling and parallel token refinement. Recent studies have applied reinforcement learning (RL) to DLMs and improved their reasoning performance. However, the iterative denoising process makes credit assignment more challenging, as final outcome rewards provide limited information about how different tokens contribute to the final output. In this paper, we propose Denoising Token Credit Assignment (DTCA), a framework for token-level credit assignment in reinforcement learning for diffusion language models. DTCA estimates token contributions by measuring the improvement in prediction confidence of remaining masked positions after token commitment and groups these signals according to part-of-speech (POS) categories. These contribution weights are used to select informative denoising timesteps and assign token-level learning signals during RL training. DTCA further calibrates these learning signals according to token-level entropy changes induced by policy updates. Experiments on LLaDA-8B-Instruct show that DTCA consistently improves mathematical reasoning performance across different generation budgets, with gains of up to 9.0 points on MATH500 and 5.3 points on GSM8K over the base model.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d/-HwSwdNgn5m4Bx9b0Riez.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.35201.png", "numComments": 3, "submittedBy": {"_id": "6485bd278d14bcd5cdbb7c8d", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/18348cb2ef91757f3450c18debd11599.svg", "fullname": "Wenqi Zhang", "name": "zwq2018", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 16, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "696461ab2d94e9a07cdb8efd", "name": "OmniAI-ZJU", "fullname": "ZJU-OmniAI", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65f2595830354d0ee043b25a/eEeRdHlGyJ148JQ6BAJ4O.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.34283", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713b", "name": "Yihao Hu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713c", "name": "Yitong Lin", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713d", "name": "Pan Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713e", "name": "Zhihao Wen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c290713f", "name": "Hang Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907140", "name": "Yiwen Zhao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907141", "name": "Deyuan Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907142", "name": "Yutong Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907143", "name": "Mingda Wan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907144", "name": "Yuchen Mao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907145", "name": "Tao Lin", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "ChronoReplay: One Answer Interface Is Not Enough for Streaming Spatial Intelligence", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "67e40cea6d0207b1fa43c826", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/_OzAJCjFC49dEkHZm4AQb.png", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Yihao Hu", "user": "HU22333", "type": "user", "name": "HU22333"}, "summary": "Training a spatial VLM to answer which side an object lies on does not require it to preserve the metric displacement that determines that answer, while supervision for a distance estimate may leave categorical relations largely unconstrained. When post-training emphasizes one readout, improvements can therefore concentrate on that interface while transfer to others remains weak or even degrades; we call this observed specialization interface lock-in. ChronoReplay addresses it by replaying heterogeneous spatial supervision as chronologically ordered episodes, retaining source-native targets, and balancing supervision across families. Under the same observation access, a single ChronoReplay checkpoint improves OVO-S-Bench, OST-Bench, and UCS-Bench together, including a 13.41-point gain on OST-Bench, and achieves the highest three-benchmark mean among the supervision settings compared here. Same-data controls further separate the ingredients of this transfer: answer serialization redistributes gains across OST and UCS, family-balanced learning improves over ordinary joint SFT and natural turn weighting, and chronological replay exceeds a shifted-order control by about 2.41 OST points across two paired seeds. Adaptive frame selection then changes evidence access while keeping the trained model fixed, providing an additional inference-time gain at lower visual cost. Streaming spatial performance depends on what distinctions supervision preserves, how heterogeneous histories are organized during learning, and what causal evidence inference makes available at test time.", "upvotes": 77, "discussionId": "6ae15489a5eb0647c2907146", "projectPage": "https://yihaohu0118.github.io/ChronoReplay/", "ai_summary": "ChronoReplay mitigates interface lock-in in spatial vision-language models by replaying heterogeneous spatial supervision chronologically, improving cross-task transfer and enabling efficient inference-time evidence selection.", "ai_keywords": ["spatial VLM", "interface lock-in", "ChronoReplay", "joint SFT", "adaptive frame selection", "OVO-S-Bench", "OST-Bench", "UCS-Bench"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "6650967d53061f0d1b5a7962", "name": "LINs-lab", "fullname": "LINs lab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65296b8a6cdea40585bd81e2/M80w6f0H8dfx3Sa1nVmfq.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u7a7a\u95f4\u89c6\u89c9\u8bed\u8a00\u6a21\u578b\uff08VLM\uff09\u7684\u8bad\u7ec3\u4e0d\u9700\u8981\u4fdd\u7559\u51b3\u5b9a\u7269\u4f53\u4f4d\u7f6e\u7684\u5ea6\u91cf\u4f4d\u79fb\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5f53\u8bad\u7ec3\u5f3a\u8c03\u5355\u4e00\u8f93\u51fa\u65f6\uff0c\u53ef\u80fd\u5bfc\u81f4\u5176\u4ed6\u63a5\u53e3\u7684\u8f6c\u79fb\u6548\u679c\u8f83\u5f31\uff0c\u79f0\u4e3a\u201c\u4e13\u4e1a\u5316\u63a5\u53e3\u9501\u5b9a\u201d\u3002</li>\n    <li>ChronoReplay\u901a\u8fc7\u987a\u5e8f\u91cd\u64ad\u4e0d\u540c\u7c7b\u578b\u7684\u7a7a\u95f4\u76d1\u7763\u6765\u89e3\u51b3\u8fd9\u4e00\u95ee\u9898\uff0c\u4fdd\u6301\u6e90\u76ee\u6807\u7684\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u4e0d\u540c\u7c7b\u522b\u4e4b\u95f4\u5e73\u8861\u76d1\u7763\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4f7f\u7528ChronoReplay\u7684\u6a21\u578b\u5728\u591a\u4e2a\u57fa\u51c6\u6d4b\u8bd5\u4e2d\u8868\u73b0\u4f18\u5f02\uff0c\u5305\u62ecOST-Bench\u63d0\u9ad8\u4e8613.41\u5206\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u6240\u6709\u76d1\u7763\u8bbe\u7f6e\u4e2d\u83b7\u5f97\u6700\u9ad8\u7684\u5e73\u5747\u5206\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u9002\u5e94\u6027\u5e27\u9009\u62e9\u5728\u4fdd\u6301\u6a21\u578b\u4e0d\u53d8\u7684\u60c5\u51b5\u4e0b\u6539\u53d8\u8bc1\u636e\u83b7\u53d6\uff0c\u8fdb\u4e00\u6b65\u63d0\u5347\u63a8\u7406\u65f6\u7684\u6027\u80fd\u3002 </li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Training a spatial Visual Language Model (VLM) to determine object positions doesn't always keep accurate distance measurements, leading to a focus on specific tasks instead of a balanced approach.</li>\n    <li>This issue, called \"specialization interface lock-in,\" can limit performance in other areas when training emphasizes one aspect too much.</li>\n    <li>ChronoReplay addresses this by using diverse spatial supervision in a chronological order, which helps improve overall performance across different benchmarks.</li>\n    <li>With ChronoReplay, there were significant gains in testing benchmarks, including a notable improvement of 13.41 points on OST-Bench, and it achieved the best average scores among the compared methods.</li>\n    <li>Adaptive frame selection allows for better use of evidence during testing, enhancing performance while minimizing visual processing demands.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-24T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "ChronoReplay: One Answer Interface Is Not Enough for Streaming Spatial Intelligence", "summary": "Training a spatial VLM to answer which side an object lies on does not require it to preserve the metric displacement that determines that answer, while supervision for a distance estimate may leave categorical relations largely unconstrained. When post-training emphasizes one readout, improvements can therefore concentrate on that interface while transfer to others remains weak or even degrades; we call this observed specialization interface lock-in. ChronoReplay addresses it by replaying heterogeneous spatial supervision as chronologically ordered episodes, retaining source-native targets, and balancing supervision across families. Under the same observation access, a single ChronoReplay checkpoint improves OVO-S-Bench, OST-Bench, and UCS-Bench together, including a 13.41-point gain on OST-Bench, and achieves the highest three-benchmark mean among the supervision settings compared here. Same-data controls further separate the ingredients of this transfer: answer serialization redistributes gains across OST and UCS, family-balanced learning improves over ordinary joint SFT and natural turn weighting, and chronological replay exceeds a shifted-order control by about 2.41 OST points across two paired seeds. Adaptive frame selection then changes evidence access while keeping the trained model fixed, providing an additional inference-time gain at lower visual cost. Streaming spatial performance depends on what distinctions supervision preserves, how heterogeneous histories are organized during learning, and what causal evidence inference makes available at test time.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.34283.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "67e40cea6d0207b1fa43c826", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/_OzAJCjFC49dEkHZm4AQb.png", "fullname": "Yihao Hu", "name": "HU22333", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 1, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6650967d53061f0d1b5a7962", "name": "LINs-lab", "fullname": "LINs lab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65296b8a6cdea40585bd81e2/M80w6f0H8dfx3Sa1nVmfq.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.06100", "authors": [{"_id": "6ac4b70a9eb4ee6bd77a9766", "user": {"_id": "65c1d1bda2239cf479ecf573", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/70483a134b3236d690fc4d9409f1ecad.svg", "isPro": true, "fullname": "Long", "user": "Quanyu001", "type": "user", "name": "Quanyu001"}, "name": "Quanyu Long", "status": "claimed_verified", "statusLastChangedAt": "2026-10-06T09:20:03.874Z", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac4b70a9eb4ee6bd77a9767", "user": {"_id": "64969bccf590e76c93938582", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/363598cba9e9496852e60144694b2f1c.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "shawncchen", "user": "chrisCCC", "type": "user", "name": "chrisCCC"}, "name": "Xiao Chen", "status": "claimed_verified", "statusLastChangedAt": "2026-10-07T08:07:49.844Z", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac4b70a9eb4ee6bd77a9768", "name": "Jianda Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac4b70a9eb4ee6bd77a9769", "name": "Haozhen Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac4b70a9eb4ee6bd77a976a", "name": "Qisheng Hu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac4b70a9eb4ee6bd77a976b", "name": "Jianzhu Bao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac4b70a9eb4ee6bd77a976c", "name": "Wenya Wang", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-05T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-09T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "From Traces to Agentic Worlds: Agentic Language World Models for Interactive Environment Simulation", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "65c1d1bda2239cf479ecf573", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/70483a134b3236d690fc4d9409f1ecad.svg", "isPro": true, "fullname": "Long", "user": "Quanyu001", "type": "user", "name": "Quanyu001"}, "summary": "Realistic environment replicas are increasingly valuable for training and evaluating LLM agents, yet the original systems may be inaccessible or impractical to reproduce. We explore agentic language world modeling: rather than rebuilding an executable environment, a world model agent serves as the environment for a task agent and supports faithful and stateful simulation. We instantiate this paradigm with Trace2Env, a learning-free framework for settings where the original system is unavailable but historical interaction traces remain accessible. Trace2Env reconstructs these traces into a reusable environment worldbook containing environment schemas, grounded evidence, and induced behavioral knowledge. At runtime, the world model agent actively consults the worldbook together with persistent episodic state to infer each action's observation and lasting state effects. Across nine environments, Trace2Env improves both next-observation fidelity and long-horizon interaction consistency over conventional prompt-based LWMs. In multi-turn interaction, task agent actions generated against Trace2Env remain valid more often when replayed in the real environment, indicating that its simulated dynamics better preserve the consequences of earlier actions across successive turns. These results establish agentic language world modeling as an alternative direction for building realistic environment replicas without reconstructing the original executable system.", "upvotes": 64, "discussionId": "6ac4b70a9eb4ee6bd77a976d", "projectPage": "https://quanyulong.net/trace2env/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/ruyue0001/trace2env", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "githubStars": 17, "organization": {"_id": "6371470aafbe42caa5a76208", "name": "nanyang-technological-university-singapore", "fullname": "Nanyang Technological University Singapore", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/637146c5afbe42caa5a75e1b/sZyHSA1AQaAS4nrGan682.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u771f\u5b9e\u73af\u5883\u590d\u5236\u54c1\u5bf9\u8bad\u7ec3\u548c\u8bc4\u4f30\u5927\u8bed\u8a00\u6a21\u578b\u4ee3\u7406\u8d8a\u6765\u8d8a\u91cd\u8981\uff0c\u4f46\u539f\u59cb\u7cfb\u7edf\u53ef\u80fd\u96be\u4ee5\u8bbf\u95ee\u6216\u91cd\u5efa\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u63a2\u7d22\u4e86\u4e00\u79cd\u65b0\u7684\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u79f0\u4e3a\u4ee3\u7406\u8bed\u8a00\u4e16\u754c\u5efa\u6a21\uff0c\u901a\u8fc7\u4e16\u754c\u6a21\u578b\u4ee3\u7406\u6765\u6a21\u62df\u73af\u5883\uff0c\u800c\u4e0d\u662f\u91cd\u5efa\u53ef\u6267\u884c\u73af\u5883\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86Trace2Env\uff0c\u4e00\u4e2a\u65e0\u9700\u5b66\u4e60\u7684\u6846\u67b6\uff0c\u53ef\u4ee5\u5728\u539f\u59cb\u7cfb\u7edf\u4e0d\u53ef\u7528\u7684\u60c5\u51b5\u4e0b\u5229\u7528\u5386\u53f2\u4ea4\u4e92\u8bb0\u5f55\u91cd\u5efa\u53ef\u7528\u73af\u5883\u3002</li>\n    <li>Trace2Env\u901a\u8fc7\u6784\u5efa\u73af\u5883\u4e16\u754c\u4e66\uff0c\u5305\u542b\u73af\u5883\u67b6\u6784\u3001\u8bc1\u636e\u548c\u884c\u4e3a\u77e5\u8bc6\uff0c\u4ee5\u652f\u6301\u4efb\u52a1\u4ee3\u7406\u7684\u51b3\u7b56\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5728\u4e5d\u4e2a\u73af\u5883\u4e2d\uff0cTrace2Env\u5728\u89c2\u5bdf\u7cbe\u5ea6\u548c\u957f\u65f6\u95f4\u4ea4\u4e92\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\u4e0a\u4f18\u4e8e\u4f20\u7edf\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u4e14\u5728\u591a\u8f6e\u4ea4\u4e92\u4e2d\u66f4\u80fd\u4fdd\u7559\u65e9\u671f\u52a8\u4f5c\u7684\u540e\u679c\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Realistic environment replicas are important for training language model agents, but recreating the original systems can be difficult.</li>\n    <li>Agentic language world modeling uses a world model agent to simulate environments without needing to rebuild them.</li>\n    <li>Trace2Env is a new framework that builds a reusable environment from historical interaction traces when the original system is unavailable.</li>\n    <li>It improves the accuracy of next observations and consistency in long interactions compared to traditional methods.</li>\n    <li>Results show that actions generated in the Trace2Env are more likely to remain valid when tested in real environments.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-04T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "From Traces to Agentic Worlds: Agentic Language World Models for Interactive Environment Simulation", "summary": "Realistic environment replicas are increasingly valuable for training and evaluating LLM agents, yet the original systems may be inaccessible or impractical to reproduce. We explore agentic language world modeling: rather than rebuilding an executable environment, a world model agent serves as the environment for a task agent and supports faithful and stateful simulation. We instantiate this paradigm with Trace2Env, a learning-free framework for settings where the original system is unavailable but historical interaction traces remain accessible. Trace2Env reconstructs these traces into a reusable environment worldbook containing environment schemas, grounded evidence, and induced behavioral knowledge. At runtime, the world model agent actively consults the worldbook together with persistent episodic state to infer each action's observation and lasting state effects. Across nine environments, Trace2Env improves both next-observation fidelity and long-horizon interaction consistency over conventional prompt-based LWMs. In multi-turn interaction, task agent actions generated against Trace2Env remain valid more often when replayed in the real environment, indicating that its simulated dynamics better preserve the consequences of earlier actions across successive turns. These results establish agentic language world modeling as an alternative direction for building realistic environment replicas without reconstructing the original executable system.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.06100.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "65c1d1bda2239cf479ecf573", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/70483a134b3236d690fc4d9409f1ecad.svg", "fullname": "Long", "name": "Quanyu001", "type": "user", "isPro": true, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6371470aafbe42caa5a76208", "name": "nanyang-technological-university-singapore", "fullname": "Nanyang Technological University Singapore", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/637146c5afbe42caa5a75e1b/sZyHSA1AQaAS4nrGan682.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": true}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.18784", "authors": [{"_id": "6ad00783aab7978cb679dcde", "user": {"_id": "680462011c5a7a2228526d9c", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/2004afde5ba4050c4b53ef1786d02012.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "YUCHEN LU", "user": "callmeethan", "type": "user", "name": "callmeethan"}, "name": "Yuchen Lu", "status": "claimed_verified", "statusLastChangedAt": "2026-10-15T08:45:06.727Z", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad00783aab7978cb679dcdf", "name": "Julian McAuley", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad00783aab7978cb679dce0", "user": {"_id": "66f47c8d29b10ae4c97292c4", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/66f47c8d29b10ae4c97292c4/W3o7EsgKT4CgNyBMH_vxh.jpeg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Lei Zhang", "user": "leiiizhang", "type": "user", "name": "leiiizhang"}, "name": "Lei Zhang", "status": "claimed_verified", "statusLastChangedAt": "2026-10-16T16:45:05.346Z", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/680462011c5a7a2228526d9c/xDGQ9e03X6BKBrr2NO0hW.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T17:35:27.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-19T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Pack Light, CarryOn: Building Object-Oriented Context for Long-Horizon Agents", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "680462011c5a7a2228526d9c", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/2004afde5ba4050c4b53ef1786d02012.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "YUCHEN LU", "user": "callmeethan", "type": "user", "name": "callmeethan"}, "summary": "Long-horizon agents accumulate progress in persistent task state, yet repeatedly process interaction histories describing how that progress was achieved. This raises a natural question: can long-horizon context instead be organized around reusable progress rather than the history that produced it? In this paper, we introduce CarryOn, an object-oriented context runtime that organizes continuation around reusable task objects. Rather than treating compressed histories or reconstructed accounts of progress as the primary basis for continuation, CarryOn builds a context view from continuation carriers, complementary execution context, and a live tail of recent interactions. A core challenge is keeping this reusable basis valid after its surrounding history is retired: how can CarryOn update the basis without reconstructing the full representation at every fold? CarryOn maintains carrier records across folds, revising affected records against bound evidence and applicable environment checks while carrying forward unaffected records and jointly updating execution context. Experiments on OSWorld 2.0 and BrowseComp-Plus show improved gains of 5.8% and 7.0% while reducing total token usage by 51.9% and 22.9%, respectively. Project page: https://ethannju.github.io/carryon", "upvotes": 63, "discussionId": "6ad00783aab7978cb679dce1", "projectPage": "https://ethannju.github.io/carryon/", "ai_summary": "CarryOn organizes long-horizon agent context around reusable task objects rather than interaction histories, improving efficiency and performance on complex benchmarks.", "ai_keywords": ["object-oriented context runtime", "continuation carriers", "carrier records", "execution context", "OSWorld 2.0", "BrowseComp-Plus"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "organization": {"_id": "65af44a1637e10fba942ed0c", "name": "McAuley-Lab", "fullname": "McAuley-Lab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64daab70c38427829daf5958/OWlh6vciWnY_MeyM099wZ.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n  <li>\u957f\u65f6\u95f4\u7684\u4efb\u52a1\u4ee3\u7406\u4f1a\u79ef\u7d2f\u6301\u7eed\u7684\u4efb\u52a1\u72b6\u6001\uff0c\u5e76\u5904\u7406\u4e92\u52a8\u5386\u53f2\u3002</li>\n  <li>\u672c\u6587\u4ecb\u7ecd\u4e86CarryOn\uff0c\u4e00\u4e2a\u9762\u5411\u5bf9\u8c61\u7684\u4e0a\u4e0b\u6587\u8fd0\u884c\u65f6\uff0c\u56f4\u7ed5\u53ef\u91cd\u7528\u7684\u4efb\u52a1\u5bf9\u8c61\u7ec4\u7ec7\u4e0a\u4e0b\u6587\u3002</li>\n  <li>CarryOn\u4e0d\u662f\u57fa\u4e8e\u538b\u7f29\u5386\u53f2\uff0c\u800c\u662f\u901a\u8fc7\u6301\u7eed\u627f\u8f7d\u4f53\u548c\u6700\u8fd1\u4e92\u52a8\u7684\u5b9e\u65f6\u5c3e\u90e8\u6784\u5efa\u4e0a\u4e0b\u6587\u89c6\u56fe\u3002</li>\n  <li>CarryOn\u9762\u4e34\u7684\u6311\u6218\u662f\u5982\u4f55\u5728\u5386\u53f2\u88ab\u6dd8\u6c70\u540e\u4fdd\u6301\u53ef\u91cd\u7528\u57fa\u7840\u7684\u6709\u6548\u6027\u3002</li>\n  <li>\u5b9e\u9a8c\u7ed3\u679c\u663e\u793a\uff0cCarryOn\u5728OSWorld 2.0\u548cBrowseComp-Plus\u4e0a\u5206\u522b\u63d0\u9ad8\u4e865.8%\u548c7.0%\u7684\u6027\u80fd\uff0c\u540c\u65f6\u51cf\u5c11\u4e8651.9%\u548c22.9%\u7684\u603b\u4ee4\u724c\u4f7f\u7528\u91cf\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>CarryOn is a system that helps long-term agents keep track of their progress in tasks without relying on the entire history of how they got there.</li>\n    <li>It organizes information around reusable task objects instead of just old interaction histories.</li>\n    <li>CarryOn updates its information efficiently, avoiding the need to fully reconstruct past data each time it needs to refresh its context.</li>\n    <li>Tests showed that CarryOn improved performance by 5.8% and 7.0% while significantly reducing the amount of data used by 51.9% and 22.9%.</li>\n    <li>You can find more information about CarryOn on its project page: <a href=\"https://ethannju.github.io/carryon\">https://ethannju.github.io/carryon</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T13:35:27.000Z", "title": "Pack Light, CarryOn: Building Object-Oriented Context for Long-Horizon Agents", "summary": "Long-horizon agents accumulate progress in persistent task state, yet repeatedly process interaction histories describing how that progress was achieved. This raises a natural question: can long-horizon context instead be organized around reusable progress rather than the history that produced it? In this paper, we introduce CarryOn, an object-oriented context runtime that organizes continuation around reusable task objects. Rather than treating compressed histories or reconstructed accounts of progress as the primary basis for continuation, CarryOn builds a context view from continuation carriers, complementary execution context, and a live tail of recent interactions. A core challenge is keeping this reusable basis valid after its surrounding history is retired: how can CarryOn update the basis without reconstructing the full representation at every fold? CarryOn maintains carrier records across folds, revising affected records against bound evidence and applicable environment checks while carrying forward unaffected records and jointly updating execution context. Experiments on OSWorld 2.0 and BrowseComp-Plus show improved gains of 5.8% and 7.0% while reducing total token usage by 51.9% and 22.9%, respectively. Project page: https://ethannju.github.io/carryon", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/680462011c5a7a2228526d9c/xDGQ9e03X6BKBrr2NO0hW.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.18784.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "680462011c5a7a2228526d9c", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/2004afde5ba4050c4b53ef1786d02012.svg", "fullname": "YUCHEN LU", "name": "callmeethan", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 2, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "65af44a1637e10fba942ed0c", "name": "McAuley-Lab", "fullname": "McAuley-Lab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64daab70c38427829daf5958/OWlh6vciWnY_MeyM099wZ.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": true}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.10528", "authors": [{"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed391", "name": "Wei Huang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed392", "name": "Bohan Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed393", "name": "Chenzhi Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed394", "name": "Isabella Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed395", "name": "Shuai Yang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed396", "name": "Weian Mao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed397", "name": "Luozhou Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed398", "name": "Yicheng Xiao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed399", "name": "Weifeng Lin", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed39a", "name": "Qixin Hu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed39b", "name": "Bryan Chu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed39c", "name": "Sifei Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed39d", "name": "Linxi Fan", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed39e", "name": "Xiaojuan Qi", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed39f", "name": "Song Han", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed3a0", "name": "Yukang Chen", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/656db3f53dc1d277e5a64410/xydlSuXEJrwor5qFTZzvU.qt"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Long-WAM: Scaling the Context of World-Action Models", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "656db3f53dc1d277e5a64410", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/656db3f53dc1d277e5a64410/9kiY2K3MCRcBDk7MrkTBK.png", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Wei Huang", "user": "AaronHuangWei", "type": "user", "name": "AaronHuangWei"}, "summary": "Real-time robot control demands enough visual history to infer motion and task progress, but processing that history can delay action. We present Long-WAM, a model-system framework for scaling the context of causal world-action models under real-time control constraints. Our central finding is that access to history is not the same as using it: longer histories pay off far more when the video foundation is pretrained autoregressively (AR). We first learn causal prediction from robot and egocentric videos without action labels, then preserve this history-to-future structure during world-action adaptation. On RoboCasa GR-1, increasing context from 0.0 to 19.2 seconds raises success from 63.3% to 78.7%, whereas a bidirectionally pretrained initialization shows no net gain; robot-domain AR pretraining further raises peak success on GR-1 and LIBERO-Long. Long-WAM also achieves the best results among compared methods on LIBERO-Long, RoboTwin 2.0, and DOMINO. Streaming observation encoding, asynchronous execution, and hardware-specific acceleration enable deployment on RTX 5090, DGX Spark, and Jetson AGX Thor without dropping future prediction; on RTX 5090, each action chunk, including future-video latent prediction, takes 107.4 ms. Real-time deployment on Unitree G1 and YAM supports dynamic and long-horizon manipulation, including 95% success on dynamic cup stacking, where Pi0.5 and Fast-WAM succeed in none of 20 trials. As a memory-informed executor, Long-WAM also complements higher-level planning in composite tasks.", "upvotes": 54, "discussionId": "6ac6fb044eb8005165aed3a1", "projectPage": "https://nvlabs.github.io/LongLive/Long-WAM/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/NVlabs/LongLive", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "githubStars": 2662, "organization": {"_id": "60262b67268c201cdc8b7d43", "name": "nvidia", "fullname": "NVIDIA", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65df9200dc3292a8983e5017/Vs5FPVCH-VZBipV3qKTuy.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>Long-WAM\u662f\u4e00\u79cd\u6a21\u578b\u7cfb\u7edf\u6846\u67b6\uff0c\u65e8\u5728\u5728\u5b9e\u65f6\u63a7\u5236\u4e2d\u63d0\u5347\u56e0\u679c\u4e16\u754c-\u52a8\u4f5c\u6a21\u578b\u7684\u4e0a\u4e0b\u6587\u5904\u7406\u80fd\u529b\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u901a\u8fc7\u5b66\u4e60\u673a\u5668\u4eba\u548c\u81ea\u6211\u4e2d\u5fc3\u89c6\u9891\u7684\u56e0\u679c\u9884\u6d4b\uff0cLong-WAM\u80fd\u591f\u5728\u6ca1\u6709\u52a8\u4f5c\u6807\u7b7e\u7684\u60c5\u51b5\u4e0b\u4f7f\u7528\u5386\u53f2\u4fe1\u606f\u6765\u63d0\u9ad8\u4efb\u52a1\u6210\u529f\u7387\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5728RoboCasa GR-1\u4e0a\uff0c\u589e\u52a0\u5386\u53f2\u4e0a\u4e0b\u6587\u65f6\u95f4\u4ece0\u79d2\u523019.2\u79d2\uff0c\u6210\u529f\u7387\u4ece63.3%\u63d0\u5347\u81f378.7%\u3002</li>\n    <li>Long-WAM\u5728\u591a\u4e2a\u5e73\u53f0\u4e0a\u5b9e\u73b0\u4e86\u6700\u4f73\u7ed3\u679c\uff0c\u5305\u62ecLIBERO-Long\u3001RoboTwin 2.0\u548cDOMINO\uff0c\u5e76\u652f\u6301\u52a8\u6001\u548c\u957f\u65f6\u95f4\u7684\u64cd\u4f5c\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8be5\u7cfb\u7edf\u5728Unitree G1\u548cYAM\u4e0a\u8fdb\u884c\u5b9e\u65f6\u90e8\u7f72\uff0c\u6210\u529f\u5b8c\u6210\u52a8\u6001\u676f\u5b50\u5806\u53e0\u4efb\u52a1\uff0c\u6210\u529f\u7387\u9ad8\u8fbe95%\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Long-WAM is a system designed to help robots make decisions in real-time by using visual history without causing delays.</li>\n    <li>Using longer video histories improves robot performance, especially when the model is pretrained effectively.</li>\n    <li>Tests showed that increasing the context from 0 to 19.2 seconds improved success rates significantly on various tasks.</li>\n    <li>Long-WAM outperformed other methods in several benchmarks, demonstrating its effectiveness in robotics.</li>\n    <li>It can be deployed on advanced hardware without compromising prediction speed and has shown high success rates in challenging tasks like dynamic cup stacking.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-06T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Long-WAM: Scaling the Context of World-Action Models", "summary": "Real-time robot control demands enough visual history to infer motion and task progress, but processing that history can delay action. We present Long-WAM, a model-system framework for scaling the context of causal world-action models under real-time control constraints. Our central finding is that access to history is not the same as using it: longer histories pay off far more when the video foundation is pretrained autoregressively (AR). We first learn causal prediction from robot and egocentric videos without action labels, then preserve this history-to-future structure during world-action adaptation. On RoboCasa GR-1, increasing context from 0.0 to 19.2 seconds raises success from 63.3% to 78.7%, whereas a bidirectionally pretrained initialization shows no net gain; robot-domain AR pretraining further raises peak success on GR-1 and LIBERO-Long. Long-WAM also achieves the best results among compared methods on LIBERO-Long, RoboTwin 2.0, and DOMINO. Streaming observation encoding, asynchronous execution, and hardware-specific acceleration enable deployment on RTX 5090, DGX Spark, and Jetson AGX Thor without dropping future prediction; on RTX 5090, each action chunk, including future-video latent prediction, takes 107.4 ms. Real-time deployment on Unitree G1 and YAM supports dynamic and long-horizon manipulation, including 95% success on dynamic cup stacking, where Pi0.5 and Fast-WAM succeed in none of 20 trials. As a memory-informed executor, Long-WAM also complements higher-level planning in composite tasks.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/656db3f53dc1d277e5a64410/xydlSuXEJrwor5qFTZzvU.qt"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.10528.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "656db3f53dc1d277e5a64410", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/656db3f53dc1d277e5a64410/9kiY2K3MCRcBDk7MrkTBK.png", "fullname": "Wei Huang", "name": "AaronHuangWei", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 19, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "60262b67268c201cdc8b7d43", "name": "nvidia", "fullname": "NVIDIA", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/65df9200dc3292a8983e5017/Vs5FPVCH-VZBipV3qKTuy.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.04299", "authors": [{"_id": "6ac445589eb4ee6bd77a9345", "name": "Jinyuan Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac445589eb4ee6bd77a9346", "name": "Chengsong Huang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac445589eb4ee6bd77a9347", "name": "Langlin Huang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac445589eb4ee6bd77a9348", "name": "Donghong Cai", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac445589eb4ee6bd77a9349", "name": "Shiping Gao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac445589eb4ee6bd77a934a", "name": "Yuyi Yang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac445589eb4ee6bd77a934b", "name": "Jiaxin Huang", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/64fc20d899123d7698a30e61/jETbBtAg6-jnVU4RPRvZb.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-03T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Questioning the Questions: Sustaining Self-Evolution in Reasoning Models", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "64fc20d899123d7698a30e61", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/9231982cf70a0689f50accedf1004702.svg", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Jinyuan Li", "user": "jinyuan222", "type": "user", "name": "jinyuan222"}, "summary": "Self-evolving reasoning models learn from their own generated questions, yet repeated self-training can lead to performance collapse. In this paper, we investigate why performance deteriorates over successive rounds and how to sustain self-evolution. Our analysis identifies two recurring quality problems in self-generated questions: invalid questions and repeated variants of the same mathematical questions. First, invalid questions become more prevalent across rounds, and answer-consistency filtering further increases their proportion in training data. Second, existing question diversity controls based on lexical similarity can miss mathematically equivalent questions expressed in different ways, which leads to question diversity collapse in later training rounds. Building on these findings, we introduce R-Quest, which uses question validity and novelty feedback to guide self-evolution. We first train the solver to recognize and reject invalid questions, then use its judgments to guide questioner rewards and filter solver training data. To avoid question repetition, we use a frozen base model to compare sampled question pairs and provide novelty feedback. Empirically, our method consistently achieves the highest average performance on 12 benchmarks in mathematical reasoning, general-domain reasoning, and code generation across two model families. Additionally, R-Quest maintains stable performance gains over ten rounds of self-evolution, peaking in the final round and outperforming R-Zero by 17.32 points.", "upvotes": 52, "discussionId": "6ac445589eb4ee6bd77a934c", "projectPage": "https://jinyuanli0012.github.io/R-Quest/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/JinYuanLi0012/R-Quest", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "githubStars": 1, "organization": {"_id": "670035f24055c4569f7dd024", "name": "HINT-lab", "fullname": "Huang's INTelligence lab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64efbf39b3610349e84db417/tbNZtAX3vJeGo2Rag_7ZN.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u81ea\u6211\u8fdb\u5316\u7684\u63a8\u7406\u6a21\u578b\u53ef\u4ee5\u901a\u8fc7\u751f\u6210\u7684\u95ee\u9898\u8fdb\u884c\u5b66\u4e60\uff0c\u4f46\u91cd\u590d\u81ea\u6211\u8bad\u7ec3\u53ef\u80fd\u5bfc\u81f4\u6027\u80fd\u4e0b\u964d\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u7814\u7a76\u53d1\u73b0\uff0c\u81ea\u751f\u6210\u95ee\u9898\u4e2d\u5b58\u5728\u65e0\u6548\u95ee\u9898\u548c\u91cd\u590d\u95ee\u9898\u7684\u8d28\u91cf\u95ee\u9898\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u65e0\u6548\u95ee\u9898\u5728\u8bad\u7ec3\u8f6e\u6b21\u4e2d\u4f1a\u589e\u52a0\uff0c\u800c\u73b0\u6709\u7684\u591a\u6837\u6027\u63a7\u5236\u65b9\u6cd5\u65e0\u6cd5\u6709\u6548\u533a\u5206\u6570\u5b66\u4e0a\u7b49\u4ef7\u4f46\u8868\u8ff0\u4e0d\u540c\u7684\u95ee\u9898\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86R-Quest\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u901a\u8fc7\u6709\u6548\u6027\u548c\u65b0\u9896\u6027\u53cd\u9988\u6765\u6307\u5bfc\u81ea\u6211\u8fdb\u5316\uff0c\u63d0\u5347\u95ee\u9898\u8d28\u91cf\u3002</li>\n    <li>R-Quest\u572812\u4e2a\u57fa\u51c6\u6d4b\u8bd5\u4e2d\u8868\u73b0\u6700\u597d\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u5341\u8f6e\u81ea\u6211\u8fdb\u5316\u4e2d\u4fdd\u6301\u7a33\u5b9a\u7684\u6027\u80fd\u63d0\u5347\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Self-evolving reasoning models can improve by learning from questions they generate, but repeated training can cause their performance to drop.</li>\n    <li>The study finds two main problems: more invalid questions appear over time, and there is less variety in the questions due to similarities.</li>\n    <li>To address these issues, the authors introduce a method called R-Quest that helps improve question quality and diversity.</li>\n    <li>R-Quest teaches the model to identify and reject invalid questions and uses feedback to encourage more unique questions.</li>\n    <li>This method shows strong results, consistently performing better across various tests and maintaining improvements over multiple training rounds.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-02T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Questioning the Questions: Sustaining Self-Evolution in Reasoning Models", "summary": "Self-evolving reasoning models learn from their own generated questions, yet repeated self-training can lead to performance collapse. In this paper, we investigate why performance deteriorates over successive rounds and how to sustain self-evolution. Our analysis identifies two recurring quality problems in self-generated questions: invalid questions and repeated variants of the same mathematical questions. First, invalid questions become more prevalent across rounds, and answer-consistency filtering further increases their proportion in training data. Second, existing question diversity controls based on lexical similarity can miss mathematically equivalent questions expressed in different ways, which leads to question diversity collapse in later training rounds. Building on these findings, we introduce R-Quest, which uses question validity and novelty feedback to guide self-evolution. We first train the solver to recognize and reject invalid questions, then use its judgments to guide questioner rewards and filter solver training data. To avoid question repetition, we use a frozen base model to compare sampled question pairs and provide novelty feedback. Empirically, our method consistently achieves the highest average performance on 12 benchmarks in mathematical reasoning, general-domain reasoning, and code generation across two model families. Additionally, R-Quest maintains stable performance gains over ten rounds of self-evolution, peaking in the final round and outperforming R-Zero by 17.32 points.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/64fc20d899123d7698a30e61/jETbBtAg6-jnVU4RPRvZb.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.04299.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "64fc20d899123d7698a30e61", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/9231982cf70a0689f50accedf1004702.svg", "fullname": "Jinyuan Li", "name": "jinyuan222", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 3, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "670035f24055c4569f7dd024", "name": "HINT-lab", "fullname": "Huang's INTelligence lab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/64efbf39b3610349e84db417/tbNZtAX3vJeGo2Rag_7ZN.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.18829", "authors": [{"_id": "6acf1130abe004a210f25dc4", "name": "Xuyang Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6acf1130abe004a210f25dc5", "name": "Shihao Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6acf1130abe004a210f25dc6", "name": "Xinyu Wei", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6acf1130abe004a210f25dc7", "name": "Minghan Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6acf1130abe004a210f25dc8", "name": "Zeqing Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6acf1130abe004a210f25dc9", "name": "Zuhao Yang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6acf1130abe004a210f25dca", "name": "Zichen Wen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6acf1130abe004a210f25dcb", "name": "Lei Zhang", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-16T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "OmniCompass: A Holistic Benchmark for Omni-Modal Video Grounding and Understanding", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "69db8a673114e93a4dbdec28", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/69db8a673114e93a4dbdec28/eJg3LOMGXpPKPa9lrzHtE.jpeg", "isPro": true, "fullname": "PolyU_VCLab", "user": "VCLab-PolyU", "type": "user", "name": "VCLab-PolyU"}, "summary": "With the rapid development of multimodal large language models (MLLMs), evaluating their video understanding capabilities has become increasingly important. Existing video understanding benchmarks cover only part of the evaluation dimensions, limiting systematic diagnosis of omni-modal video understanding. We introduce OmniCompass, a holistic benchmark for omni-modal video grounding and understanding. OmniCompass defines two evaluation tracks: OmniCompass-VL assesses vision-language models on tasks requiring visual evidence, while OmniCompass-AV assesses omni-modal models on tasks requiring joint audio-visual evidence. Each track includes three complementary tasks: temporal grounding (TG), spatial grounding (SG), and question answering (QA), assessing when a referenced target appears, where it is, and what happens. QA covers five perception and three reasoning categories in OmniCompass-VL, and three perception and two reasoning categories in OmniCompass-AV. OmniCompass-VL contains 820 videos and 2,460 instances across domains and three duration types: short, medium, and long. A narrative- and audio-rich subset forms OmniCompass-AV with 240 videos and 720 instances. Each instance is evaluated under two paired settings: text-only and text+image, specifying the same target through text and visual-language guidance. All instances are manually annotated with multi-round quality verification. Evaluations of 50 MLLMs show that open-source models still lag behind proprietary counterparts, particularly in audio-visual understanding. Precise temporal and spatial grounding remains challenging even for leading models, and their predictions still exhibit inconsistencies across the paired target specifications.", "upvotes": 51, "discussionId": "6acf1131abe004a210f25dcc", "projectPage": "https://omnicompass.netlify.app/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/PolyU-VCLab/OmniCompass", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "OmniCompass is a holistic benchmark for omni-modal video grounding and understanding that evaluates vision-language and audio-visual reasoning through temporal grounding, spatial grounding, and question answering tasks.", "ai_keywords": ["multimodal large language models", "video understanding", "temporal grounding", "spatial grounding", "question answering", "audio-visual reasoning", "vision-language models"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 54, "organization": {"_id": "69dba0c8dc88214a5ddca3f2", "name": "VCLab-HKPU", "fullname": "VCLab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/69db8a673114e93a4dbdec28/WgIv-pv3Mt2HBSdoR35eW.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u968f\u7740\u591a\u6a21\u6001\u5927\u578b\u8bed\u8a00\u6a21\u578b\u7684\u53d1\u5c55\uff0c\u8bc4\u4f30\u5b83\u4eec\u7684\u89c6\u9891\u7406\u89e3\u80fd\u529b\u53d8\u5f97\u8d8a\u6765\u8d8a\u91cd\u8981\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u63a8\u51fa\u4e86OmniCompass\uff0c\u8fd9\u662f\u4e00\u4e2a\u5168\u9762\u7684\u89c6\u9891\u5b9a\u4f4d\u548c\u7406\u89e3\u57fa\u51c6\u3002</li>\n    <li>OmniCompass\u5305\u62ec\u4e24\u4e2a\u8bc4\u4f30\u8f68\u9053\uff1aOmniCompass-VL\u548cOmniCompass-AV\uff0c\u5206\u522b\u8bc4\u4f30\u89c6\u89c9\u8bed\u8a00\u6a21\u578b\u548c\u97f3\u89c6\u9891\u6a21\u578b\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6bcf\u4e2a\u8f68\u9053\u6709\u4e09\u4e2a\u4efb\u52a1\uff1a\u65f6\u95f4\u5b9a\u4f4d\u3001\u7a7a\u95f4\u5b9a\u4f4d\u548c\u95ee\u7b54\uff0c\u8986\u76d6\u4e86\u4e0d\u540c\u7684\u611f\u77e5\u548c\u63a8\u7406\u7c7b\u522b\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8bc4\u4f30\u7ed3\u679c\u663e\u793a\uff0c\u5f00\u6e90\u6a21\u578b\u5728\u97f3\u89c6\u9891\u7406\u89e3\u65b9\u9762\u4ecd\u7136\u843d\u540e\u4e8e\u4e13\u6709\u6a21\u578b\uff0c\u5c24\u5176\u662f\u5728\u65f6\u95f4\u548c\u7a7a\u95f4\u5b9a\u4f4d\u4e0a\u8868\u73b0\u4e0d\u4f73\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>OmniCompass is a new benchmark designed to evaluate how well multimodal large language models (MLLMs) understand videos.</li>\n    <li>It includes two tracks: one for models that use visual information (OmniCompass-VL) and another for those that use both audio and visual information (OmniCompass-AV).</li>\n    <li>There are three main tasks in each track: finding out when (temporal grounding), where (spatial grounding), and what happens (question answering) in the videos.</li>\n    <li>OmniCompass-VL has 820 videos with various durations, while OmniCompass-AV has 240 videos that are rich in narrative and audio.</li>\n    <li>Tests show that open-source models perform worse than proprietary ones, especially in understanding audio-visual content, and often struggle with precise timing and location in their predictions.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "OmniCompass: A Holistic Benchmark for Omni-Modal Video Grounding and Understanding", "summary": "With the rapid development of multimodal large language models (MLLMs), evaluating their video understanding capabilities has become increasingly important. Existing video understanding benchmarks cover only part of the evaluation dimensions, limiting systematic diagnosis of omni-modal video understanding. We introduce OmniCompass, a holistic benchmark for omni-modal video grounding and understanding. OmniCompass defines two evaluation tracks: OmniCompass-VL assesses vision-language models on tasks requiring visual evidence, while OmniCompass-AV assesses omni-modal models on tasks requiring joint audio-visual evidence. Each track includes three complementary tasks: temporal grounding (TG), spatial grounding (SG), and question answering (QA), assessing when a referenced target appears, where it is, and what happens. QA covers five perception and three reasoning categories in OmniCompass-VL, and three perception and two reasoning categories in OmniCompass-AV. OmniCompass-VL contains 820 videos and 2,460 instances across domains and three duration types: short, medium, and long. A narrative- and audio-rich subset forms OmniCompass-AV with 240 videos and 720 instances. Each instance is evaluated under two paired settings: text-only and text+image, specifying the same target through text and visual-language guidance. All instances are manually annotated with multi-round quality verification. Evaluations of 50 MLLMs show that open-source models still lag behind proprietary counterparts, particularly in audio-visual understanding. Precise temporal and spatial grounding remains challenging even for leading models, and their predictions still exhibit inconsistencies across the paired target specifications.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.18829.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "69db8a673114e93a4dbdec28", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/69db8a673114e93a4dbdec28/eJg3LOMGXpPKPa9lrzHtE.jpeg", "fullname": "PolyU_VCLab", "name": "VCLab-PolyU", "type": "user", "isPro": true, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 9, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "69dba0c8dc88214a5ddca3f2", "name": "VCLab-HKPU", "fullname": "VCLab", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/69db8a673114e93a4dbdec28/WgIv-pv3Mt2HBSdoR35eW.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.20571", "authors": [{"_id": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de82", "name": "Weijie Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de83", "name": "Haomin Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de84", "name": "Ye Li", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de85", "name": "Akide Liu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de86", "name": "Zeyu Zhang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de87", "name": "Haoyu Zhao", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de88", "name": "Yefei He", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de89", "name": "Donny Y. Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de8a", "name": "Feng Chen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de8b", "name": "Bohan Zhuang", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/66699aa8a33847217b5a49c7/YgsTMXs-dTVwLakJbrZfc.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Any Time, Any Space: A World-State-Centric Framework for Agentic Video Generation", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "66699aa8a33847217b5a49c7", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/noauth/u8Z-6U8U7ARXOpdBDI7Qm.png", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Weijie Wang", "user": "lhmd", "type": "user", "name": "lhmd"}, "summary": "Long-form agentic video generation must turn a screenplay into shots that remain spatially and temporally consistent across nonconsecutive requests. Passing prompts or recent frames between shots does not provide a shared spatial frame for revisiting a scene or a time-indexed record for replaying an earlier event. We present Any Time, Any Space (ATAS), which compiles a screenplay into shot requests with explicit location, camera, and event-time references. Before generation, it retrieves relevant evidence from a scene memory of registered static geometry and an event memory of dynamic observations indexed by scene, event, and time. After generation, ATAS reconstructs and registers the clip. It updates memory only when review and state checks accept the observation, while film delivery remains independent of memory admission. Across four video generators, ATAS improves all reported cross-shot consistency measures and achieves the highest aggregate benchmark score for each backbone. It also outperforms the compared agents and 3D-guided methods on target-image spatial revisit, leads three event-recapture criteria, and receives the highest human ratings on all six criteria. The video results and code are available on our project page: https://lhmd.top/atas/.", "upvotes": 51, "discussionId": "6ad0370daab7978cb679de8c", "projectPage": "https://lhmd.top/atas/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/ziplab/ATAS", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "githubStars": 65, "organization": {"_id": "61bac2af530e5c78d7b99667", "name": "zju", "fullname": "Zhejiang University", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/5e1058e9fcf41d740b69966d/7G1xjlxwCdMEmKcxNR0n5.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>ATAS\uff08Any Time, Any Space\uff09\u80fd\u5c06\u5267\u672c\u8f6c\u6362\u4e3a\u955c\u5934\u8bf7\u6c42\uff0c\u786e\u4fdd\u7a7a\u95f4\u548c\u65f6\u95f4\u7684\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u5b83\u4ece\u573a\u666f\u8bb0\u5fc6\u548c\u4e8b\u4ef6\u8bb0\u5fc6\u4e2d\u68c0\u7d22\u76f8\u5173\u4fe1\u606f\uff0c\u4ee5\u652f\u6301\u955c\u5934\u751f\u6210\u3002</li>\n    <li>ATAS\u5728\u751f\u6210\u540e\u7684\u91cd\u5efa\u548c\u6ce8\u518c\u8fc7\u7a0b\u4e2d\uff0c\u66f4\u65b0\u8bb0\u5fc6\u4ec5\u5728\u5ba1\u6838\u901a\u8fc7\u65f6\u8fdb\u884c\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8be5\u65b9\u6cd5\u5728\u56db\u4e2a\u89c6\u9891\u751f\u6210\u5668\u4e2d\u63d0\u9ad8\u4e86\u955c\u5934\u95f4\u4e00\u81f4\u6027\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u57fa\u51c6\u6d4b\u8bd5\u4e2d\u83b7\u5f97\u4e86\u6700\u9ad8\u5206\u3002</li>\n    <li>ATAS\u5728\u76ee\u6807\u56fe\u50cf\u91cd\u8bbf\u548c\u4e8b\u4ef6\u6355\u6349\u65b9\u9762\u8d85\u8d8a\u4e86\u5176\u4ed6\u5bf9\u6bd4\u65b9\u6cd5\uff0c\u5e76\u5728\u516d\u4e2a\u6807\u51c6\u4e2d\u83b7\u5f97\u6700\u9ad8\u4eba\u7c7b\u8bc4\u5206\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>ATAS helps create consistent video shots from a screenplay by using clear references for location, camera, and timing.</li>\n    <li>It uses a memory system to keep track of scenes and events, ensuring accuracy when generating video clips.</li>\n    <li>ATAS updates its memory only when certain checks are passed, keeping video production separate from memory updates.</li>\n    <li>The method improves consistency across video shots and scores better than other video generation methods in multiple tests.</li>\n    <li>It has received high ratings from human reviewers and is available for public access on a project page.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Any Time, Any Space: A World-State-Centric Framework for Agentic Video Generation", "summary": "Long-form agentic video generation must turn a screenplay into shots that remain spatially and temporally consistent across nonconsecutive requests. Passing prompts or recent frames between shots does not provide a shared spatial frame for revisiting a scene or a time-indexed record for replaying an earlier event. We present Any Time, Any Space (ATAS), which compiles a screenplay into shot requests with explicit location, camera, and event-time references. Before generation, it retrieves relevant evidence from a scene memory of registered static geometry and an event memory of dynamic observations indexed by scene, event, and time. After generation, ATAS reconstructs and registers the clip. It updates memory only when review and state checks accept the observation, while film delivery remains independent of memory admission. Across four video generators, ATAS improves all reported cross-shot consistency measures and achieves the highest aggregate benchmark score for each backbone. It also outperforms the compared agents and 3D-guided methods on target-image spatial revisit, leads three event-recapture criteria, and receives the highest human ratings on all six criteria. The video results and code are available on our project page: https://lhmd.top/atas/.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/66699aa8a33847217b5a49c7/YgsTMXs-dTVwLakJbrZfc.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.20571.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "66699aa8a33847217b5a49c7", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/noauth/u8Z-6U8U7ARXOpdBDI7Qm.png", "fullname": "Weijie Wang", "name": "lhmd", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 17, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "61bac2af530e5c78d7b99667", "name": "zju", "fullname": "Zhejiang University", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/5e1058e9fcf41d740b69966d/7G1xjlxwCdMEmKcxNR0n5.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.33568", "authors": [{"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7903", "name": "Yihao Hu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7904", "name": "Zhihao Wen", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7905", "name": "Yitong Lin", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7906", "name": "Siyuan Lu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7907", "name": "Pan Wang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7908", "name": "Wei Wu", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a7909", "name": "Jiang-Ming Yang", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ae00a6fa5efa215400a790a", "name": "Tao Lin", "hidden": false}], "publishedAt": "2026-10-24T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "Trajectory2Gym: Decision-Grounded Compositional Interaction Synthesis for Tool-Use Agents", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "67e40cea6d0207b1fa43c826", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/_OzAJCjFC49dEkHZm4AQb.png", "isPro": false, "fullname": "Yihao Hu", "user": "HU22333", "type": "user", "name": "HU22333"}, "summary": "Successful tool-use trajectories are not necessarily useful supervision. Even successful interactions may underrepresent multi-step dependencies or contain actions that are unjustified by the information visible to the agent at decision time. We identify two coupled requirements for effective trajectory supervision: compositional coverage, so that important observation-action dependencies are represented, and decision grounding, so that each supervised action is supported by the learner-visible interaction history. We introduce Trajectory2Gym, which constructs executable interactions around under-supported compositional structures and derives supervision only from grounded decisions in their realized executions. Structural-support selection allocates the resulting experience, while parent-normalized supervised fine-tuning preserves the semantic interaction as the unit of training weight. This reframes trajectory generation as the deliberate construction of learnable interaction experience, rather than the collection of successful trajectories. Across five Qwen3 scales, Trajectory2Gym improves Tau1, Tau2, and BFCL at every tested scale, increasing the equal-weight three-benchmark mean by 16.9-29.8 percentage points. The gains are strongest in stateful and multi-turn interaction, where later decisions depend on earlier observations and feedback. Our results suggest that scaling tool-use agents depends not only on collecting more successful trajectories, but on constructing interactions whose dependencies are represented and whose decisions are learnable from the agent's visible history.", "upvotes": 51, "discussionId": "6ae00a70a5efa215400a790b", "projectPage": "https://yihaohu0118.github.io/Trajectory2Gym-page/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/yihaohu0118/Trajectory2Gym", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "ai_summary": "Trajectory2Gym improves tool-use training by constructing interactions with compositional coverage and decision grounding rather than collecting successful trajectories.", "ai_keywords": ["compositional coverage", "decision grounding", "Trajectory2Gym", "structural-support selection", "parent-normalized supervised fine-tuning", "multi-turn interaction", "tool-use agents"], "ai_summary_model": "thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small", "githubStars": 2, "organization": {"_id": "68d4ae9941614425f8aa7490", "name": "ant-intl", "fullname": "ant-international", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68e9c86d2551cb38000fa77c/qG2KcieHqYFuWXi2-weuP.jpeg"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u6210\u529f\u7684\u5de5\u5177\u4f7f\u7528\u8f68\u8ff9\u5e76\u4e0d\u603b\u662f\u80fd\u63d0\u4f9b\u6709\u6548\u7684\u76d1\u7763\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6709\u6548\u7684\u8f68\u8ff9\u76d1\u7763\u9700\u8981\u6ee1\u8db3\u4e24\u4e2a\u8981\u6c42\uff1a\u89c2\u5bdf-\u52a8\u4f5c\u4f9d\u8d56\u7684\u7ec4\u5408\u8986\u76d6\u548c\u51b3\u7b56\u7684\u57fa\u7840\u652f\u6301\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86Trajectory2Gym\uff0c\u6784\u5efa\u53ef\u6267\u884c\u7684\u4ea4\u4e92\u5e76\u4ec5\u57fa\u4e8e\u53ef\u89c1\u7684\u51b3\u7b56\u5386\u53f2\u63d0\u4f9b\u76d1\u7763\u3002</li>\n    <li>Trajectory2Gym\u5728\u4e94\u4e2aQwen3\u89c4\u6a21\u4e0a\u63d0\u9ad8\u4e86Tau1\u3001Tau2\u548cBFCL\u7684\u8868\u73b0\uff0c\u589e\u5e45\u4e3a16.9-29.8\u4e2a\u767e\u5206\u70b9\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u6211\u4eec\u7684\u7814\u7a76\u8868\u660e\uff0c\u63d0\u5347\u5de5\u5177\u4f7f\u7528\u4ee3\u7406\u7684\u80fd\u529b\u4e0d\u4ec5\u9700\u8981\u6536\u96c6\u6210\u529f\u8f68\u8ff9\uff0c\u8fd8\u9700\u8981\u6784\u5efa\u5177\u6709\u53ef\u5b66\u4e60\u4f9d\u8d56\u5173\u7cfb\u7684\u4ea4\u4e92\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Successful tool use doesn't always provide good guidance for learning.</li>\n    <li>Effective supervision needs to cover important observation-action links and ensure that actions are based on visible information.</li>\n    <li>Trajectory2Gym helps create better learning experiences by focusing on interactions that highlight these important connections.</li>\n    <li>This method shows significant improvements in performance across different test scales, especially in situations where past actions influence future decisions.</li>\n    <li>The study suggests that improving tool-use agents requires building interactions that clearly show dependencies and are based on what the agent can see and learn from.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-23T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "Trajectory2Gym: Decision-Grounded Compositional Interaction Synthesis for Tool-Use Agents", "summary": "Successful tool-use trajectories are not necessarily useful supervision. Even successful interactions may underrepresent multi-step dependencies or contain actions that are unjustified by the information visible to the agent at decision time. We identify two coupled requirements for effective trajectory supervision: compositional coverage, so that important observation-action dependencies are represented, and decision grounding, so that each supervised action is supported by the learner-visible interaction history. We introduce Trajectory2Gym, which constructs executable interactions around under-supported compositional structures and derives supervision only from grounded decisions in their realized executions. Structural-support selection allocates the resulting experience, while parent-normalized supervised fine-tuning preserves the semantic interaction as the unit of training weight. This reframes trajectory generation as the deliberate construction of learnable interaction experience, rather than the collection of successful trajectories. Across five Qwen3 scales, Trajectory2Gym improves Tau1, Tau2, and BFCL at every tested scale, increasing the equal-weight three-benchmark mean by 16.9-29.8 percentage points. The gains are strongest in stateful and multi-turn interaction, where later decisions depend on earlier observations and feedback. Our results suggest that scaling tool-use agents depends not only on collecting more successful trajectories, but on constructing interactions whose dependencies are represented and whose decisions are learnable from the agent's visible history.", "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.33568.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "67e40cea6d0207b1fa43c826", "avatarUrl": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/no-auth/_OzAJCjFC49dEkHZm4AQb.png", "fullname": "Yihao Hu", "name": "HU22333", "type": "user", "isPro": false, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "68d4ae9941614425f8aa7490", "name": "ant-intl", "fullname": "ant-international", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/68e9c86d2551cb38000fa77c/qG2KcieHqYFuWXi2-weuP.jpeg"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}, {"paper": {"id": "2610.10524", "authors": [{"_id": "6ac6fef64eb8005165aed3e0", "name": "Jiyoung Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fef64eb8005165aed3e1", "name": "Paul Hyunbin Cho", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fef64eb8005165aed3e2", "name": "Jisu Nam", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fef64eb8005165aed3e3", "name": "Donghoon Lee", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fef64eb8005165aed3e4", "name": "Hyunsung Go", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fef64eb8005165aed3e5", "name": "Yeonkyeong Lee", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fef64eb8005165aed3e6", "name": "Hansaem Kim", "hidden": false}, {"_id": "6ac6fef64eb8005165aed3e7", "name": "Seungryong Kim", "hidden": false}], "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65cb4ce4d5822659ec122119/vuLISuzr1b3izauYyA274.mp4"], "publishedAt": "2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z", "submittedOnDailyAt": "2026-10-08T00:00:00.000Z", "title": "GRACE: Generation-aware latent compression for efficient video generation", "submittedOnDailyBy": {"_id": "65cb4ce4d5822659ec122119", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/b7e4b4c6914b32e097648dd5991dec26.svg", "isPro": true, "fullname": "jee0", "user": "chimaharicox", "type": "user", "name": "chimaharicox"}, "summary": "Highly compressed video autoencoders offer an effective way to accelerate video diffusion models, as the Diffusion Transformer (DiT) operates on far fewer tokens. However, such autoencoders are challenging to train, since a higher compression ratio degrades reconstruction quality and recovering it requires more channels, which is known to slow the convergence of the DiT. The compressed latent also differs from the one the DiT was trained on, so the pretrained DiT must be either retrained from scratch or adapted at considerable cost. Compressing the autoencoder the DiT was trained with appears to preserve compatibility, yet optimizing it for reconstruction alone still shifts the latent away from the distribution the DiT has learned. To address this, we propose Generation-Aware Latent Compression for Efficient Video Generation (GRACE), a two-stage framework that compresses a pretrained video autoencoder while keeping it compatible with the pretrained DiT. Specifically, we keep a frozen base latent from the pretrained encoder and learn a residual latent for the information lost under stronger compression, while aligning the compressed latent with the pretrained latent in the feature space of the frozen DiT so that the autoencoder is optimized for generation. We then adapt the DiT with lightweight fine-tuning and asymmetric denoising, where the base is denoised ahead of the residual. GRACE reduces the token count of Wan2.1-I2V-14B by 8x and its latency by 11.1x at 480x832x81, while matching the generation quality of the pretrained pipeline before compression on VBench.", "upvotes": 51, "discussionId": "6ac6fef64eb8005165aed3e8", "projectPage": "https://cvlab-kaist.github.io/GRACE/", "githubRepo": "https://github.com/cvlab-kaist/GRACE", "githubRepoAddedBy": "user", "githubStars": 12, "organization": {"_id": "6475760c33192631bad2bb38", "name": "kaist-ai", "fullname": "KAIST AI", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6469949654873f0043b09c22/aaZFiyXe1qR-Dmy_xq67m.png"}, "summary_zh": "<ul>\n    <li>\u9ad8\u5ea6\u538b\u7f29\u7684\u89c6\u9891\u81ea\u7f16\u7801\u5668\u53ef\u4ee5\u52a0\u901f\u89c6\u9891\u6269\u6563\u6a21\u578b\uff0c\u56e0\u4e3aDiffusion Transformer (DiT) \u5904\u7406\u7684token\u6570\u91cf\u5927\u5e45\u51cf\u5c11\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u8bad\u7ec3\u8fd9\u6837\u7684\u81ea\u7f16\u7801\u5668\u5f88\u56f0\u96be\uff0c\u9ad8\u538b\u7f29\u6bd4\u4f1a\u964d\u4f4e\u91cd\u5efa\u8d28\u91cf\uff0c\u5e76\u4e14\u9700\u8981\u66f4\u591a\u901a\u9053\uff0c\u8fd9\u6837\u4f1a\u51cf\u6162DiT\u7684\u6536\u655b\u901f\u5ea6\u3002</li>\n    <li>\u4e3a\u4e86\u4fdd\u6301\u517c\u5bb9\u6027\uff0c\u6211\u4eec\u63d0\u51fa\u4e86GRACE\uff0c\u4e00\u4e2a\u4e24\u9636\u6bb5\u6846\u67b6\uff0c\u538b\u7f29\u9884\u8bad\u7ec3\u7684\u89c6\u9891\u81ea\u7f16\u7801\u5668\uff0c\u5e76\u4e0e\u9884\u8bad\u7ec3\u7684DiT\u517c\u5bb9\u3002</li>\n    <li>GRACE\u901a\u8fc7\u4fdd\u6301\u51bb\u7ed3\u7684\u57fa\u7840\u6f5c\u5728\u5411\u91cf\u548c\u5b66\u4e60\u6b8b\u5dee\u6f5c\u5728\u5411\u91cf\u6765\u4f18\u5316\u751f\u6210\uff0c\u540c\u65f6\u5bf9\u9f50\u538b\u7f29\u540e\u7684\u6f5c\u5728\u5411\u91cf\u3002</li>\n    <li>GRACE\u5728480x832x81\u7684\u8bbe\u7f6e\u4e0b\uff0c\u5c06Wan2.1-I2V-14B\u7684token\u6570\u91cf\u51cf\u5c11\u4e868\u500d\uff0c\u5ef6\u8fdf\u51cf\u5c11\u4e8611.1\u500d\uff0c\u540c\u65f6\u5728VBench\u4e0a\u751f\u6210\u8d28\u91cf\u4e0e\u538b\u7f29\u524d\u7684\u9884\u8bad\u7ec3\u7ba1\u9053\u5339\u914d\u3002</li>\n</ul>", "summary_simple": "<ul>\n    <li>Video autoencoders help speed up video diffusion models by using fewer tokens.</li>\n    <li>Training highly compressed autoencoders is difficult because high compression can lower video quality.</li>\n    <li>The proposed method, GRACE, improves video generation by compressing a pretrained autoencoder while keeping it compatible with the trained model.</li>\n    <li>GRACE uses a two-step approach: it retains a base latent from the initial encoder and learns a new latent for lost information, ensuring better generation quality.</li>\n    <li>This method significantly reduces the number of tokens and processing time while maintaining video quality.</li>\n</ul>"}, "publishedAt": "2026-10-06T20:00:00.000Z", "title": "GRACE: Generation-aware latent compression for efficient video generation", "summary": "Highly compressed video autoencoders offer an effective way to accelerate video diffusion models, as the Diffusion Transformer (DiT) operates on far fewer tokens. However, such autoencoders are challenging to train, since a higher compression ratio degrades reconstruction quality and recovering it requires more channels, which is known to slow the convergence of the DiT. The compressed latent also differs from the one the DiT was trained on, so the pretrained DiT must be either retrained from scratch or adapted at considerable cost. Compressing the autoencoder the DiT was trained with appears to preserve compatibility, yet optimizing it for reconstruction alone still shifts the latent away from the distribution the DiT has learned. To address this, we propose Generation-Aware Latent Compression for Efficient Video Generation (GRACE), a two-stage framework that compresses a pretrained video autoencoder while keeping it compatible with the pretrained DiT. Specifically, we keep a frozen base latent from the pretrained encoder and learn a residual latent for the information lost under stronger compression, while aligning the compressed latent with the pretrained latent in the feature space of the frozen DiT so that the autoencoder is optimized for generation. We then adapt the DiT with lightweight fine-tuning and asymmetric denoising, where the base is denoised ahead of the residual. GRACE reduces the token count of Wan2.1-I2V-14B by 8x and its latency by 11.1x at 480x832x81, while matching the generation quality of the pretrained pipeline before compression on VBench.", "mediaUrls": ["https://cdn-uploads.huggingface.co/production/uploads/65cb4ce4d5822659ec122119/vuLISuzr1b3izauYyA274.mp4"], "thumbnail": "https://cdn-thumbnails.huggingface.co/social-thumbnails/papers/2610.10524.png", "numComments": 1, "submittedBy": {"_id": "65cb4ce4d5822659ec122119", "avatarUrl": "/avatars/b7e4b4c6914b32e097648dd5991dec26.svg", "fullname": "jee0", "name": "chimaharicox", "type": "user", "isPro": true, "isHf": false, "isHfAdmin": false, "isMod": false, "followerCount": 1, "isUserFollowing": false}, "organization": {"_id": "6475760c33192631bad2bb38", "name": "kaist-ai", "fullname": "KAIST AI", "avatar": "https://cdn-avatars.huggingface.co/v1/production/uploads/6469949654873f0043b09c22/aaZFiyXe1qR-Dmy_xq67m.png"}, "isAuthorParticipating": false}]
};
